INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the eight levels of faith', 'student:757e677f8fc56c66', 1, 'As for fulfilling the rights owed to those limits, that consists of u…', 'As for fulfilling the rights owed to those limits, that consists of upholding seven obligations of religion and twelve practices incumbent upon the God-fearing.', '<p>The doctrinal principle that bridges those hierarchical figures and those practical obligations is the Ismaili teaching of the divine degrees (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حدود</span>): every rank in the spiritual hierarchy has an active counterpart in the believer''s personal religion. The connection between the seven Imams and the <strong>seven obligations</strong>, or between the twelve proofs and the twelve practices, is structural, not incidental.</p><p>The seven Imams recur in the world like the days of the week because they represent the complete, unbroken presence of Allah''s guidance across every age and cycle. Each of the seven obligations corresponds to one dimension of that living presence, and they begin with <strong>guardianship</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولاية</span>) because it is the root from which every other obligation draws its meaning. Prayer, fasting, and pilgrimage are themselves forms of that love, made visible.</p><p>The twelve proofs (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجج</span>) are spread throughout the world as the Imam''s ambassadors, sustaining the community''s moral and social fabric wherever they reach. The twelve incumbent practices are exactly that fabric, as the believer lives it from within:</p><ul><li><p>Honoring parents and maintaining kinship ties</p></li><li><p>Protecting neighbors and enjoining what is good</p></li><li><p>Forbidding what is wrong, feeding others, visiting the sick</p></li></ul><p>These are not courtesies added on top of religion; they are the <strong>hierarchy enacted in daily life</strong>, rippling outward from the Imam''s light through the proofs and into every household.</p><p>The key that binds the two levels together is the Ismaili principle of the outward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظاهر</span>) and the inward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>). The seven Imams and twelve proofs are the inner, hierarchical reality; the seven obligations and twelve practices are their outer expression in time. This is why the text insists so firmly: without <strong>both knowledge and action</strong>, the name of faith cannot rightly apply.</p>', '["حدود (divine degrees): from the root ح-د-د, meaning to be sharp, to mark a precise boundary. A حدّ is the exact edge where one thing ends and another begins — like a blade''s cutting edge. The حدود are the sharply defined ranks of the spiritual hierarchy, each marking exactly where one degree of being ends and a higher one begins.", "ولاية (guardianship): from the root و-ل-ي, meaning to be near, to be adjacent, to take charge. A وليّ is one close enough to act on your behalf. The ولاية is nearness made covenant: the Imam is near to Allah, and the believer draws near to the Imam, forming a chain of proximity from the divine all the way down to us.", "حجة (proof): from the root ح-ج-ج — the same root as pilgrimage (حجّ) — meaning to go toward, to visit, to overcome in argument. The حجة is the one who overcomes doubt by his very presence — the living argument that Allah''s guidance has never been withdrawn from the world.", "ظاهر (outward): from the root ظ-ه-ر, meaning to appear, to rise above the surface — as the sun rises (ظَهَرَ). The ظاهر is whatever is visible and accessible, the outer face of religion. The same root gives ظَهْر, the back — the surface others see when facing you.", "باطن (inward): from the root ب-ط-ن, meaning to lie inside, to be hidden within — as the belly (بَطْن) is hidden inside the body. The باطن is the concealed meaning the outer form protects. The same root gives بطانة, the lining of a garment: invisible, but essential to its structure."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the eight levels of faith', 'student:5a4c4e7e67bda6ce', 2, 'These twelve are not obligatory upon women.', 'These twelve are not obligatory upon women.', '<p>The silence on justification is itself meaningful. In Ismaili jurisprudence, authoritative teaching (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تعليم</span>) — <strong>the Imam''s own knowledge transmitted through his chain of callers</strong> — is itself the proof; no Quranic verse or named hadith needs appending. The readers of this text were members of the call (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span>), accepting a ruling on the strength of who transmitted it, tracing back through the callers (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدعاة</span>) to the Imam himself.</p><p>The exemption of women becomes clearer when we see what the twelve practices correspond to. They map onto the twelve proofs (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحجج</span>) scattered across the world — the regional pillars of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span> hierarchy. In the Tayyibi <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span> of this era, that was a public, institutional role held by men; the twelve obligations belong to <strong>a specific institutional office</strong>, not to every believer equally.</p><p>Several items on the list presuppose a male, institutionally-positioned agent:</p><ul><li><p>&quot;Good conduct with one''s spouse&quot; addresses a husband, not a spouse in the abstract.</p></li><li><p>&quot;Keeping the bond among the brethren&quot; implies the fraternal network of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span>.</p></li><li><p>&quot;Enjoining what is good and forbidding what is wrong&quot; carries a public dimension tied to social authority and position.</p></li></ul><p>Women are not exempt from <strong>the spirit</strong> of these acts — they are exempt from the formal obligation attached to that institutional office.</p><p>A caller (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داعي</span>) transmits doctrine rather than constructing arguments from scratch; the chain (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سلسلة</span>) of Imams and callers <strong>is itself the only authority that matters</strong> — the book''s opening pages name that chain, and in naming it, they answer the question the reader is asking.</p>', '["تعليم (authoritative teaching): from the root ع-ل-م, meaning to know or to mark something with a sign. The verb علّم means to teach — to plant a mark of knowledge in another. In Ismaili doctrine, تعليم is not casual instruction but Imam-sourced transmission: the knowledge is valid only through its living source, so teacher and truth are inseparable.", "حجة (proof / regional representative): from the root ح-ج-ج, meaning to intend purposefully or to overcome in argument. The pilgrimage (الحج) shares this root — a directed journey toward a centre. A حجة is a proof that defeats doubt, and in the دعوة hierarchy it names the Imam''s regional representative: a person who embodies the living argument for the Imam''s authority.", "دعوة (the call): from the root د-ع-و, meaning to call out or to invite. To make a دعوة is to extend an invitation across a distance. In Ismaili tradition, the دعوة is the institution through which the Imam''s guidance reaches the world, each caller (داعي) extending that invitation to those with ears willing to hear.", "سلسلة (chain of transmission): from the root س-ل-س-ل, whose repeated syllables mimic the interlocking of links. A سلسلة is a chain in which each link depends on the one before it. In Ismaili doctrine, the chain''s authority rests entirely on the integrity of its first link — the Imam himself, from whom all knowledge originally flows."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'tooth-stick, dust, and the purity of food', 'student:8dd1c77d3b175060', 1, 'eating the cooking of a serving girl who is not careful about ritual…', 'eating the cooking of a serving girl who is not careful about ritual purity is permitted', '<p><em>Researched outside this library — the knowledge base carries nothing on this passage. Sources are listed at the end.</em></p><p>This ruling touches upon a beautiful and subtle distinction in the understanding of purity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طهارة</span>). The difference between the two cases lies not in the action, but in the <strong>fundamental state</strong> of the person preparing the food. It is a distinction between an inherent state of being and a temporary condition.</p><p>The concern with food prepared by an unbeliever, as mentioned in the text, stems from a legal concept where polytheism (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شرك</span>) is viewed as a state of profound spiritual impurity. This is not merely about physical cleanliness but about an essential spiritual state that, in this specific legal view, is considered transferable through moisture. Imagine a spring whose very source is clouded; any water drawn from it will also be clouded. In this analogy, the food becomes defiled because the impurity is seen as inherent to the person''s state of disbelief, and wetness acts as the medium for its transfer.</p><p>The case of the Muslim serving girl is entirely different. As a believer, her fundamental state is one of purity. If she is &quot;not careful about ritual purity,&quot; it means she may be in a temporary state of minor ritual impurity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حدث</span>), the kind that is resolved by performing ablution before prayer. This state affects her readiness for worship but does not alter her essential purity as a Muslim. Her condition is like a clean cup that has been used but can be readily rinsed for prayer. It is not inherently impure, and therefore, her touch does not defile the food she prepares. The food is lawful because it originates from a source that is, in its essence, pure.</p><p>***</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طهارة</span> (purity): From the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ط</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ر</span>, which means to be clean, pure, and free from filth. The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طهارة</span> signifies the act of purification, both in a physical sense, like washing with water, and in a spiritual sense, such as cleansing the soul from sin.</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نجاسة</span> (impurity): From the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ن</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ج</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">س</span>, meaning to be unclean, dirty, or defiled. The derived word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نجاسة</span> refers to something that is inherently impure, which can then contaminate other things it comes into contact with, especially through moisture.</p><p><strong>Sources consulted</strong></p><ul><li><p>islamqa.info</p></li><li><p>collectiveijtihad.org</p></li><li><p>almahdi.edu</p></li><li><p>deenin.com</p></li></ul>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'bodies, hair, and the reform of custom', 'student:3d1ea3464ac08c56', 1, 'the deliberate deferral of girls'' circumcision carries a meaning — on…', 'the deliberate deferral of girls'' circumcision carries a meaning — one among the meanings the Mighty Creator intends', '<p><em>Researched outside this library — the knowledge base carries nothing on this passage. Sources are listed at the end.</em></p><p>In Ismaili thought, every outward, physical act of religious life is understood to be a symbol for a deeper, spiritual reality. The relationship between the two is like that of a body to a soul; one is the sensible, outward form (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الظاهر</span>), while the other is the intelligible, inward meaning (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الباطن</span>). The text points to this very principle when it states, &quot;Every sensible matter has an intelligible counterpart set opposite it, and every outward likeness holds within it that which is likened.&quot;</p><p>The specific meaning of deferring a girl''s circumcision until the age of seven, therefore, is not found in the physical act itself but in the spiritual reality it represents. The outward rules of the religion (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشريعة</span>) are a guide, a physical path that prepares the soul to understand its inner purpose. To contravene the established practice is to damage the path toward its inner meaning, which is the ultimate goal. The timing is not arbitrary; it is a crucial part of the symbol.</p><p>The number seven, in particular, holds a special significance in Ismaili thought and throughout Islamic tradition. It often symbolizes a stage of completion, perfection, or the culmination of a cycle, like the seven days of creation or the seven heavens. By prescribing the age of seven, the law suggests a connection between the physical act and a specific stage of the soul''s development and readiness. The intended meaning is tied to this spiritual maturation.</p><p>Therefore, when the text speaks of a &quot;meaning the Mighty Creator intends,&quot; it refers to this esoteric counterpart. The deliberate deferral of the physical act until a symbolically significant age is meant to align the believer with a corresponding process occurring in the spiritual realm (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الباطن</span>). To ignore the outward timing is to misunderstand its purpose and, in doing so, to disrupt the soul''s journey toward grasping the deeper truth that the physical ritual is meant to unveil.</p><p>***</p><p><strong>Etymology</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span> (inward):</strong> From the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ب</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ط</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ن</span>, which means to be inside, concealed, or in the belly (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بطن</span>). The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بطن</span> refers to the abdomen, the ultimate interior of the body. Thus, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span> is that which is hidden deep inside, the secret or esoteric meaning concealed within an outer form.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Sources consulted</strong></p><ul><li><p>wikipedia.org</p></li><li><p>simerg.com</p></li><li><p>ismailimail.blog</p></li><li><p>reddit.com</p></li><li><p>youtube.com</p></li><li><p>islamqa.info</p></li></ul>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'bodies, hair, and the reform of custom', 'student:3af2407989c5e258', 2, 'the one whom they claim as their master and the owner of their affair…', 'the one whom they claim as their master and the owner of their affair had already gone before them in that practice', '<p>{ &quot;body&quot;: &quot;The passage leaves this person deliberately unnamed, and that silence is itself the point. Mawlana Jafar ibn Sulayman, writing in his letter <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّيْفُ الْأَقْطَعُ فِي إِبْطَالِ الْبِدَعِ</span> to the Ismaili community in Yemen, uses the phrase &quot;the one whom they claim as their master and the owner of their affair&quot; as a sharp rhetorical jab — one that lands hardest when the audience already knows who is meant.\n\nIn the polemical language of the Fatimid <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span>, the phrase <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صاحب أمرهم</span> — the master and owner of their affair — typically points to whoever a rival faction recognizes as its <strong>legitimate authority</strong>, most often the Abbasid caliph, the symbolic head of Sunni Islam during the Fatimid period. The communities Mawlana Jafar addresses in Yemen looked to that rival seat rather than to the Fatimid Imam.\n\nThe specific instance of henna dyeing he alludes to is not spelled out. The author trusts his readers to recall a known episode — circulating in the community''s oral memory or sectarian literature — in which that unnamed figure had himself dyed his hands with henna in the manner of women. By invoking it without naming it, Mawlana Jafar shames the opponents by linking their practice to their own authority while avoiding giving that figure the weight of a direct mention.\n\nThe passage does not supply the name, does not cite a date or occasion, and was never meant to. The accusation stands as an open wound in the argument — felt most keenly by those inside the community who already knew what it referred to, and deliberately opaque to outsiders. Trying to pin a definitive name or incident to it, beyond what the text itself gives us, would be adding to the passage rather than reading it.&quot;, &quot;etymology&quot;: [ &quot;<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّيْفُ الْأَقْطَعُ</span> (the severing sword): from the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ق</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ط</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>, meaning to cut, sever or interrupt completely. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأقطع</span> is the intensive form — not merely a cutting sword but one that severs without remainder. The title frames the letter as a decisive blade against <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بدعة</span>, innovation.&quot; ] }</p>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'skins, blood, and waiting periods', 'student:ec273b1ab5d345d4', 1, 'prayer is not permitted in it until it has been washed, yet the mater…', 'prayer is not permitted in it until it has been washed, yet the material itself is not impure', '<p>The answer''s two halves seem to contradict each other, but they address two entirely different legal questions. <strong>The first asks whether the material is ritually defiling; the second asks whether it meets the ritual condition required for prayer.</strong> The ruling is only a paradox if we collapse them into one.</p><p>A person waking from sleep needs ablution (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وضوء</span>) before prayer, even though nothing defiling has touched them overnight. That ablution is not washing away a stain — it is conferring a <strong>positive status of ritual purity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طهارة</span>)</strong>. The same logic applies to shorn wool.</p><p>The wool or hair taken from a living lawful animal carries no active defilement. But it has left the animal''s body without the legal event that Ismaili law sets as the threshold for animal materials to qualify for worship: proper slaughter (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذبح</span>). That act is not merely about ending a life — it is the <strong>sanctifying threshold</strong> that moves animal matter into a fully qualified ritual status. Without it, the material occupies a middle ground: not impure (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نجس</span>), but not yet in the positive state of ritual purity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طهارة</span>) that prayer demands.</p><p>The washing closes that gap, not by scrubbing away something bad, but by <strong>completing the material''s transition into a state fit for the sacred act of prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلاة</span>)</strong>.</p>', '["طهارة (ritual purity): from root ط-ه-ر, meaning to be clean and unblemished. The root conveys brightness and freedom from anything that dims. The derived noun طهارة names not merely an absence of dirt but a positively conferred state — to stand in طهارة is to have actively attained a condition, not simply inherited it by default.", "نجاسة (ritual impurity): from root ن-ج-س, meaning to be unclean or defiled. The root نجس carries the sense of something actively contaminating — a quality that reaches outward and affects whatever it contacts. نجاسة is therefore not just an absence of purity but a positive, transmissible force of defilement.", "ذبح (proper slaughter): from root ذ-ب-ح, meaning to cut at the throat with precision. The root captures a deliberate, decisive act of severance. In ritual law, ذبح is more than the ending of life — it is the legal threshold that transforms the status of animal matter from ambiguous to ritually qualified."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'skins, blood, and waiting periods', 'student:034b9144dd2fbfce', 2, 'whoever misses the prayer of the Feast of Ghadir and its fast makes t…', 'whoever misses the prayer of the Feast of Ghadir and its fast makes them up after that day, on whatever day it may be', '<p>The grammatically general Arabic construction ''whoever misses'' covers any believer, for any reason — but its placement inside a dense discussion of menstruation is <strong>anything but accidental</strong>. A woman who menstruates during the days surrounding the Feast of Ghadir (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عيد الغدير</span>) cannot pray or fast, and one of the most sacred days of the year has just slipped past her through no fault of her own. The compiler, working through a chain of practical ritual obligations, placed this ruling exactly where the need for it would be felt most.</p><p>The phrase ''Another matter'' signals a digression that grows directly from the case preceding it: what does a woman who has lost the feast to menstruation owe for it? The ruling answers that question, then deliberately opens its scope wider, using ''whoever'' to cover illness, travel, ritual impurity, or any other obstacle — a specific case prompting a general principle.</p><p>The mechanism prescribed is make-up (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قضاء</span>) — the settling of a missed obligation — performed at the time of the sun''s decline, which mirrors the original hour of the feast prayer. That insistence on the precise time is the ruling''s real weight: it demands <strong>recovery in the feast''s own form</strong>, not a vague gesture toward what was missed. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عيد الغدير</span> is the day Maulana Ali was designated by the Prophet as the culmination of his mission, and no circumstance — not even menstruation — is permitted to simply erase it.</p>', '["عيد الغدير: عيد is from ع-و-د, to return — a feast is a recurring occasion that ''comes back.'' الغدير is from غ-د-ر, to leave water standing; a غدير is a pool left by receding floodwater. The Prophet''s declaration at a pool named Khumm gave this feast its name. The phrase names the feast that keeps returning to that momentous day.", "قضاء (make-up): from the root ق-ض-ي, meaning to decide, pronounce, or settle finally. A قاضي (judge) settles disputes; قضاء of a missed prayer or fast is the settling of a spiritual debt owed to Allah — the believer ''pronounces'' the obligation fulfilled, however late the fulfillment comes."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'skins, blood, and waiting periods', 'student:7f6722eb38740dd0', 3, 'what is meant is only that the fornicator has no right in the lineage…', 'what is meant is only that the fornicator has no right in the lineage of the child', '<p>The hadith at the center of this discussion reads:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَلَدُ لِلْفِرَاشِ، وَلِلزَّانِي الْحَجَرُ</span> The child belongs to the lawful bed; for the fornicator, the stone.</p><p>The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحجر</span> is where the disagreement lives. In the dominant classical reading, it points to stoning — the prescribed corporal punishment for fornication. The Ismaili text from <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِتَابُ الزِّينَةِ</span> says something different: what is meant is only that the fornicator has <strong>no right in the child''s lineage</strong>. The stone, in this reading, is not thrown at him; it is placed before the door of filiation.</p><p>The context the passage builds around the hadith supports this. The text is not treating criminal sanctions; it is navigating civil questions — inheritance, lineage, and legal filiation. The case placed immediately after confirms it: a man who fornicates with a slave girl and then purchases her cannot claim the child of that union, even as its biological father, because the lawful bed (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الفراش</span>) holds the prior legal claim. The hadith functions here as <strong>a rule of filiation</strong>, not a penalty clause.</p><p>The root of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحجر</span> carries both possibilities, and the text exploits this deliberately. One branch yields <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحَجَر</span> — the stone as physical object. The other yields <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحَجر</span> — legal interdiction, the <strong>blocking of legal capacity</strong>: the term <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محجور عليه</span> means a person''s right to act is sealed off entirely. The Ismaili reading draws on this second branch: the fornicator (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الزاني</span>) is not stoned but interdicted, his path to the child''s lineage permanently foreclosed.</p><p>This reading belongs to the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span> tradition, which holds that the outward letter always carries an inner legal and spiritual meaning. The outer text says <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحجر</span>; the inner reality it conveys is exclusion from filiation — <strong>the most irreversible legal barrier</strong> a father can face.</p>', '["الحجر (interdiction): from the root ح-ج-ر, meaning to prevent, confine, or block. Two distinct branches emerge: الحَجَر (a stone, a physical object) and الحَجر (legal interdiction — the blocking of someone''s legal capacity). The Ismaili reading draws on the second branch: the fornicator is not stoned but legally barred from the child''s lineage.", "الفراش (the lawful bed): from the root ف-ر-ش, meaning to spread or lay flat. الفراش is literally a sleeping mat or bed. As a legal term in this hadith, it becomes a metonym for the marriage bond itself — whatever falls within the husband''s lawful claim, the child born there belongs to him.", "الزاني (the fornicator): from the root ز-ن-ي, meaning to transgress a boundary unlawfully. الزاني is the active participle — the one committing fornication. The root''s sense of crossing a forbidden line maps directly onto both the moral violation and the legal exclusion the word carries in this ruling."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the pillar of religion and its appointed hours', 'student:e63672ae32b2b43a', 1, 'the bedouins are harder in disbelief and hypocrisy, and it is not per…', 'the bedouins are harder in disbelief and hypocrisy, and it is not permissible to pray behind them', '<p>The ruling that prayer behind a bedouin (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعْرَابِيّ</span>) imam is void rests on a legal principle: <strong>the imam''s religious fitness is a precondition for the congregation''s prayer to be valid</strong>. When we pray behind an imam, our prayer attaches to his — spiritually and legally. If his standing before Allah is compromised, that bond breaks.</p><p>The Quranic characterization is the legal evidence. Allah says:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَعْرَابُ أَشَدُّ كُفْرًا وَنِفَاقًا وَأَجْدَرُ أَلَّا يَعْلَمُوا حُدُودَ مَا أَنزَلَ اللَّهُ عَلَىٰ رَسُولِهِ</span> The bedouins are harder in disbelief and hypocrisy and more likely to be unaware of the limits of what Allah has revealed to His Messenger. At-Tawbah 9:97</p><p>Notice that final phrase: <em>unaware of the limits of what Allah revealed</em>. An imam <strong>must know those limits to lead others across them correctly</strong>; when Allah Himself declares a group likely to lack that knowledge — and deeper in disbelief (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كفر</span>) and hypocrisy (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفاق</span>) than most — the law treats that declaration as disqualifying them from leading prayer.</p><p>The man in the passage had also emigrated, crossing into a higher religious covenant. <strong>Praying behind someone the Quran places at the outermost edge of the believing community</strong> directly contradicts the act of migration he had undertaken — which is why his emigrant status intensifies rather than excuses the ruling.</p><p>A surgeon''s assistant cannot hand the scalpel to someone unlicensed to hold it, even if the operation itself proceeds correctly. <strong>The content of the man''s prayer was sound; what failed was the authority through which it was communally offered.</strong></p>', '["نفاق (hypocrisy): from the root ن-ف-ق, whose core noun is نَفَق — a tunnel or burrow with two openings, one visible entry and one hidden exit. The hypocrite enters from one side while planning escape from the other. The image captures the double life the word names: a person whose outward faith conceals an entirely different interior.", "كفر (disbelief): from the root ك-ف-ر, meaning to cover or conceal. Pre-Islamic Arabic used كافر for a farmer who buries seeds beneath the soil, covering them over. Applied to faith, كفر is the act of burying — beneath habit, pride, or indifference — a truth that has already been placed clearly before you.", "أَعْرَابِيّ (bedouin): from the root ع-ر-ب, whose verbal form عَرَّبَ means to make something clear or articulate. Yet أَعْرَابِيّ names one defined by distance from settled communal life — the very life in which religious clarity is forged. The root illuminates the irony: those who carry the language in its purest desert form stand furthest from its spiritual cultivation."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the pillar of religion and its appointed hours', 'student:76a0969191c14a62', 2, 'then the shaf and the witr, then the sunan', 'then the shaf and the witr, then the sunan', '<p>Making up missed prayers follows a strict ranked sequence:</p><ul><li><p>the obligatory prayers (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرائض</span>) first,</p></li><li><p>then the even prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شفع</span>) and the odd prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وتر</span>),</p></li><li><p>then the recommended prayers (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنن</span>).</p></li></ul><p>Two rakahs performed as a matched pair, the even prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شفع</span>) is <strong>the night prayer''s paired prelude</strong> — it comes immediately before the odd prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وتر</span>) at the close of the night prayer sequence, like two hands cupped together before the final release. Together they form the concluding portion of the night prayer that Maulana Ali described as a benefit to the body, an opening to mercy, and a holding fast to the character of the prophets.</p><p>The odd prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وتر</span>) earns its middle rank because its very name carries the fragrance of divine oneness — a single concluding act that <strong>seals night worship with an echo of Allah''s singularity</strong>. In the tradition described here, it consists of three units with the qunut supplication. Because it points so directly toward divine uniqueness, several jurists consider it close to binding, which is why it outranks ordinary recommended prayers.</p><p>The recommended prayers (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنن</span>) are the prayers that frame each obligatory prayer — rakahs before and after the prescribed times, following the Prophet''s habitual practice. They enrich worship and deepen its quality, but they carry <strong>no urgency of obligation</strong>; they are the gentle, well-worn groove of a prophetic life.</p><p>This hierarchy carries real practical weight: when a person dies and leaves a bequeathed sum for making up their missed prayers, the sequence is applied in order:</p><ol><li><p>Obligatory prayers (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرائض</span>) first.</p></li><li><p>Then the even prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شفع</span>) and the odd prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وتر</span>).</p></li><li><p>Then the recommended prayers (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنن</span>), to whatever the remaining wage allows.</p></li></ol>', '["شفع (the even prayer): from the root ش-ف-ع, meaning to be even-numbered, to pair, or to come in twos. The derived word names the two rakahs that stand together before the witr. Beautifully, the same root gives شفاعة (intercession) — where one soul pairs itself with another to plead before Allah, two voices becoming one appeal.", "وتر (the odd prayer): from the root و-ت-ر, meaning to be singular, solitary, or odd-numbered. The name mirrors the prayer''s purpose: a single act standing alone at the close of night worship. The root also evokes a taut bowstring — stretched, solitary, releasing with finality — which captures exactly what the witr does to the night prayer.", "سنة (recommended practice): from the root س-ن-ن, meaning to establish a smooth, well-worn path or to set a flowing precedent. The سنة is the Prophet''s habitual groove — a path so often traveled it becomes natural. The root suggests something fluid and precedent-setting, not sharp or commanded, which is precisely why سنن prayers rank lowest in the hierarchy.", "فريضة (obligatory prayer): from the root ف-ر-ض, meaning to cut, to notch, or to prescribe with precision. What is فرض has been notched into the law — fixed and non-negotiable, like a groove carved into wood. The same root yields فرضة, a fixed landing-place on a riverbank: something precisely located and impossible to move."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the pillar of religion and its appointed hours', 'student:64d02bb72cacf4c5', 3, 'her release and her discharge, which are known in the books of the fr…', 'her release and her discharge, which are known in the books of the friends of God', '<p>Specific procedures are left unnamed here, with a deliberate pointer toward ''the books of the friends of God'' — the Imams and the senior figures of the Ismaili Da''wa — as the proper source of instruction. This is characteristic of how Ismaili religious writing handles sensitive detail: it lives in authorized transmission through the Da''wa hierarchy, not in texts laid open to every reader.</p><p>The word ''release'' (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إطلاق</span>) carries the image of untying a knot; in religious usage, it means freeing a soul from the binding weight of unmet obligations and accumulated sins. The release involves <strong>specific supplications and intercessions</strong> made on her behalf by the living, asking Allah to untie what had bound her.</p><p>''Discharge'' (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إبراء</span>) is a legal term meaning to formally clear someone of an outstanding claim. Spiritually, this means making good the religious debts she left behind:</p><ul><li><p>making up her missed prayers (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قضاء الصلوات</span>) by proxy</p></li><li><p>completing her missed fasts, or paying their ransom (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فدية</span>)</p></li><li><p>settling any expiations (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كفارات</span>) still owed</p></li></ul><p>Among all the deeds one can send on her behalf, <strong>the pilgrimage</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحج</span>) stands above everything else — Imam Jafar al-Sadiq''s reply to his companion makes this unmistakably clear: even giving a mountain''s weight in charity would not reach the reward of a single Hajj. If her faith and prayer were uncertain, making up her prayers becomes the most urgent task, because prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصلاة</span>) is, as Maulana Ali taught, the pillar of the religion and the first thing Allah examines of a person''s deeds. Ongoing charity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صدقة جارية</span>) flowing to the believers is also among the doors of goodness one can open on her behalf.</p>', '["إطلاق (release): from the root ط-ل-ق, carrying the sense of flowing free, being unbound. The verb طَلَّقَ means to release or divorce — to sever a binding tie. إطلاق is the noun of that act: the untying itself. When applied to a soul weighed down by unmet debts, it names the moment the knot is cut and the burden falls away.", "إبراء (discharge): from the root ب-ر-أ, meaning to be clear, innocent, free of defect or claim. The root gives بَرِيء (innocent) and بُرْء (recovery from illness — being cleared of it). إبراء is the act of formally clearing someone of an obligation, as a creditor releases a debtor. Spiritually, it is acquittal turned into action.", "قضاء (making up): from the root ق-ض-ي, meaning to decide, complete, settle finally. The root gives قَاضٍ (judge) and قَضِيَّة (case, affair). قضاء in prayer law means performing what was owed after its time passed — a verdict of completion, carrying the finality of a judge''s ruling.", "كفارة (expiation): from the root ك-ف-ر, meaning to cover or conceal. The same root gives كَافِر — one who covers over truth. كفارة is a compensating deed placed over a sin in the scales, covering it. It does not erase; it buries."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the pillar of religion and its appointed hours', 'student:5c1b8221a1742693', 4, 'if it belonged to the night prayer, the fasting person would be permi…', 'if it belonged to the night prayer, the fasting person would be permitted to eat at its time', '<p>The principle al-Muizz draws on is elegant: in Islamic legal reasoning, <strong>a prayer''s classification as a night prayer and the fasting person''s right to eat share the same boundary line.</strong> The fast (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصوم</span>) of Ramadan is governed by one rule — night is the period when eating is permitted, and day is when it is forbidden. If a prayer belongs to &quot;the night,&quot; its time is when the faster may eat.</p><p>That logic is a precise test:</p><ol><li><p>If Fajr were a night prayer, its time would fall within the night.</p></li><li><p>Night time is, legally, the period when eating is permitted for the fasting person.</p></li><li><p>But at Fajr, eating stops — the fast begins.</p></li><li><p>Therefore Fajr cannot belong to the night.</p></li></ol><p>Night and day here are not just astronomical facts; they are <strong>legal territories</strong> with distinct rulings that switch over at precise thresholds.</p><p>This is exactly why al-Muizz places Fajr in a class of its own — &quot;one set midway between all four, belonging to neither the night''s division nor the day''s division.&quot; It is the prayer of the threshold. The Prophet''s own words confirm its singular standing:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةُ الْغَدَاةِ يَحْضُرُهَا مَلَائِكَةُ اللَّيْلِ وَمَلَائِكَةُ النَّهَارِ</span> &quot;The morning prayer is attended by the angels of the night and the angels of the day.&quot;</p><p>No other prayer shares that witness. The passage also describes Fajr as a <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعْوَة</span> (a call or summons) that has no call after it — the final summons before the full weight of the day descends.</p>', '["دَعْوَة: from root د-ع-و, meaning to call out or summon. Related words: دَعَا (he called), دُعَاء (supplication — the act of calling out to Allah), دَاعِي (the caller). A دَعْوَة is a single act of summoning. Here each prayer is a divine call; Fajr is the last — a call after a preceding call, yet with no call following it.", "بَعْدَ: from root ب-ع-د, whose core meaning is distance or remoteness. Related words: بَعِيد (far away), بُعْد (distance itself). When Arabic says بَعْدَ (after), it casts time as space — what comes ''after'' lies at a remove from what came before. In the passage, Fajr is a call بَعْدَ a prior call, yet with nothing بَعْدَهَا — after it."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the disputed words of the call', 'student:eef78328e45db082', 1, 'if it is administered according to the judgment of one appointed by t…', 'if it is administered according to the judgment of one appointed by the friends of God', '<p>The text''s answer to whether a salary from the public treasury (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَيْتُ الْمَالِ</span>) is lawful rests on <strong>a single, decisive condition</strong>: who administers it. If the person distributing the funds was appointed by the friends of Allah (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أولياء الله</span>), the income is lawful; if not, it is not.</p><p>In Ismaili understanding, &quot;the friends of Allah&quot; (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أولياء الله</span>) are not a loose description for any righteous individual. They refer <strong>specifically to the Imams of the Ahl al-Bayt</strong> — the living, hereditary successors of the Prophet, each bearing divine authority in their own time, not by popular approval or conquest.</p><p>That authority was announced openly by the Prophet on the Day of Ghadir, when he said:</p><blockquote><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ كُنْتُ مَوْلَاهُ فَعَلِيٌّ مَوْلَاهُ</span> Whoever''s master I am, Ali is his master.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Maulana Ali became the first</strong> guardian (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span>) after the Prophet — not simply a beloved figure, but one whose appointment came directly from Allah — and every Imam descended from him carried that same authority forward.</p><p>Think of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَيْتُ الْمَالِ</span> as a community trust fund: the wealth belongs to everyone, but only the <strong>legally named trustee</strong> can distribute it lawfully. When the Imam, or someone the Imam explicitly delegates, manages that distribution, any salary drawn from it is clean and permissible.</p><p>A tyrant who seizes the treasury distributes wealth without a divine mandate. <strong>The legitimacy of the hand</strong> that controls it is what matters — and that hand belongs to the Imams alone, and to those they formally authorize.</p>', '["وَلِيّ (guardian): from root و-ل-ي, meaning to be close or to follow immediately after. The nearest one — standing right beside you — is the وَلِيّ. Proximity becomes guardianship: the one who comes closest holds the right of care and command. Derived forms include مَوْلَى (master, patron) and وِلَايَة (guardianship, authority).", "أولياء (friends of Allah): plural of وَلِيّ, from root و-ل-ي. The plural does not simply multiply individuals — it names a succession, a chain of those standing nearest to Allah across time, each the closest guardian in his own age.", "بَيْتُ الْمَالِ (public treasury): compound of بَيْت, from root ب-ي-ت (to lodge overnight; hence a house or dwelling), and مَال, from root م-ي-ل (to incline, to lean). Wealth is literally what one inclines toward; the بَيْتُ الْمَالِ is the dwelling where that communal inclination — the community''s resources — is gathered and kept.", "مَوْلَى (master, patron): from the same root و-ل-ي as وَلِيّ, but carrying heavier relational weight — one who stands as master and protector. The Prophet''s deliberate choice of this precise word at Ghadir made the point unmistakable: Ali stands nearest in authority and in love."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'eight proofs for the best of human beings', 'student:7566e54ef1daadbe', 1, 'Likeness to the Prophet is therefore established for him. He is the b…', 'Likeness to the Prophet is therefore established for him. He is the best of human beings alongside him', '<p>The question is sharp and honest: if the Prophet''s rank as &quot;best of human beings&quot; includes prophecy — an office Maulana Ali explicitly does not hold — how does shared purity alone carry him to that same rank? <strong>Purity is the opening proof, not the complete argument.</strong></p><p>The Purification Verse records something far deeper than a moral quality:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّمَا يُرِيدُ اللهُ لِيُذْهِبَ عَنكُمُ الرِّجْسَ أَهْلَ الْبَيْتِ وَيُطَهِّرَكُمْ تَطْهِيرًا</span> Allah only wishes to remove all impurity from you, people of the household, and to purify you completely. Al-Ahzab 33:33</p><p>This is <strong>Allah''s own undeflectable will</strong> — not a virtue bestowed but a divine election. The purification willed here is the same in kind for the Prophet and the household; the difference lies in the <em>role</em> each carries, not in the quality of their nearness to Allah.</p><p>The hadith of position is then the crucial hinge:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِيٌّ مِنِّي بِمَنْزِلَةِ هَارُونَ مِنْ مُوسَى إِلَّا أَنَّهُ لَا نَبِيَّ بَعْدِي</span> Ali stands to me in the station of Aaron to Moses, except that there is no prophet after me.</p><p>Notice what the exception removes: the <em>office</em> of prophecy — not the quality of excellence. The rest of the analogy stands whole:</p><ul><li><p>Shared task</p></li><li><p>Shared authority</p></li><li><p>Shared guardianship over the community</p></li></ul><p>Aaron was himself a prophet, yet he remained the secondary figure while Moses was primary.</p><p>The call to prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أذان</span>) therefore makes a precise, not an extravagant, claim. It says Muhammad and Maulana Ali are jointly the best of human beings — not that they are identical in every attribute. Think of two master builders: one who conceives the design and one who executes it with complete faithfulness. Both are rightly called the finest in their craft. Excellence (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فضل</span>) in the Quran flows from <strong>closeness to Allah, righteous action, and divine appointment</strong> — and all three are unambiguously present in Maulana Ali, as the guardianship verse (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آية الولاية</span>) and the declaration at Ghadir Khumm (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غدير خم</span>) both confirm.</p>', '["طهارة (purification): from the root ط-ه-ر, meaning to be clean and free of defilement. The derived noun طهارة names this state. Notably, the verb يُطَهِّرُ in the Purification Verse is causative — purification is not self-achieved but conferred from above. The one called مُطَهَّر has been made pure by a higher agency, not merely observed to be so.", "فضل (excellence): from the root ف-ض-ل, meaning to exceed, to overflow, to remain as surplus after a need is fully met. فَضَلَ means to be left over — to be more than enough. فضل is therefore not ordinary goodness but overflowing grace. When Allah bestows His فضل, He gives well beyond all measure.", "منزلة (station): from the root ن-ز-ل, meaning to descend and settle in a place. نَزَلَ is to alight and take up dwelling. منزلة is where one has settled — a fixed, established rank. The Prophet''s hadith assigns Maulana Ali a منزلة: a rank that has come down upon him and become his permanent, immovable standing.", "ولاية (guardianship): from the root و-ل-ي, meaning nearness and immediate contiguity. وَلِيَ means to stand adjacent, to come directly after. The ولي is the one who stands closest — guardian, protector, and successor in one. ولاية is the authority that flows from that proximity: to stand beside, to govern, and to guide.", "خير (the best): from the root خ-ي-ر, meaning to be inherently preferable and to excel. خَيَّرَ means to present a choice assuming one option holds genuine superiority. خير is not merely relative better in a mundane sense but intrinsic excellence — the quality of being, by nature and by divine recognition, the finest.", "أذان (call to prayer): from the root أ-ذ-ن, connected to the ear (أُذُن). أَذَّنَ means to cause something to be heard, to announce widely. The أذان is the act of making a truth audible — not simply marking a prayer time but proclaiming a reality that listening hearts are invited to receive."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'eight proofs for the best of human beings', 'student:40df604f210396cf', 2, 'the family of Yasin — upon whom God, glorified is He, has sent peace…', 'the family of Yasin — upon whom God, glorified is He, has sent peace — are the family of the Messenger of God', '<p>The verse at the heart of this second proof is:</p><blockquote><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ عَلَى آلِ يَاسِينَ إِنَّا كَذَلِكَ نَجْزِي الْمُحْسِنِينَ</span> Peace be upon the family of Yasin. Thus do We reward those who do good.</p></blockquote><p>Al-Saffat 37:130</p><p>The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَاسِين</span> is not a standard Arabic noun built on a three-letter root. It is the name-form of the two Quranic letters <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يس</span> — the same letters that open Surah Ya-Sin (Chapter 36). In that chapter, Allah addresses a messenger directly and with unmistakable intimacy, confirming that he is indeed among the messengers. Every major current of Islamic commentary understands that address as being to the Prophet Muhammad. Because <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يس</span> opens a chapter in which Allah speaks to him personally, those two letters became <strong>one of his living titles</strong>, and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَاسِين</span> is their full, vocalized rendering as a name.</p><p>When <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَاسِين</span> appears in Al-Saffat 37:130, the Ismaili and broader Shia exegetical tradition reads &quot;family (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آل</span>) of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَاسِين</span>&quot; as the family of the Prophet Muhammad. This reading emerges from the Quran''s own self-consistency rather than being imposed from outside it. The interpretive authority traces to the Imams of the household (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَهْلِ الْبَيْت</span>); the identification was made <strong>explicitly by Imam Jafar al-Sadiq</strong>, who named <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آلِ يَاسِينَ</span> as the Prophet''s own family.</p><p>Some classical commentators outside this tradition connected <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَاسِين</span> to the Prophet Ilyas (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلْيَاس</span>), reading it as an alternate rendering of his name. The author sets that reading aside through a simple appeal to the Quran''s own logic: since <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يس</span> is already a dedicated address to the Prophet Muhammad in Surah Ya-Sin, the same figure is honored when <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَاسِين</span> appears in Al-Saffat. He then invokes the Quran''s own declaration of its inviolability — <strong>falsehood cannot approach it from any direction</strong> — making this identification not a school''s preference but a truth the Book itself bears witness to.</p>', '["يَاسِين (Yasin): Unlike ordinary Arabic words, يَاسِين has no standard trilateral root. It is the fully vocalized, name-bearing form of the disconnected letters يس that open Surah 36. Because those letters function in that chapter as a direct address to the Prophet Muhammad, يَاسِين came to be recognized as one of his titles — and it is that same figure who is honored in Al-Saffat 37:130.", "آل (family): Scholars trace آل to an elided form of أَهْل (people, family), with the ه worn away through long use. The root أ-هـ-ل carries a sense of intimate belonging — آل are not merely blood relatives; they are those to whom one intrinsically belongs, those gathered at a single, indivisible source.", "مُحْسِنِين (those who do good): from the root ح-س-ن, meaning beauty and excellence. The verb أَحْسَنَ means to act beautifully and masterfully; a مُحْسِن brings that quality to everything he does. In Arabic, goodness and beauty share a single root — to do good is, at its heart, to make something beautiful."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'eight proofs for the best of human beings', 'student:0c4ab4d484b49d64', 3, 'this phrase and the testimony to guardianship issued from the Command…', 'this phrase and the testimony to guardianship issued from the Commander of the Believers, Ali ibn Abi Talib, concerning himself', '<p>The text does not cite a named chain of transmission to ground the claim that Maulana Ali personally initiated the testimony of guardianship in the call to prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أذان</span>). What it offers is considered structurally stronger: a <strong>theological argument from first principles</strong>, woven from Quranic reasoning and a recognized pattern in divine speech.</p><p>The first thread is self-testimony. Allah bears witness to His own oneness in His Book: <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهِدَ اللهُ أَنَّهُ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا هُوَ</span> Allah bears witness that there is no god but He. Al Imran 3:18 The Prophet testified for himself to prophethood and messengership. The author''s logic follows the <strong>same unbroken pattern</strong>: when the office of guardianship (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولاية</span>) passed to Maulana Ali after the Prophet, it was entirely fitting — even necessary — for him to testify to it concerning himself.</p><p>The second thread is the verse of guardianship, the <strong>Quranic foundation</strong> of the whole case:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّمَا وَلِيُّكُمُ اللهُ وَرَسُولُهُ وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا الَّذِينَ يُقِيمُونَ الصَّلَاةَ وَيُؤْتُونَ الزَّكَاةَ وَهُمْ رَاكِعُونَ</span> Your guardian is only Allah, and His Messenger, and those who believe: those who establish the prayer and give the alms while bowing. Al-Ma''idah 5:55</p><p>The opponents'' own books are filled with narrations confirming this verse was revealed about Maulana Ali — so their sources become witnesses against their own denial.</p><p>One element needs separating. The narration from al-Qadi al-Numan, transmitted from Imam Jafar al-Sadiq, does appear in the chapter — but it is deployed for a <strong>separate question entirely</strong>: the removal of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَّ عَلَى خَيْرِ الْعَمَلِ</span> from the call to prayer. It is not brought forward as a chain of transmission for the testimony of guardianship itself.</p>', '["شهادة (testimony): from root ش-ه-د, meaning to be present at an event and witness it directly. The شاهد is one who was there — whose physical presence is itself the proof. This makes شهادة not merely speech but the utterance of a living witness. The root also gives مشاهدة, direct vision. The word carries the weight of an eyewitness, not a reporter.", "ولاية (guardianship): from root و-ل-ي, to be near, to be close. The ولي stands in proximity — so close that authority flows naturally from the bond itself. The word carries warmth: not a rulership imposed from a distance, but nearness becoming responsibility. ولي also means friend and ally, layering love into the meaning of authority.", "أذان (call to prayer): from root أ-ذ-ن, the same root as أذن, the ear. The أذان is literally what is made known to the ear — a public declaration opened to the hearing of the whole community. إذن (permission) shares this root, carrying the sense of opening one''s ear to receive something freely given."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'where a muslim stands to pray', 'student:d23199399f5b4462', 1, 'one of the licensed men of the mission leads the prayer', 'one of the licensed men of the mission leads the prayer', '<p>The &quot;mission&quot; is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدعوة</span> — literally, the call or summons — through which the Imam of the Time reaches out to humanity. <strong>The call is the living system of guidance</strong> the Imam maintains in every age: a structured hierarchy of teachers and representatives who carry his knowledge, his authority, and his light into the world. Think of it like a royal dispatch network — the Imam at its center, sending out authorized messengers into every community.</p><p>A man who is &quot;licensed&quot; within this mission holds what is called permission (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إذن</span>) — <strong>a delegation of spiritual authority</strong>, not merely a certificate of learning, granted by the Imam or his senior representatives; it connects the holder to the Imam''s chain of living guidance. The one who carries it is called a licensed representative (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مأذون</span>), a word rooted in the Arabic for ear, because true authorization begins with the Imam truly hearing and endorsing that person.</p><p>This is why the text treats praying in the mosque, under a <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مأذون</span>, with such seriousness. The mosque led by an authorized representative of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدعوة</span> is not simply a building — it is <strong>a living node in the Imam''s network</strong>, where the congregation gathers not only facing the direction of prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القبلة</span>) but in spiritual alignment with the Imam''s mission itself, connected to something far larger than any single act of worship.</p>', '["الدعوة (mission / the call): from root د-ع-و, to call or invite. The root yields الداعي (the caller, the representative) and the call itself. Every Arabic invitation — even a wedding دعوة — comes from here. In the Ismaili tradition, the الدعوة is Allah''s standing summons to humanity, channeled through the Imam and his authorized hierarchy.", "إذن (permission / authorization): from root أ-ذ-ن, meaning to give ear or to grant leave. Notice that أُذُن (the ear) shares this root — true permission begins with genuinely hearing someone. To grant إذن is to have listened and said yes. The مأذون is one whose request the Imam has truly heard and endorsed.", "مأذون (the licensed representative): from root أ-ذ-ن, cast in the passive pattern مَفْعُول — meaning one to whom إذن has been given. The form signals that authority flows downward from the Imam, not upward from the crowd. He holds his role as a trust received, never self-claimed — the pattern itself encodes that humility."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'where a muslim stands to pray', 'student:cd21eda8fe5ea330', 2, 'he prays toward both directions together; and the like of this has be…', 'he prays toward both directions together; and the like of this has been mentioned concerning the four directions', '<p>The situation described is one where no amount of careful thinking resolves anything — the worshipper has genuinely exhausted his independent judgment (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اجتهاد</span>) and still finds both directions equally convincing. The solution is to pray twice, once facing each of the two suspected directions. One of those prayers must face the correct direction (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القبلة</span>), fulfilling the obligation regardless. The ruling is about <strong>covering both possibilities</strong> when the mind cannot genuinely choose between them.</p><p>The parallel ruling about four directions extends this same logic outward. If all four cardinal directions appear equally plausible — north, south, east, and west all looking like valid candidates — the worshipper prays four separate times, once toward each. When certainty is impossible, <strong>completeness becomes the answer</strong>.</p><p>The ruling carries mercy: the worshipper is not paralyzed by confusion, nor simply let off, but given a way forward that honors both the obligation and his genuine limitation. The tradition does not punish sincere effort — it shapes it into action.</p><p>What leads up to this ruling also matters. A worshipper who can form a judgment must form one and pray — and even if he discovers an error mid-prayer, he simply turns toward the House (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الكعبة</span>) and completes it, as long as he was not facing entirely away from it. The &quot;pray toward both&quot; or &quot;pray toward all four&quot; ruling applies only at the very <strong>edge of total uncertainty</strong>, when no direction prevails at all.</p>', '["القبلة (the prayer direction): from root ق-ب-ل, meaning to face, to turn toward, to receive. The verb قَبَلَ means ''he faced.'' القبلة is literally ''the facing place'' — the direction a worshipper turns toward Allah. The same root gives مُقَابِل (opposite, facing) and قِبَل (in front of), showing how directed orientation runs through every derivative.", "اجتهاد (independent judgment): from root ج-ه-د, meaning to strive and exhaust one''s effort. The verb جَهَدَ means ''he struggled.'' اجتهاد is the verbal noun of the eighth verb form اِجْتَهَدَ — naming the full mental exertion applied to reach a ruling. The same root gives جِهَاد (striving) and مُجْتَهِد (one who exerts judgment): اجتهاد is the mind pushed to its absolute limit."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'where a muslim stands to pray', 'student:e73144b39c7eb33e', 3, 'prayer has seven ranks, by which the prayers of those who pray are gr…', 'prayer has seven ranks, by which the prayers of those who pray are graded one above another', '<p>The seven ranks of prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلاة</span>) rest on <strong>a single governing principle</strong>: proximity to the living center of divine guidance amplifies the weight of every act of worship. Think of it like sound waves radiating from a single source — the closer you stand, the fuller and more resonant the sound.</p><p><strong>Each number is anchored in the spiritual arithmetic of the tradition</strong>:</p><ul><li><p>Home → 1 prayer</p></li><li><p>Market mosque → 12 prayers</p></li><li><p>Tribal mosque → 25 prayers</p></li><li><p>Congregational mosque → 100 prayers</p></li><li><p>The Farthest Mosque → 1,000 prayers</p></li><li><p>The Prophet''s mosque in Madinah → 10,000 prayers</p></li><li><p>The Sacred House → 100,000 prayers</p></li></ul><p>Twelve is deeply embedded in Ismaili spiritual reckoning — twelve months, twelve proofs (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجج</span>) who anchor a cycle of guidance through time. Twenty-five echoes the teaching that congregational prayer surpasses solitary prayer by that exact measure, and from a hundred onward the multiplier climbs by a factor of ten at every step, signaling not merely a larger gathering but a qualitative leap into a new order of spiritual magnitude.</p><p>The <strong>sevenfold structure</strong> itself carries weight. In Ismaili thought, seven marks the completed cycle: seven Imams in each prophetic era, seven degrees in the chain of divine guidance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حدود الدين</span>), seven heavens in the cosmological map. The seven prayer-locations mirror those seven degrees exactly — at one end the solitary soul in its private room, at the other the Sacred House (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحرم</span>), the axis around which all prayer-direction (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قبلة</span>) is oriented.</p><p>The &quot;abundant meanings and likenesses of wisdom&quot; that the text promises but does not supply are precisely the <strong>inner interpretation</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span>) that maps each location onto a rung in the hierarchy of divine knowledge. The numbers are not prizes for religious travel; they are a map of how deeply a soul has moved toward its origin. A worshipper who grasps this does not merely count the multiplier — she or he feels the pull of that sacred center in every act of turning toward the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قبلة</span>.</p>', '["مسجد (mosque): from the root س-ج-د, meaning to press the forehead to the ground in prostration. The derived noun مسجد names the place where that act of surrender (سجود) is performed. The root captures something physical — an actual pressing-down — and the word built from it carries that gravity: not just a building, but consecrated ground of bowing.", "تأويل (inner interpretation): from the root أ-و-ل, meaning to return to the very first thing. The Form II verb أوّل means to lead something back to its origin, and تأويل is that act of leading back. In Ismaili thought this is exactly what inner interpretation does — it does not add new meaning to the outward text; it retrieves the first and hidden source the letter conceals.", "حدود (degrees of the hierarchy): from the root ح-د-د, to sharpen or set a precise boundary. The singular حد is a sharp edge, a defined line. In Ismaili religious usage, حدود الدين are the ranked stations of the divine mission — each حد a clear boundary where one level of spiritual authority ends and the next begins.", "قبلة (direction of prayer): from the root ق-ب-ل, meaning to face, to come before, or to receive. The قبلة is literally the facing — the direction toward which we turn. Crucially the root also carries the sense of reception; turning toward the قبلة is not only an act of address but an opening of oneself to what flows from that sacred center."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'who may lead, and who may not', 'student:4f5a2931d0daf84a', 1, 'one granted license who prays with the people while some of the commo…', 'one granted license who prays with the people while some of the common people are with him', '<p>The one granted license (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مأذون</span>) is a <strong>formally authorized representative</strong> within the Ismaili da''wa hierarchy — &quot;license&quot; translates <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إذن</span>, a word rooted in the idea of opening one''s ear to someone and granting them access. The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مأذون</span> has been vetted, trusted, and appointed by someone of higher rank to act on behalf of the hierarchy in a defined space: a mosque, a city, or a region.</p><p>Authority flows downward through the ranks. At the summit sits the Imam; below him stand figures like the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجة</span> and the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داعي</span>. It is typically the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داعي</span> who grants the license, effectively saying: &quot;In this place, you represent us.&quot; The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مأذون</span> of Rajpur leads in Rajpur, and when two <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مأذون</span> meet outside their own jurisdictions, the one whose mosque it is holds the stronger right.</p><p>Not all license-holders stand at the same level. An <strong>unrestricted</strong> <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مأذون</span> holds considerably wider powers:</p><ul><li><p>He may appoint a substitute to complete the prayer if his own ablution breaks mid-prayer.</p></li><li><p>He may lead a congregation even without having explicitly declared an intention to lead.</p></li><li><p>When a figure of higher rank arrives, that person takes precedence — not as a challenge, but as the hierarchy expressing itself naturally.</p></li></ul><p>The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مأذون</span> often prays among those who do not follow the way of the People of the House (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِمُ السَّلَامُ</span>), and so <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تقية</span> — protective concealment — becomes part of his daily practice. He may lower his voice when reciting:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful</p><p>or omit the communal response:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا لَكَ الْحَمْدُ</span> Our Lord, to You is all praise</p><p>when outsiders stand beside him. This is not weakness — it is <strong>pastoral stewardship</strong>, a recognition that preserving the community is itself a sacred act.</p>', '["مأذون (one granted license): from the root أ-ذ-ن, meaning to open the ear and, by extension, to grant permission. مأذون is ''the one who has been permitted.'' The same root gives إذن (permission) and أذان (the call to prayer): each is an act of opening access — one calls souls to worship, the other authorizes a person to serve.", "تقية (protective concealment): from the root و-ق-ي, meaning to shield or ward off. The believer guards their faith by not exposing it to those who would harm them. The same root yields تقوى, Allah-consciousness: both centre on protection — تقوى guards the soul from sin; تقية guards the believer from danger.", "داعي (summoner): from the root د-ع-و, meaning to call, invite, or summon. The داعي is ''the one who calls,'' inviting souls toward recognition of the Imam. The same root yields دعاء (supplication) and دعوة (the call or mission): calling upon Allah and calling others toward Him spring from the same impulse.", "حجة (proof): from the root ح-ج-ج, meaning to argue conclusively or establish proof. The حجة is the Imam''s senior representative, whose authority is itself evidence of the Imam''s presence. The same root gives حج, the pilgrimage: the pilgrim, like the حجة, travels toward an undeniable center."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'who may lead, and who may not', 'student:ac388051164759d7', 2, 'the nasib is set aside if he has led them from the side', 'the nasib is set aside if he has led them from the side', '<p>The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نصيب</span>, at its root, means a fixed allotment — a share formally set up and assigned to someone. When <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْمُنْتَخَبَةُ</span> says that &quot;the nasib is set aside,&quot; it is referring to <strong>the blind imam''s own entitlement</strong> to that act of leading. The three conditions <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْمُنْتَخَبَةُ</span> lays down form a coherent sequence: he may only lead when someone physically orients him; he cannot hold a permanent appointment as standing imam; and if he has led from the side, even that one-time leading does not stand.</p><p>&quot;Leading from the side&quot; describes an error almost unique to a blind imam. The imam stands at the head of the rows, squarely facing the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قبلة</span> and positioned ahead of everyone. A blind man who has not been properly set straight may drift laterally — ending up beside the first row rather than ahead of it, offset from the apex of the congregation. He might be reciting correctly and moving through every posture, yet standing to the left or right of the people rather than in front of them.</p><p>Think of it like a conductor who ends up standing among the musicians rather than facing them — the music may still play, but <strong>the role itself has not been discharged</strong>. His <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نصيب</span> is cancelled. The blind imam''s permission to lead is always conditional and narrow; this is the narrowest edge of that condition.</p>', '["نصيب (share, allotment): from the root ن-ص-ب, meaning to set up, erect, or fix firmly in place. A نصيب is a portion that has been stood up and formally appointed for a person — their fixed due. The فعيل pattern reinforces the sense of something established and stable. This is precisely why leading from the side forfeits it: what was fixed for the blind imam is undone by the irregularity of his position."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'who may lead, and who may not', 'student:e975004f51ac2ec9', 3, 'the imam''s hujja, who stands in the imam''s place after him, arrives a…', 'the imam''s hujja, who stands in the imam''s place after him, arrives and likewise finds no place in the rows, he steps forward and stands level with the imam and prays with him. This is permitted to no one else.', '<p>The standing of worshippers in prayer rows is not a matter of physical convenience alone — <strong>it is a living map of spiritual rank</strong>. In the Ismaili understanding, every act of outward worship carries an inner meaning (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span>), and the arrangement of bodies in prayer mirrors the hierarchy of the sacred summons (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span>).</p><p>The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجة</span> occupies a unique position in this order. The word comes from the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ح</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ج</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ج</span>, meaning to establish conclusive proof and to overcome in argument, and so the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجة</span> is <strong>the Imam''s living proof</strong> before the faithful. He is designated to stand in the Imam''s place when the Imam is absent, carrying the Imam''s authority downward through the ranks of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span>. This is not a ceremonial title — he is the Imam''s active presence to those below him.</p><p><strong>Standing level with the imam</strong> is therefore a privilege the text reserves exclusively for the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجة</span>. When every row is full and an ordinary worshipper arrives, he prays alone to the side and never steps forward into the imam''s rank — the prayer''s outer form confirms that truth. But the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجة</span> already belongs at that level of proximity — for him to stand apart would make the outer form lie about the inner reality.</p><p>Think of it like a court. <strong>The chief deputy may share the bench</strong> when no other seat remains, because his authority already belongs there; any other officer who did the same would be presuming beyond their rank. The prayer row works by the same logic: no one may claim a position above their standing in the spiritual order, even under necessity.</p><p>This coherence runs through all of Ismaili practice — <strong>the outer form and the inner reality are always made to agree</strong>. The visible (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظاهر</span>) and the hidden (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>) are not two separate worlds; they are the same truth seen from two directions. The prayer row is the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span> made visible, each soul arranged by its proximity to truth, all prostrating together before Allah.</p>', '["حجة (proof): from the root ح-ج-ج, meaning to argue, to establish conclusive proof, and to overcome in disputation. The same root yields حج — pilgrimage — where both ideas converge in purposeful, directed movement toward a centre of truth. The حجة makes the Imam''s reality undeniable to those who seek it.", "دعوة (summons): from the root د-ع-و, meaning to call, to summon, to invite toward. The same root gives دعاء (supplication) — in both cases a voice reaches across distance seeking response. The دعوة is the sacred call extended through the Imam''s hierarchy, drawing souls toward the light of his authority.", "إمام (leader): from the root أ-م-م, meaning to go before, to stand in front, to be a model others orient toward. Related to أمام (in front of), أم (mother — the one all return to), and أمة (community). The إمام is the one through whom direction is found; to follow him is to be oriented toward truth.", "تأويل (inner meaning): from the root أ-و-ل, meaning to return to the first principle, to trace a thing back to its source. The تأويل of an act is not interpretation in the ordinary sense — it is the restoration of its original depth. The root ties meaning-making to the idea of return.", "ظاهر (outward): from the root ظ-ه-ر, meaning to appear, to be manifest, to rise to the surface. The same root gives ظهر (back — the visible side of the body) and ظهور (emergence). In Ismaili thought, the ظاهر is the surface of a truth whose depth the باطن holds.", "باطن (hidden): from the root ب-ط-ن, meaning to be concealed within, to lie in the interior. The same root gives بطن (belly — the interior of the body). What is باطن lies inside, invisible to the surface glance. Every outward form carries a باطن that gives it its real and lasting meaning."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the shape of the prayer itself', 'student:74c5bffe9452977e', 1, 'whoever abandons the prayer without excuse, or abandons any of the pr…', 'whoever abandons the prayer without excuse, or abandons any of the prescribed works, has broken the covenant', '<p>The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ميثاق</span> — the pledge of loyalty and obedience — is formally taken with the living Imam through the summoner (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داعي</span>), his appointed representative. Entering it is not agreeing to a list of rules but <strong>a living bond between the soul and its spiritual guide</strong>. Everything listed — prayer, moral conduct, obedience — is daily evidence that this bond holds.</p><p>This is why each act listed breaks the covenant. Abandoning prayer without excuse, committing fornication, killing a believer, or refusing to believe a fellow believer''s oath — <strong>each tears the fabric of the community</strong> that the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ميثاق</span> is meant to weave together and protect, each an act of withdrawal from a solemnly contracted relationship.</p><p>The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ميثاق</span> is rooted in something far older — the primordial agreement in which every soul bore witness before Allah:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ أَخَذَ رَبُّكَ مِن بَنِى ءَادَمَ مِن ظُهُورِهِمْ ذُرِّيَّتَهُمْ وَأَشْهَدَهُمْ عَلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ أَلَسْتُ بِرَبِّكُمْ ۖ قَالُوا۟ بَلَىٰ ۛ شَهِدْنَآ</span></p><p>And when your Lord took from the children of Adam — from their loins — their descendants and made them testify: ''Am I not your Lord?'' They said, ''Yes, we have testified.'' Al-A''raf 7:172</p><p>This original pledge is <strong>renewed in every era through the living Imam</strong>, who holds the believer accountable to it in their time.</p><p>The instruction about striving with person and wealth at the summoner''s command brings this into sharpest focus. The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داعي</span> does not issue personal orders — he relays the Imam''s guidance, and <strong>refusing him without excuse is refusing the Imam himself</strong>. Breaking the covenant is the severance of the soul''s living connection to the Imam''s light.</p>', '["ميثاق (covenant): from the root و-ث-ق, meaning to be firm, secure, and to bind tightly. Related words: وثيقة (a binding document) and وثيق (firm, strong). The ميثاق is literally something that holds you fast — not just a promise spoken once, but a tie that fixes the believer to something larger than themselves.", "داعي (summoner): from the root د-ع-و, meaning to call, to invite, to summon. Related words: دعوة (mission or call) and دعاء (supplication). The داعي is literally ''the one who calls'' — the root captures exactly his role: to bridge the believer and the Imam by drawing souls toward the light of guidance.", "عهد (covenant, agreement): from the root ع-ه-د, meaning to commit, to know closely, to entrust. Related: عاهد (one who covenants) and معهد (institute or place of covenant). The عهد is not signed once and set aside — it is something continuously remembered and lived, which is why abandoning its terms is treated as an active breach."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the shape of the prayer itself', 'student:4db67c6723bcb23f', 2, 'the one who abandons my sunnah is cursed. Meaning: the one who abando…', 'the one who abandons my sunnah is cursed. Meaning: the one who abandons my legatee is one banished from the mercy of God', '<p>The Ismaili method of inner interpretation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span>) peels back the surface of a word to reveal the truth it was always pointing toward — its hidden reality (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>) beneath its outward form (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظاهر</span>). This is what happens when the hadith</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تارك سنتي ملعون</span> The one who abandons my sunnah is cursed.</p><p>is read as pointing to the legatee — not a creative departure, but a return to what the words actually carry. The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ملعون</span>, from the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ن</span>, means one who has been <strong>driven to the far distance</strong> — expelled, cut off. &quot;Banished from the mercy of Allah&quot; is therefore a linguistically exact rendering, not a loose paraphrase.</p><p>The key lies in the word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنة</span> itself. From the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">س</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ن</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ن</span> — to lay down a path, to set something in continuous flow — a <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنة</span> is not a collection of habits but a living way that must be carried forward. In Ismaili understanding, the one appointed to carry the Prophet''s way forward across time is the legatee (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وصي</span>), Maulana Ali. Abandoning the legatee is therefore <strong>abandoning the sunnah at its living source</strong>.</p><p>This is reinforced through the structure of prayer, which divides into two kinds: obligatory and sunnah. In Ismaili doctrine, the Prophet and the legatee are always a pair — the speaking one (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ناطق</span>) who declares the revelation, and the preserving one (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وصي</span>) who holds its inner reality. The obligatory prayer corresponds to the Prophet''s revelation; the sunnah prayer corresponds to the legatee. <strong>The sunnah dimension of religion is the legatee dimension</strong> — to abandon one is to abandon the other. Maulana Ali''s own words confirm this:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا حظ في الإسلام لمن ترك الصلاة</span> There is no portion in Islam for the one who abandons the prayer.</p>', '["سنة (sunnah): from the root س-ن-ن, meaning to lay down a path, set something flowing, or to smooth and shape a course. سنة is an established way — worn into being by repeated motion. The root captures something that must be actively kept moving to stay alive; without a carrier, a سنة simply ceases to flow.", "ملعون (the banished one): from the root ل-ع-ن, meaning to drive away, to expel to the outer boundary. ملعون is not vaguely ''cursed'' — it is one who has been cast out, severed, pushed to the extreme far edge. ''Banished from the mercy of Allah'' is therefore a linguistically exact reading, not an interpretive addition.", "تأويل (inner interpretation): from the root أ-و-ل, meaning to return to the beginning, to bring something back to its first origin. تأويل traces a word or act back to the original truth it emerged from — not projecting meaning in, but following meaning home. The root tells us that every outward form has an originating reality it came from.", "وصي (legatee): from the root و-ص-ي, meaning to join, connect, or to bequeath by binding one thing to another. A وصي is the one to whom something is entrusted at the moment of transmission — the one who receives the Prophet''s trust and holds the chain unbroken. The root itself is a description of continuity."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the shape of the prayer itself', 'student:e84b71b6fa32fff7', 3, 'it is because the outward prayer neither forbids nor commands', 'it is because the outward prayer neither forbids nor commands', '<p>When the people of Shuayb ask whether his prayer commanded them to abandon their fathers'' worship, they are treating <strong>the outward act of worship</strong> as though it were itself the source of moral command — demanding that visible movements justify themselves as an authority.</p><p>The outward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الظاهر</span>) is contrasted with the inward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الباطن</span>) — the distinction running through all Ismaili thought about worship. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الظاهر</span> is the visible form: the movements, the words, the postures. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الباطن</span> is <strong>the living reality those forms are meant to carry</strong>:</p><ul><li><p>the soul''s recognition of the Imam of the Time</p></li><li><p>the walaya (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الولاية</span>) flowing from him</p></li><li><p>the genuine submission that reshapes a person from within</p></li></ul><p>It is this inward reality that <strong>truly commands and forbids</strong>, because it acts upon the heart and not merely on the body.</p><p>The hadith cited just before the Shuayb passage makes this unmistakable: <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ لَمْ تَنْهَ صَلَاتُهُ عَنِ الْفَحْشَاءِ وَالْمُنْكَرِ لَمْ يَزْدَدْ مِنَ اللَّهِ اِلَّا بُعْدًا</span> Whoever is not restrained by his prayer from indecency and wrongdoing increases in nothing but distance from Allah.</p><p><strong>A prayer without its inward meaning</strong> does not transform the person — it only repeats the same movements, accumulating distance rather than closeness.</p><p>Shuayb''s people could only see the shell; they could not perceive the prophetic guidance animating his worship from within. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الظاهر</span> is real and obligatory, but it is a vessel. The <strong>living walaya</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الولاية</span>) is what the vessel carries, and that is what truly commands the soul.</p>', '["ظاهر (outward, manifest): from the root ظ-ه-ر, meaning to rise to the surface and appear plainly. الظاهر is literally what shows itself — the visible face of a thing. The root also gives ظهر (the back), which faces outward by nature. In worship, الظاهر is everything the prayer presents to the eye.", "باطن (inward, hidden): from the root ب-ط-ن, meaning belly or interior. الباطن is what lives in the belly of a thing — concealed beneath the surface. The root gives بطن (abdomen), suggesting depth and hiddenness. In worship, الباطن is the inner reality the form carries but cannot itself make visible.", "صلاة (prayer): from the root ص-ل-و, carrying the sense of connection and following closely. Prayer is a living link between the servant and Allah. Some linguists trace it to صَلَا (the lower back), since bowing and prostration involve that part of the body, binding physical movement to inward submission.", "الولاية (walaya, guardianship and proximity): from the root و-ل-ي, meaning nearness, closeness, and taking charge. الولي is one who is both close to someone and entrusted with their care. الولاية combines intimate nearness with legitimate authority — not mere loyalty, but a living bond carrying the Imam''s divine guidance to the believer''s soul."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the shape of the prayer itself', 'student:75609ef82ccded4e', 4, 'every prayer in which there is no first sitting has a qunut that stan…', 'every prayer in which there is no first sitting has a qunut that stands in its place in the count', '<p>The twelve sunnahs (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنة</span>) of prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلاة</span>) form a <strong>complete structural system</strong> — not a loose collection of extras, but a precise count that must balance across every prayer type. A two-cycle prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ركعة</span>) has only one sitting — the final one — so the &quot;first sitting&quot; simply does not exist in its structure. Without something to fill that slot, the count falls to eleven, and the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قنوت</span> steps in exactly there.</p><p>The <strong>first sitting</strong> in a four-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ركعة</span> prayer is a deliberate mid-prayer pause: the worshipper sits after the second cycle and recites the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تشهد</span> — a verbal act of witness before Allah. The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قنوت</span> performs that same act: hands raised, voice lowered, the worshipper addresses Allah in extended supplication. Same inner movement, different posture.</p><p>What makes them <strong>structurally equivalent</strong> is what they share:</p><ul><li><p>Both are deliberate, worded moments of address to Allah beyond the main recitation</p></li><li><p>Both interrupt the physical rhythm of bowing and prostrating with a pause of inner presence</p></li><li><p>Both are <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنة</span> elements — not the bare minimum for a valid prayer, but what makes it whole</p></li></ul><p>This is why prayers with two sittings carry no <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قنوت</span> — the first sitting <strong>already occupies that slot</strong>. The system is exact; each prayer type assembles its twelve <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنة</span> elements differently but always arrives at the same total.</p><p>In Ismaili thought, every physical act of worship reflects an inner reality. The first sitting and the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قنوت</span> are two expressions of the same spiritual moment: the point where the worshipper makes <strong>direct, personal address</strong> to Allah — not in prescribed recitation, not in communal praise, but in a voice entirely their own. That functional identity is why one can stand in for the other.</p>', '["قنوت: from the root ق-ن-ت, meaning to be obedient, to remain devoutly standing. The root carries sustained, humble compliance before a master — inner and physical at once. From it comes قانت (the devoutly obedient one). The قنوت is that posture made verbal: a standing of quiet, worded submission directed entirely at Allah.", "تشهد: from the root ش-ه-د, meaning to witness, to be present, to testify. The شاهد is one who was there and can attest. The verb form تشهّد means ''he bore witness.'' In the prayer''s sitting, the worshipper تشهّد — formally declares their faith before Allah — turning a moment of stillness into an act of conscious, personal testimony.", "ركعة: from the root ر-ك-ع, meaning to bow, to bend the back in deference. The ركوع — the bowing — is the act that defines a ركعة, so the full cycle of standing, bowing, and prostrating takes its name from that single gesture of submission. A prayer of two ركعات has fewer cycles, which is why its structure differs."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'supplication, speech, and silence', 'student:29991a3821e1c599', 1, 'the supplication of drawing near, or all supplications', 'the supplication of drawing near, or all supplications', '<p>The supplication of drawing near (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ التَّقَرُّبِ</span>) is a <strong>specific, named liturgical prayer</strong> — a known text with a known purpose — that Ismaili worshippers recite after the daily obligatory prayers. The chapter assumes familiarity with it the way a teacher assumes a student already knows what &quot;the opening chapter&quot; means; every practitioner recognizes it.</p><p>The seven types that follow are categories based on <strong>posture and gesture</strong> — each defined not by what is said but by how the body is held:</p><ul><li><p>asking (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ الْمَسْأَلَةِ</span>) — palms facing the worshipper</p></li><li><p>seeking refuge (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ الْاِسْتِعَاذَةِ</span>) — palms turned away</p></li><li><p>humble submission (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ الْاِسْتِكَانَةِ</span>) — hands grasping the shoulders</p></li><li><p>devotion (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ التَّبَتُّلِ</span>) — the index finger raised</p></li><li><p>entreaty (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ التَّضَرُّعِ</span>) — the finger moving</p></li><li><p>fervent appeal (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ الْاِبْتِهَالِ</span>) — both arms extended skyward</p></li><li><p>abasement in the dust (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ التَّعْفِيرِ</span>) — cheeks pressed to the ground</p></li></ul><p>Not one of these is defined by a particular text or topic — they are modes, not prayers.</p><p>Imagine someone asks: &quot;Should we sing our national anthem tonight, or any song at all?&quot; The national anthem is <strong>a specific, cherished text with its own identity</strong>; &quot;a song&quot; is a broad category. The supplication of drawing near (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ التَّقَرُّبِ</span>) is like the national anthem; the seven types are like musical genres — hymn, lament, march — that describe how any song might be performed.</p><p>The questioner wonders whether the ruling — that supplication is discouraged after the afternoon prayer — applies only to this one well-known prayer, or to <strong>every form of supplication</strong> altogether. The answer is that it covers all supplications, not only this specific one, and that no sin falls on whoever does supplicate.</p><p>The supplication of drawing near (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ التَّقَرُّبِ</span>) is not missing from the list of seven — it was never meant to appear there. The seven types are a taxonomy of form; <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءُ التَّقَرُّبِ</span> is a specific act of worship. One could recite it with palms spread wide in the mode of fervent appeal, or with the index finger raised in the mode of devotion — the two frameworks describe <strong>different dimensions</strong> of the same practice.</p>', '["دُعَاءُ (supplication): from the root د-ع-و, meaning to call or to summon. دَعَا means to call out toward someone; the noun دُعَاءُ is the act of that calling. Every supplication is therefore a cry directed upward — a voice naming what the heart most deeply needs, cast across the distance between us and Allah.", "التَّقَرُّبِ (drawing near): from the root ق-ر-ب, meaning to be close. The verb قَرُبَ means to come near; the reflexive form تَقَرَّبَ means to bring oneself near — to actively close the distance between the soul and its source. This supplication is not merely a request; it is a movement of the whole self toward Allah.", "الْاِبْتِهَالِ (fervent appeal): from the root ب-ه-ل, meaning to be free and unrestrained — to pour oneself out without holding anything back. The form اِبْتِهَالٌ carries this sense of total release, which is why this mode calls for both palms spread wide and both arms raised high: the body itself becomes the expression of an open heart.", "التَّعْفِيرِ (abasement in the dust): from the root ع-ف-ر, referring to dust or the bare surface of the earth (عَفَر). To perform تَعْفِيرٌ is to press one''s cheek into that earth — not as degradation, but as the soul''s acknowledgment that before Allah all pretension dissolves, and all distance collapses."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'supplication, speech, and silence', 'student:7dc0e3b5c2a1067b', 2, 'Supplication turns back the decree that has been firmly settled', 'Supplication turns back the decree that has been firmly settled', '<p>The paradox is only on the surface. The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مبرم</span> carries the image of a rope twisted and braided until it cannot be undone. So when Maulana Ali says that supplication (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدعاء</span>) reverses even this, he is not describing a loophole in the divine order. He is pointing to a <strong>mechanism woven into creation itself</strong>.</p><p>The twelve foundations of supplication make that mechanism visible. Before any petition is voiced, the believer repents, gives charity, fasts or prays, purifies the heart — and then, most critically, draws near through the Imams of the religion, naming them one by one until reaching the living Imam of the age. A supplication built this way is no longer the cry of an isolated, unchanged self; it is a soul that has passed through the living chain of divine guidance and <strong>reached the level where decrees originate</strong>.</p><p>The Quran names this movement:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَيْهِ يَصْعَدُ ٱلْكَلِمُ ٱلطَّيِّبُ وَٱلْعَمَلُ ٱلصَّلِحُ يَرْفَعُهُ</span> To Him ascends the good word, and the righteous deed raises it.</p><p>Fatir 35:10</p><p>It is righteous action that <strong>carries the word upward</strong> — which is precisely what Maulana Ali means when he says the one who supplicates without deeds is like an archer with no bowstring. The arrow is the petition; the string is the transformed life behind it.</p><p>The deepest point is this: the decree was sealed upon a soul in a specific state — heedless, distant, unchanged. <strong>The transformed soul</strong> that emerges from the twelve foundations is no longer that person, and the seal no longer fits the new shape of the self. Because Allah''s knowledge already contained this moment of turning, the reversal was always part of the divine order, not a breach of it.</p>', '["القضاء (decree): from the root ق-ض-ي, meaning to complete, to settle, or to execute to the end. What has been قُضِيَ is cut through and finalised — the root carries the image of a thing driven to its conclusion with no thread left hanging. The word serves for both ''decree'' and ''judgment'' because in divine speech the two are a single act.", "مبرم (firmly settled): from the root ب-ر-م, meaning to twist or braid tightly. A cord that has been أُبْرِمَ is wound so fast it resists loosening. The word is tactile — you feel the grip of it. That is precisely what makes Maulana Ali''s teaching so charged: even the braided rope of fate yields to sincere supplication.", "الدعاء (supplication): from the root د-ع-و, meaning to call out or to summon. The same root gives دعا — he called. Supplication is not a silent petition; it is a voice launched across the distance between creature and Creator, naming what is needed and trusting it will be heard and answered."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'three questions from the king of rum', 'student:cd12ec9ae674f147', 1, 'the first thing that appeared on the face of the earth, it is the val…', 'the first thing that appeared on the face of the earth, it is the valley of Dab', '<p>The valley of Dab (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وادي دب</span>) is not described in terms of its landscape or location. What it gives instead is something more consequential: the name is spoken by Imam al-Hasan (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) as the answer to a direct challenge about cosmological knowledge, posed by a Syrian envoy sent to test whether the household of the Prophet hold <strong>knowledge that no political appointment can manufacture</strong>.</p><p>The tradition belongs to a book called <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أنس الأفئدة المنيبة</span>, a text preserving accounts of the wisdom and rulings of Maulana Ali (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) and those who inherit his authority. In that account, the envoy poses eleven questions as a proof of legitimate leadership. Al-Hasan (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) answers every one, and when the answers reach Ibn al-Asfar — the man who originally composed the questions — he concedes without hesitation: ''these come only from the mine of prophecy and the seat of the message.'' The answer itself is <strong>the proof of authority</strong>, and no rival claimant had produced a single word in response.</p><p>In Ismaili cosmological thought, this matters beyond mere geography. The celestial spheres were understood to have shaped the earth in ordered succession, each one drawing forth minerals, plants, and living things according to its nature. The earth''s first moment of emergence from the primordial waters is a <strong>sacred threshold</strong> in that picture. The same passage identifies <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصالحون</span> as the Imams, citing the Quran:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَّ ٱلْأَرْضَ يَرِثُهَا عِبَادِىَ ٱلصَّلِحُونَ</span> The earth is inherited by My righteous servants. Al-Anbiya 21:105</p><p>If the earth belongs to the Imams, it is the Imam who most fittingly holds knowledge of where that earth first began.</p><p>No other widely known Islamic tradition names <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وادي دب</span> as the first land to appear; the identification is <strong>particular to the Ismaili narrative tradition</strong>, carried through the chain of the Imam''s knowledge.</p>', '["وادي (valley): from the root و-د-ي, conveying flow and movement. A وادي is a channel carved by running water — the valley is named for what shaped it. The derivative وَدِيَ means to flow freely. This name reminds us that the first valley was not just a landform; it was a record of water''s earliest movement across the face of the earth.", "دب (the valley''s name): from the root د-ب-ب, meaning to creep or move slowly and close to the ground. The derivative دابة means a creature that moves upon the earth. The name evokes quiet, steady emergence — earth pressing upward from beneath the primordial water for the first time, like something alive beginning to stir.", "صالح (righteous): from the root ص-ل-ح, meaning to be sound, fitting, and properly ordered. The word does not merely mean ''good'' — it means fit for purpose, in the right condition for what is required. In this passage the Imams are الصالحون — those most fit to carry the divine trust and to inherit the earth as its rightful stewards.", "مولى (master, guardian): from the root و-ل-ي, meaning to be close, to stand near, to take charge. A مولى is the one who stands nearest to you — protector, intimate, and authority in one word. Allah uses this word for His own relationship to the Prophet, and Maulana Ali (ع) carries this title because his nearness to the message is unmatched."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'what the worshipper wears and stands upon', 'student:e0ad2eb7ebe951f1', 1, 'Obligatory prayer upon them is not permitted; only the supererogatory…', 'Obligatory prayer upon them is not permitted; only the supererogatory and the sunna prayer.', '<p>The ruling turns on a principle that runs through all of Ismaili jurisprudence: <strong>the weightier the obligation, the stricter its conditions.</strong> Obligatory prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فرض</span>) is what every worshipper owes Allah as an inescapable debt. Supererogatory prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نافلة</span>) and the sunna are freely given acts of devotion beyond that minimum, held to a lighter standard.</p><p>A Prophetic saying bears directly on the question:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُعِلَتْ لِي الْأَرْضُ مَسْجِدًا وَطَهُورًا</span> The earth has been made for me a place of prostration and a means of purification.</p><p>From this flows a precise ruling: <strong>anything from which dry ablution (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تيمم</span>) cannot be performed is not a valid surface for prayer.</strong> Dry ablution works by striking fine earth and drawing it across the face and hands as a substitute purification. Both stone and unbaked brick fall short of that standard:</p><ul><li><p>Stone is hard and yields no fine dust.</p></li><li><p>Unbaked brick, though shaped from clay, has stiffened past that loose, purifying quality.</p></li></ul><p>For voluntary prayers, the calculus shifts — not because the conditions are abandoned, but because the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نافلة</span> is a gift, not a debt. Think of how a formal contract requires precise language and witnesses, while a personal letter may be written on whatever paper is at hand — <strong>the obligatory is the contract; the supererogatory is the letter.</strong> Stone and unbaked brick, imperfect as they are, are close enough to earth to carry the voluntary prayer without invalidating it.</p>', '["فرض: from the root ف-ر-ض, meaning to cut, notch, or incise. Just as a notch carved into wood marks a fixed, irreversible point, a فرض is a duty cut into the law — binding and inescapable. The image of incision explains why an obligatory act admits no relaxation in its conditions.", "نافلة: from the root ن-ف-ل, meaning to give something additional or beyond what is owed. The plural أنفال — extra gains — appears in the Quran. A نافلة is a prayer offered as a bonus beyond the required account, which is precisely why it carries lighter ritual conditions than the فرض.", "تيمم: from the root ي-م-م, meaning to aim at or to intend toward something. Performing تيمم means purposefully turning to the earth as a substitute purifier when water is unavailable. The root''s sense of deliberate direction reminds us that intention is woven into the very name of this act.", "سجود: from the root س-ج-د, meaning to bow low or to incline toward the ground in complete submission. The word captures not a polite nod but a total lowering of the self — which is why the surface of prostration is guarded so carefully, and the forehead''s resting place must be worthy."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'what the worshipper wears and stands upon', 'student:32b1238fb1ef358f', 2, 'A man is not to pray in a shirt draped loosely across him, for that i…', 'A man is not to pray in a shirt draped loosely across him, for that is the practice of the people of Lot.', '<p>The link Maulana Ali draws is not arbitrary — it is a convergence of moral memory and physical risk. The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">متوشح</span> style, a shirt draped loosely across the body without being belted or properly fastened, was associated in the tradition with the people of Lot, a community whose identity in the Quran is inseparable from the <strong>deliberate abandonment of shame</strong>. When a man enters prayer wearing the mark of that association, he carries the spirit of that rejection into the most sacred moment of his day.</p><p>During bowing and prostrating, a loosely draped garment shifts unpredictably, and the private area (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عورة</span>) — the part of the body that must remain covered for prayer to be valid — can be exposed. Even a single thick shirt is permitted in prayer, but only if the worshipper belts it securely; every movement of prayer must leave <strong>nothing exposed</strong>. The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">متوشح</span> style cannot guarantee that, and so it fails the prayer''s most basic condition.</p><p>Prayer is an audience before Allah. Every detail of how we present ourselves — what we wear, how we wear it, what associations we carry — matters because the One we are standing before is not indifferent to such things. The people of Lot are remembered precisely for their indifference to the limits Allah set; to dress like them in prayer is to <strong>echo that indifference</strong> at the worst possible moment.</p><p>Maulana Ali''s counsel closes the circle: let none of you stand before Allah, the Mighty and Exalted, except in a <strong>clean garment</strong>. Clean encompasses more than freedom from physical impurity; it means the garment carries no associations that diminish the holiness of the moment.</p>', '["متوشح (draped loosely across the body): from the root و-ش-ح, which describes crossing something diagonally over a surface — like a sash worn across the chest. The derived verb تَوَشَّحَ means to drape a garment over oneself in this crossing manner without fastening it. The root captures the instability of the style: placed across, not secured.", "عورة (private area, that which must be covered): from the root ع-و-ر, which conveys a defect, a gap, or an exposed vulnerability — seen also in أَعْوَر, meaning one-eyed or impaired in one eye. The عورة is literally the ''vulnerable point'': that which, left uncovered, opens a gap in the prayer''s legal and spiritual validity."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'friday and the two festivals', 'student:089f7a7ddb381b69', 1, 'Do not count the prayer behind a الناصب or a الحروري as valid', 'Do not count the prayer behind a الناصب or a الحروري as valid', '<p>Two very different kinds of wrong leadership appear in the passage, and the line between them is doctrinal, not merely moral. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الناصب</span> is someone who has actively planted <strong>enmity against the People of the House (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أهل البيت</span>)</strong> — the Prophet''s family and the chain of Imams. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحروري</span> takes its name from Harura (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرُورَاء</span>), near Kufa, where <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الخوارج</span> first broke from Maulana Ali, declaring him a disbeliever and seeking his death. Both have cut themselves off, at the root, from the Imam''s spiritual authority.</p><p>This is why the instruction is almost architectural: <strong>treat him as one of the pillars of the mosque</strong> — not a leader, not a conduit, just stone. You recite for yourself as if alone, because spiritually you are alone. A person who has turned against the Imams carries no light to pass to those praying behind them.</p><p>The &quot;unjust&quot; imam discussed immediately afterward is something else entirely. He is a summoner — a <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داعي</span> — within the true hierarchy whose conduct has failed but who has never renounced the Imam or declared war on the People of the House (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أهل البيت</span>). His fault is <strong>conduct, not creed</strong> — and prayer behind him is obligatory.</p><p>Dissimulation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تقية</span>) is the principle that holds this together, and what lifts it above a legal convenience is that <strong>the Imams named it as their religion</strong>:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التقية ديني ودين آبائي</span> Dissimulation is my religion and the religion of my fathers.</p><p>Even Imam al-Husayn ibn Ali allowed Muawiya''s governor to lead prayer over al-Hasan, saying only that the practice compelled it. The community is not passive, though — when injustice appears in a summoner, they must bring it before the holder of authority and await judgment.</p><p>The contrast is this:</p><ul><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الناصب</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحروري</span> have broken with <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإمامة</span> at the creedal level — prayer behind them is spiritually null.</p></li><li><p>The unjust summoner has bent in conduct but remains within the living hierarchy — prayer is valid; the wrong must be reported.</p></li></ul><p>A spiritual leader is meant to be a window through whom the Imam''s light reaches those who pray. When someone has <strong>closed that window from the inside</strong> by making the Imams their enemies, they become exactly what the instruction calls them: a pillar — part of the building, and nothing more.</p>', '["ناصب (enemy of أهل البيت): from the root ن-ص-ب, meaning to erect, set up, or plant firmly. The active participle نَاصِب describes one who has \"erected\" a thing — here, hostility toward the Imams as a fixed, deliberate stance. Think of a stake driven into the ground: this figure has planted enmity and left it standing.", "الحروري (Kharijite): named after Harura (حَرُورَاء), a town near Kufa where الخوارج first broke from Maulana Ali after the Battle of Siffin. The place name connects to حَرّ, meaning intense heat — fitting for a movement ablaze with its own certainty. They declared Maulana Ali a disbeliever and sought his death.", "تقية (dissimulation): from the root و-ق-ي, meaning to guard or shield. The same root gives وِقَايَة (protection) and the verb اتَّقَى (to guard oneself, to exercise deep reverence for Allah). The term describes guarding one''s inner faith under pressure — letting the outer conform while the heart remains aligned with the Imam.", "جائر (unjust): from the root ج-و-ر, meaning to deviate or veer off course. The noun جَوْر is injustice understood as a swerving from uprightness. A جائر is one whose conduct has bent off-line — though in this passage he remains within the community''s living hierarchy and has not broken with the Imams."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'friday and the two festivals', 'student:11db34eb95c25bd4', 2, 'that preacher is the property of the man who entered through the door…', 'that preacher is the property of the man who entered through the door of the congregational mosque, for that man had appointed him as his deputy', '<p>The answer rests on a principle Islamic jurisprudence has always held: <strong>representation ends the instant the represented party arrives</strong>. A deputy (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نائب</span>) holds his office because his principal is absent; the moment the principal appears, the gap simply closes.</p><p>Think of a manager left to run a company while the owner is abroad — <strong>the authority is lent, and lending ends on return</strong>. The moment the owner walks through the door, the manager cannot chair a session ''on behalf of'' someone sitting right there in the room.</p><p>The preacher was in exactly this position. At some earlier point, the man who entered had granted him permission (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إذن</span>) to occupy that pulpit. The summons (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span>) operates this way at every level:</p><ol><li><p>The Imam authorizes his proof (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجة</span>).</p></li><li><p>The proof authorizes the summoner (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داعي</span>).</p></li><li><p>The summoner licenses the authorized one (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مأذون</span>) below him.</p></li></ol><p>Once the authorizing rank is physically present, the lower rank cannot act ''on its behalf.'' Each holder of office preaches only because the rank above breathed authority into him; <strong>no rank in the chain is self-appointing</strong>.</p><p>The man who entered had once placed the preacher in that pulpit, in effect saying: ''lead in my name.'' That single act made him the preacher''s master — not on the day he walked through the door, but from <strong>the very first sermon</strong>.</p>', '["نائب (deputy): from root ن-و-ب, meaning to rotate, alternate, or take turns. A نائب is one who ''comes in another''s turn'' — standing where the principal would stand, in the principal''s absence. The root quietly marks deputyship as inherently temporary: when the original holder returns, the نائب''s turn is simply over.", "إذن (permission): from root أ-ذ-ن, whose core sense is the ear (أُذُن). To grant إذن is to ''lend your ear'' — to hear and approve. Permission, in this root, is not merely a formal clearance; it is an act of attentive reception, a bending of the higher rank toward the one below before authorizing them to act.", "دعوة (the summons): from root د-ع-و, meaning to call or invite. The root yields دَعَا (he called), دُعَاء (supplication — calling upon Allah), and دَاعِي (the one who calls). In the Ismaili context, the summons is a living chain of calling: each rank calls the one below toward the Imam, as the Imam calls toward Allah.", "حجة (proof): from root ح-ج-ج, which also gives حَجَّ (to make pilgrimage — to move toward a sacred destination). The حجة demonstrates the Imam''s truth by their very presence, as a destination proves a journey was real. The root links argument with arrival: proof is not merely logical; it is a presence you travel toward."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'friday and the two festivals', 'student:cd3a97f93b17466e', 3, 'it is a likeness of what obtains in the summons of the five speakers…', 'it is a likeness of what obtains in the summons of the five speakers — the possessors of firm resolve — who correspond to the five prayers, drawn from the mention of the ten spiritual ranks and the ten bodily ranks, and the mention of the speaker of the cycle, his executor, and the Imam of the age', '<p>The twenty-three magnifications (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تكبير</span>) rest on a straightforward sum: ten spiritual ranks, ten bodily ranks, and three specific persons — the speaker of the cycle (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ناطق</span>), his executor or foundation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أساس</span>), and the Imam of the age. That is 10 + 10 + 3 = <strong>twenty-three</strong>.</p><p>The ten spiritual ranks refer to the hierarchy of the unseen, luminous world — the chain of spiritual intelligences that Ismaili cosmology describes as the structure of reality above the material plane. The ten bodily ranks are their living mirror: the chain of summoners who carry the call (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span>) outward from the Imam through every level of society to reach the seeker. Together these twenty ranks <strong>map the full architecture of creation</strong>, from its highest, most luminous origin down to the most earthly expression.</p><p>The three named figures — the speaker (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ناطق</span>), the executor (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أساس</span>), and the Imam of the age — are not abstract stations but living persons: the one who delivers the outward law, the one who carries its inner meaning, and <strong>the Imam</strong> as the present axis around whom both orbit. In every prophetic cycle, these three have been the irreducible poles of divine authority.</p><p>The five great prophets — the possessors of firm resolve (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أولو العزم</span>) — and the five daily prayers they correspond to do not add to the count. They <strong>supply the frame</strong>: each daily prayer is a re-enactment of one prophetic cycle, and all five together rehearse the complete arc of divine summons across history. The magnifications of the festival days are therefore reckoned against this full picture — not one cycle alone, but the sweep of all five speakers and the twenty-three ranks and persons their summons contains.</p><p>When we raise our hands in magnification during those blessed days, each repetition is not empty ceremony — it is an acknowledgment, count by count, of the entire living structure through which <strong>the mercy of Allah reaches us</strong>.</p>', '["تكبير: from the root ك-ب-ر, meaning to be great or vast in measure and standing. The form تكبير names the act of declaring that greatness — the utterance اللهُ أَكْبَر. The root''s core sense of immeasurable magnitude flows directly into the gesture: every magnification is a proclamation that Allah''s greatness exceeds all comparison.", "عزم: the heart of أولو العزم; from ع-ز-م, meaning to resolve with unflinching firmness and to commit without retreat. عزم is the quality of a decision that does not waver once made. أولو العزم — those who hold this quality — are the five great prophets distinguished not only by their mission but by the steel of their inner resolve.", "ناطق: from ن-ط-ق, meaning to produce distinct, articulate sound — not mere noise but intelligible, clear speech. The Ismaili ناطق is the prophet who articulates divine will into a law human beings can hear and follow. The root captures both the physical act of clear enunciation and the higher act of making the hidden knowable through spoken form.", "حدود: from ح-د-د, meaning to mark a sharp boundary or limit. A حدّ is a defined edge between two things. In the summons hierarchy, the حدود are precise stations within the chain of authority — defined boundaries so that each summoner occupies exactly the place appointed, neither overreaching above nor falling short below."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'forgetting, doubting, and beginning again', 'student:df1e368b3d54e38e', 1, 'the Imam delivers the salaam of the dhikr yet he did not intend the p…', 'the Imam delivers the salaam of the dhikr yet he did not intend the prayer', '<p>Within every prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلاة</span>) is a moment of intimate recitation — the seated testimony (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التشهد</span>) performed in the final position of the prayer. Nestled inside it is a <strong>salaam directed toward the Prophet and the congregation</strong>: &quot;Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah.&quot; This is the salaam of the remembrance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سلام الذكر</span>) — a salaam spoken from inside the sanctuary of the prayer, not one that opens its door.</p><p>The closing salaam (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التسليم</span>), by contrast, is the act that <strong>ends the prayer entirely</strong>. When a worshipper turns and says <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السلام عليكم</span>, they step out of the state of ritual consecration and back into ordinary life. Think of the prayer as a sealed room of communion: the salaam of the remembrance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سلام الذكر</span>) is a word of peace uttered inside that room, while <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التسليم</span> is the act of opening the door and leaving.</p><p>This distinction carries real legal force in the passage we are reading. A follower has declared his intent to follow the Imam, but by the time the Imam reaches the salaam of the remembrance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سلام الذكر</span>), the follower has not yet truly formed his intention (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النية</span>) in his heart. The answer turns on whether the <strong>Imam carries the intention on the follower''s behalf</strong>: if the Imam does, the prayer stands; if not, it lapses. The Imam is not merely a leader in posture — he is a spiritual custodian whose sincerity can shelter the whole congregation.</p><p>Because the salaam of the remembrance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سلام الذكر</span>) does not seal the prayer, <strong>a window still exists</strong>. The follower can still align his heart before <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التسليم</span> arrives and shuts the gate. Maulana Ali taught that doubt does not erase certainty — and the law does not rush to invalidate a prayer that still has life in it.</p>', '["الذكر (remembrance): from the root ذ-ك-ر, meaning to remember and to mention aloud — a union the tradition treats as inseparable. Memory here is never silent; to truly remember something is to voice it. The noun الذكر names any act that keeps something alive through both heart and tongue working together.", "السلام (peace): from the root س-ل-م, whose core sense is wholeness, safety, and freedom from harm. The verb سَلِمَ means to be intact and undamaged. When the worshipper says السلام, they offer not merely a greeting but a declaration — an extension of the state of being whole to another.", "التسليم (the closing salaam): also from س-ل-م, but cast in the intensive Form II pattern, giving it a causative force: to bestow peace, to hand it outward. التسليم is the act of releasing peace across the threshold — the worshipper gives peace away and, in that giving, formally steps outside the prayer.", "التشهد (the testimony): from the root ش-ه-د, to witness and to be present. الشاهد is the witness; الشهادة is testimony. التشهد is the act of bearing witness — placing oneself before truth and declaring it aloud — making the seated recitation a living moment of witnessing the divine."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'forgetting, doubting, and beginning again', 'student:1ac66b4482ba7779', 2, 'a surah taken from above, which is not what is required in the obliga…', 'a surah taken from above, which is not what is required in the obligatory prayer', '<p>When the text speaks of &quot;a surah taken from above,&quot; it is describing the <strong>Quran''s own sequence</strong> as it runs from beginning to end. The surahs at the start — Al-Fatiha, Al-Baqarah, and those that follow — sit &quot;above,&quot; while the shorter surahs near the end, like Al-Ikhlas and An-Nas, sit &quot;below.&quot; So if a worshipper recites Al-Ikhlas in the first unit (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ركعة</span>) of prayer and then, in the second, reaches back to Al-Kawthar — which appears earlier in the Quran — they have taken a surah &quot;from above.&quot;</p><p>The practice expected here is called <strong>ordering</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ترتيب</span>): the surah of the second rakah should follow, not precede, the surah of the first. Think of it as reading a book — we would not flip back to an earlier chapter after having already moved past it.</p><p>The prostration of omission is not stipulated for this mistake — the prayer stands. But the text adds: &quot;he should not do it deliberately,&quot; marking the act as <strong>disliked</strong>, something that falls short of the beauty we owe to prayer without breaking it.</p><p>It belongs to the etiquette (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أدب</span>) of worship, not to the <strong>pillars</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أركان</span>). This same spirit runs through the earlier ruling, where reciting prayer &quot;in reverse order, from bottom to top&quot; also carries no obligation for the prostration of omission. Together, these rulings affirm that the Quran''s order shapes the dignity of our prayer, yet Allah, in His mercy, does not hold the inattentive worshipper to the same account as one who misses a pillar.</p>', '["ترتيب (ordering): from the root ر-ت-ب, meaning to place things in fixed ranks or rows. The noun رَتْبَة is a rank or grade. ترتيب is the act of establishing that sequence — connecting directly to prayer, where the surahs recited across rakahs are expected to follow the Quran''s own ranked order from beginning to end.", "ركعة (unit of prayer): from the root ر-ك-ع, meaning to bow or incline the body forward. A ركعة is one complete unit of prayer, named after its most distinctive physical act — the bow of humility before Allah. The root locks the name to the posture that gives the unit its identity.", "أدب (etiquette): from the root أ-د-ب, whose earliest sense was to invite others to a feast or gathering. Over time it came to mean cultivated and refined conduct. In religious law, أدب names acts that are beautiful and proper in worship — honourable and recommended, though not obligatory pillars.", "أركان (pillars): from the root ر-ك-ن, meaning a corner or load-bearing support. The singular رُكْن is a cornerstone. In prayer, الأركان are the foundational acts — bowing and prostration among them — that bear the structure of the prayer upright. Remove them, and the edifice falls."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'forgetting, doubting, and beginning again', 'student:a72154c8f73aa273', 3, 'he delivers the salaam, prays two rakahs seated, and performs the two…', 'he delivers the salaam, prays two rakahs seated, and performs the two prostrations of omission', '<p>The two cases pivot on a single difference: <strong>how many rakahs are genuinely uncertain.</strong> When we doubt between three and four, only one rakah hangs in the balance; when we doubt between two and four, two full rakahs are unknown.</p><p>A seated rakah carries a lighter legal weight than a standing one — sufficient to repair a gap of one, yet too light to corrupt a prayer that needed no repair at all. If the prayer was already complete at four, the seated rakahs become voluntary prayer appended at the end; if it was truly at three, they provide the <strong>cautious completion</strong> the prayer needs.</p><p>When the uncertain gap is two full rakahs, seated rakahs would not suffice — the obligatory prayer requires <strong>full standing rakahs</strong> to restore its structure. If the prayer was already at four, those two standing rakahs become voluntary; if only two were prayed, they complete the prayer properly.</p><p>Two rules hold across both cases:</p><ul><li><p>The remedial rakahs are performed after the salaam, so the obligatory prayer is formally closed before they begin — any error in the remedy cannot reach back and corrupt it.</p></li><li><p>The prostrations of omission (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سجدتا السهو</span>) follow in both cases, formally sealing the uncertainty.</p></li></ul><p>This flows from Maulana Ali''s teaching — that doubt neither dispels certainty nor diminishes it; we <strong>act on what we know</strong> and supply what remains in a form precisely sized to protect the prayer''s obligatory core.</p>', '["سهو (omission): from the root س-ه-و, meaning to slip inadvertently in awareness or to drift from attention. The root conveys a quiet, unintentional wandering of the mind — not wilful neglect but a moment when focus simply was not there. سهو names this unguarded lapse, and the derived word stays faithful to that image of attention quietly slipping away.", "شك (doubt): from the root ش-ك-ك, meaning to pierce something through, dividing it. When two possibilities simultaneously run through the mind and split it — neither resolved, neither dispelled — that divided state is شك. The image is of something cut cleanly in two: certainty has been severed, and the mind sits suspended between the two halves."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'forgetting, doubting, and beginning again', 'student:f702bc0d7bc0d494', 4, 'Three things interrupt the prayer: a woman, a donkey, and a black dog', 'Three things interrupt the prayer: a woman, a donkey, and a black dog', '<p>The chapter presents both reports back to back; the discomfort a reader feels is intentional — that is where the teaching lives. The first says that a woman, a donkey, and a black dog are among those things that interrupt the prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلاة</span>). The second is the Prophet''s own lived response.</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثَلَاثٌ تَقْطَعُ الصَّلَاةَ الْمَرْأَةُ وَالْحِمَارُ وَالْكَلْبُ الْأَسْوَدُ</span> Three things interrupt the prayer: a woman, a donkey, and a black dog.</p><p>All three passed before the Prophet while he was praying. He did not stop or begin again; he completed his worship, and when it was done, he turned to those watching and said:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَأَيْتُ الَّذِي رَأَيْتُمْ وَلَيْسَ يَقْطَعُ صَلَاةَ الْمُؤْمِنِ شَيْءٌ وَلَكِنِ ادْرَءُوا مَا اسْتَطَعْتُمْ</span> I saw what you saw, and nothing interrupts the prayer of a believer; but ward off what you are able to ward off.</p><p>The Prophet himself is the reconciliation — his action and word establish that <strong>the prayer of a believer is legally unbroken</strong>. What the first report describes is not a legal verdict but a spiritual warning — these are forces powerful enough to shatter a worshipper''s inner presence, to pull the heart away from Allah at the very moment it should be most still.</p><p>Think of a candle flame caught in a sudden gust of wind. The flame trembles, flickers, nearly disappears — but it does not go out. These three things are that gust.</p><p>The phrase &quot;ward off what you are able&quot; is the bridge between the two reports. It is a counsel of care, not a legal command. Protect the sacred space of worship with every means available — not because the prayer will otherwise be void, but because what is at stake is its <strong>depth and sincerity before Allah</strong>.</p><p>Maulana Ali brings the same reasoning to another situation:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا يَقْطَعُ الصَّلَاةَ التَّبَسُّمُ وَيَقْطَعُهَا الْقَهْقَهَةُ</span> Smiling does not interrupt the prayer, but loud laughter interrupts it.</p><p>The tradition draws a clear and repeated line between disturbance and legal disruption. A passing shadow disturbs the heart; a burst of uncontrolled laughter breaks the act itself.</p>', '["تقطع (to interrupt): from the root ق-ط-ع, meaning to cut or sever cleanly — like a blade through rope. The image is sharp and final. Derivatives include قطعة (a piece cut off) and قاطع (decisive, cutting). In the prayer context, it names an act severed so completely that it cannot simply continue where it left off.", "التبسم (smiling): from the root ب-س-م, meaning to smile gently. The root suggests a slight, soft opening — a parting without force. This mildness is built into the word itself, which is why the tradition sets it so clearly apart from القهقهة. What ب-س-م names is controlled, inward, and still compatible with the state of worship.", "القهقهة (loud laughter): built on the quadrilateral root ق-ه-ق-ه, a reduplicated form that mimics the sound of the act — a burst repeated and echoed in the throat. Arabic roots that double their elements often signal intensity or uncontrolled repetition. The very structure of this word captures why it breaks prayer: it is sound that overflows and cannot be contained."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'children, travelers, and the sick', 'student:8da418bc30073fb0', 1, 'If he has land of the extent that would ordinarily require the shorte…', 'If he has land of the extent that would ordinarily require the shortening of the prayer, and he wishes to walk in it for pleasure, he does not shorten the prayer', '<p>The passage draws a legal distinction. A journey earns the concession of <strong>shortened prayer</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قصر</span>) not merely because of the distance covered, but because of what that distance means for the person covering it. The ruling asks: are you truly displaced, or are you still, in some essential sense, at home?</p><p>The <strong>dispensation</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رخصة</span>) was designed to ease the hardship of a man setting out across land he does not own — heading toward an unfamiliar horizon, cut off from his routines and resources. When a man walks across his own estate, however vast, he remains in his own domain.</p><p>Purpose alone cannot serve as the legal criterion because <strong>purpose is too easy to fake</strong>. A landowner could claim any walk across his fields is a &quot;journey&quot; if declared intent were enough to trigger the concession. Ownership, by contrast, is a concrete and verifiable legal fact — it anchors a person to a place in a way that words cannot.</p><p>Think of two people walking the same path across the same open countryside. One owns the land; the other is a stranger to it. The stranger is a traveler (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مسافر</span>); the landowner is simply moving through his own domain. The distance is identical — the <strong>legal condition is entirely different</strong>.</p>', '["سفر (journey): from the root س-ف-ر, meaning to uncover or strip away. Pre-dawn light was called سفر because it strips darkness from the sky. A journey (سفر) carries that same sense — it strips a person from the covering of home, exposing them to unfamiliar ground. The traveler (مسافر) is, at root, the one who has been uncovered.", "قصر (shortening of prayer): from the root ق-ص-ر, meaning to shorten, confine, or hold back. The same root gives قصور (falling short) and قصر (a walled enclosure). When prayer is shortened (قصر), it is drawn inward — a mercy that holds the full obligation within tighter bounds for one genuinely displaced from home.", "رخصة (dispensation): from the root ر-خ-ص, meaning to be easy, light, or low in cost. رخيص means inexpensive. A رخصة in law is a lowering of the burden — Allah making an obligation lighter for those in genuine hardship. It is not a loophole; it is a mercy, and it only functions when the hardship it relieves is real."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'children, travelers, and the sick', 'student:b24632da8269e44d', 2, 'This is particular to the people of the high ranks, and does not exte…', 'This is particular to the people of the high ranks, and does not extend to others among all the hudud', '<p>The passage raises a precise question about authority within the dawa hierarchy, and the text itself does not enumerate specific grade-titles. Muhammad ibn Fahd states simply that the two dispensations — a traveling imam giving salutation after two rakahs before residents have finished, and one who has performed dry ablution (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تيمم</span>) leading those who have performed full ablution — are &quot;particular to the people of the high ranks, and do not extend to others among all the hudud (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حدود</span>).&quot; The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حدود</span> here means the ordained limits or ranks of the sacred mission, covering the entire hierarchy from the most senior caller down to the newest initiate.</p><p>The only concrete example offered is the Dai of the age himself — Jafar ibn Sayyidina Sulayman — performing tayammum and then leading the congregation, afterward explaining to his most senior hudud that this dispensation exists &quot;when some weighty matter makes it necessary for the good of the guiding dawa and the raising of its standard.&quot; That phrase is the real key: the dispensation belongs not to a fixed list of grade-titles but to whoever bears <strong>responsibility for the welfare of the dawa as a whole</strong> — a condition that, by its nature, applies at the summit of the hierarchy.</p><p>The Tayyibi dawa recognises a layered structure of ranks:</p><ul><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإمام</span> — the Imam, the living proof and axis of the mission</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحجة</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الباب</span> — the highest deputies</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الداعي المطلق</span> — the Absolute Caller, caretaker of the dawa during the concealment</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المأذون</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المكاسر</span> — the licensed preacher and the one who breaks resistance</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المستجيب</span> — the initiate who has responded to the summons</p></li></ul><p>The passage aligns the dispensation with a dai of the highest standing, then says it does not reach the remainder of the ranks. Whether a Hujja or Ma''dhun of exceptional excellence might also qualify, the text leaves open. What it closes firmly is the possibility that an ordinary rank-holder could claim the dispensation simply by invoking it. The governing condition is the weighty necessity of the dawa, judged by those whose station places them in charge of it.</p>', '["حدود (limits, ranks): from the root ح-د-د, meaning to sharpen, to mark a boundary, or to define an edge. A حد is the point where one thing ends and another begins. In the dawa, the حدود are the ordained boundaries of each rank — each person stands at a defined limit within the hierarchy, neither above it nor below it.", "داعي (caller): from the root د-ع-و, meaning to call out, to invite, to summon. A داعي is literally one who calls — he reaches into the world and draws souls toward the inner truth of the faith. The same root gives us دعوة, the sacred mission or summons itself.", "درجات (ranks, grades): from the root د-ر-ج, meaning to climb step by step or to ascend gradually. A درجة is a single step or rung. The plural درجات captures the ladder-like structure of the dawa, where each rank is one step higher in knowledge, responsibility, and trust.", "مأذون (licensed preacher): from the root أ-ذ-ن, meaning to give ear to someone and then to give permission. The مأذون is one who has been listened to, approved, and granted leave to act — his authority is explicitly conferred from above, not self-assumed."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'children, travelers, and the sick', 'student:c8131d6d421d9639', 3, 'warriors who cut the road', 'warriors who cut the road', '<p><em>Researched outside this library — the knowledge base carries nothing on this passage. Sources are listed at the end.</em></p><p>The phrase ''warriors who cut the road'' is a literal translation for the term used to describe <strong>brigands or highway robbers</strong>. This group is included with merchants, tax collectors, and others who do not shorten their prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلاة</span>) while traveling because the nature of their journey is the key, not the morality of their purpose. The concession to shorten prayers is a mercy for someone undertaking a temporary journey away from their settled home, with a clear plan to return.</p><p>Imagine the difference between a family taking a vacation and a long-haul truck driver. The vacationing family is on a temporary trip and would shorten their prayers. The truck driver, however, is at their workplace; being on the road <em>is</em> their normal state. The six categories of people listed, including the highway robber, all share this quality: their travel is their profession or continuous activity. They are not considered travelers in the specific legal sense that permits the concession, so they pray in full, just as one would at home.</p><p>This ruling teaches us that the special permissions Allah grants for travel are tied to a specific kind of journey. It is for the person who has temporarily left their normal life behind. For those whose normal life <em>is</em> to be on the move, their situation is different, and the rule that applies is that of a resident.</p><p>***</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قاطع</span> (one who cuts): From the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ق</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ط</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>, which means to cut, to sever, or to traverse. The active participle <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قاطع</span> describes someone who performs the action of cutting. When paired with <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الطريق</span> (the road), the phrase <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قاطع الطريق</span> vividly depicts one who intercepts travelers, cutting off their path to rob them, or one who makes a route unsafe, severing a connection between places.</p><p><strong>Sources consulted</strong></p><ul><li><p>almerja.com</p></li><li><p>awkafonline.gov.eg</p></li><li><p>juancole.com</p></li><li><p>shia-online.com</p></li></ul>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'eclipse, fear, rain, and the night vigil', 'student:c121efefd432d698', 1, 'A statement has come from Mawlana, the author of the letters', 'A statement has come from Mawlana, the author of the letters', '<p>The questioner has identified a genuine puzzle. The epistles (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رسائل</span>) of the Brethren of Purity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إخوان الصفا</span>) state that a solar eclipse happens only at the <strong>end of the lunar month</strong> — the moon must reach conjunction with the sun near the month''s close before a new crescent can rise. Yet eclipses were also observed at the first days of Ramadan, which seemed to violate this rule entirely.</p><p>The Imam called &quot;the author of the letters&quot; is the same Imam — or the Imam under whose authority — those epistles (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رسائل</span>) were produced. In Ismaili understanding, these letters were composed within and guided by the mission (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span>), so the Imam and the letters form <strong>a single source of knowledge</strong>, encountered at different moments rather than two competing voices.</p><p>Appealing to the Imam therefore resolves rather than restates the problem. A written text is sealed at the moment of its writing. The Imam''s living word can do something no fixed text can: <strong>expand and complete it</strong>. The epistles (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رسائل</span>) gave one half of the astronomical picture — the end of the month. The Imam''s transmitted clarification adds the other half: a solar eclipse may also fall at the beginning of the month. This is not a contradiction of the letters; it is the fuller truth the letters had left unspoken.</p><p>There is a quiet astronomical point beneath this too. In a visibility-based lunar calendar, &quot;the end&quot; of one month and &quot;the beginning&quot; of the next describe the <strong>same astronomical moment</strong> — the conjunction of sun and moon. Whether an observer reckons the new moon as closing Sha''ban or opening Ramadan depends on local sighting conditions and method of reckoning. The letters named one face of this reality; the Imam named both.</p><p>The passage closes with: &quot;The matter is in the hand of Allah.&quot; That phrase is not a retreat into vagueness — it is the acknowledgement that <strong>no fixed calculation can stand above living guidance</strong>. The heavens move; so does the Imam''s word.</p>', '["رسائل (epistles): from the root ر-س-ل, meaning to send or dispatch. The verb أَرْسَلَ means to send a messenger, and رَسُول is the messenger himself. A single رِسَالَة is literally a \"sending\" — a mind dispatched across distance. The plural رَسَائِل names multiple such dispatches, each carrying the sender''s thought to a distant reader.", "إخوان (brethren): the singular is أَخ, from the root أ-خ-و, which expresses the closeness of those who cleave to one another. Naming the letter-writers إخوان places their community not among mere colleagues but among people bound by deep, chosen kinship — a brotherhood of souls, not simply of shared opinion.", "صَفَاء (purity): from the root ص-ف-و, meaning to be clear, limpid, and free of sediment — as still water that settles into transparency. الصَّفَاء is that quality of settled clarity. The title إخوان الصفا names its members as brothers of clear-heartedness: people whose inner selves have been stilled and made transparent to the light.", "تأويل (inner interpretation): from the root أ-و-ل, meaning to return a thing to its origin. أَوَّلَ is to trace back to a first principle. تأويل is the act of returning a word or event to its true, originary meaning — peeling back the surface to find what first gave it form. This is the Imam''s living capacity, demonstrated precisely here.", "دعوة (mission): from the root د-ع-و, meaning to call or summon. دَعَا is to call out, and الدَّاعِي is the one who calls. The دعوة is the Imam''s living summons sent through trained emissaries into the world, carrying his knowledge and authority to all who hear and respond."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'eclipse, fear, rain, and the night vigil', 'student:d078dfdd51875336', 2, 'it is permissible to pray it before dawn, on account of the noble mea…', 'it is permissible to pray it before dawn, on account of the noble meaning beneath this that permits it', '<p>The passage raises a question it leaves deliberately unanswered. It cites &quot;the books of the friends of Allah (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِمُ السَّلَامُ</span>)&quot; without naming any of them, then closes with the phrase &quot;so know this&quot; — a classic Ismaili literary signal that the deeper truth must be sought through the living chain of guidance, not extracted from a page alone.</p><p>In Ismaili thought, <strong>every outward act carries an inward reality</strong> — the exoteric (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظاهر</span>) and the esoteric (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>) are always paired. The sunnah of dawn sits at a uniquely charged moment: Mawlana Ali himself said that after these two bowings, &quot;the things most desired are granted.&quot; The permission to pray them before physical dawn has spread follows a spiritual logic — the soul''s inner orientation toward divine light is not bound by any horizon.</p><p>The literature of interpretation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيل</span>) produced by the Imams and their representatives is the body of work the text gestures toward without naming. Works like <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيلُ الدَّعَائِمِ</span> — cited elsewhere in the same chapter — belong to this family of texts that unpack the hidden realities beneath outward worship. The path (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سبيل</span>) into those realities runs from the living Imam downward to those ready to receive it — and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سبيل</span> itself, at its root, names something let loose to hang freely and carry what it was sent to carry.</p><p><strong>Spiritual dawn precedes physical dawn.</strong> The light of the Imam''s guidance is not tied to any astronomical reckoning; when the soul has already turned toward that light, the outward condition of spreading dawn becomes secondary.</p>', '["سَبِيل (path): root س-ب-ل. Lane''s Lexicon: سَبَل is something let loose to hang and flow freely toward the ground. A سَبِيل — road, channel, way — is what pours downward from its source to those who travel it. The title سَبِيلُ الْهُدَى captures this exactly: guidance is something that flows clearly down to the one seeking it.", "فَجْر (dawn): root ف-ج-ر, to burst or split open with force. Dawn is the moment darkness is violently ruptured by light — not a gentle fading but a sudden breaking. The spiritual intensity of the sunnah of فَجْر belongs to this image: the night does not yield; it is split open.", "بَاطِن (the inward): root ب-ط-ن, the belly or interior of something — what it holds within, unseen from outside. In Ismaili hermeneutics, every exoteric form (ظاهر) has a بَاطِن that is its living core, the purpose the outward act was made to carry.", "مَعْنَى (meaning): root ع-ن-ي, to intend or to be occupied with something. The مَعْنَى of an act is what was always intended beneath its outer form — the inner aim the outward ruling exists to protect. The noble meaning (المعنى الشريف) is precisely that aimed-at truth concealed beneath the apparent ruling.", "تَأْوِيل (interpretation): root أ-و-ل. تَأْوِيل is the act of returning something to its أَوَّل — its first and original meaning. Just as a traveler returns to the source, تَأْوِيل takes the outward text back to its deepest intent. In Ismaili thought, it is the living science entrusted to the Imam."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'eclipse, fear, rain, and the night vigil', 'student:58505fa49262ce0e', 3, 'when I asked him about it, and whether that supplication may be recit…', 'when I asked him about it, and whether that supplication may be recited in a month other than Rajab, he said', '<p>The passage presents a layered attribution. Mawlana Jafar first cites the historical Imam al-Sadiq (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) as the source of the original ruling — that the prayer of Umm Daud was prescribed only for the month of Rajab, and that he commanded her to purify herself.</p><p>Then the register suddenly shifts: &quot;And when I asked him about it, and whether that supplication may be recited in a month other than Rajab, he said...&quot; A living, responsive voice answers with a new ruling about the day of ʿArafah and Fridays. <strong>The grammar points unmistakably to a contemporary, not a historical figure.</strong> Mawlana Jafar is writing from Yemen in or around the eleventh century of the Islamic era — centuries after the historical Imam al-Sadiq left this world.</p><p>The &quot;him&quot; must therefore be a living authority in Mawlana Jafar''s own era. In the Tayyibi tradition, where the Imam al-Tayyib is in concealment (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غيبة</span>), the absolute representative (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الداعي المطلق</span>) holds delegated Imamic authority, and his words carry the standing of a binding text (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نص</span>). A second possibility that Ismaili spiritual literature allows: Mawlana Jafar, as a senior figure in the mission (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دعوة</span>), is describing a consultation that ran upward through the living chain of the hierarchy to the hidden Imam al-Tayyib himself — not met in the flesh, but reached through the vertical connection that binds every rank to the one above.</p><p><strong>Either way, the ruling does not stop with history.</strong> It flows through a living pole in every age, and the &quot;him&quot; of this passage is that pole.</p>', '["نص (authoritative text): from the root ن-ص-ص, meaning to raise something up so it stands visible and plain. A نص is a ruling ''stood upright'' where everyone can see it — explicit, unambiguous, not buried in inference. This is why the passage insists a practice needs a نص before it is valid: it must stand up openly, not merely be assumed.", "بدعة (innovation): from the root ب-د-ع, meaning to bring something into existence that had no prior model. The same root gives بديع, meaning wondrous or unprecedented. A بدعة in religious practice is an act ''originated'' without grounding in the transmitted tradition — however sincere the intention, it lacks the standing of what was handed down.", "دعوة (mission, call): from the root د-ع-و, meaning to call out, to summon, to invite. The root gives both دعوة (a summons, a mission) and داعي (the caller, the summoner). In Ismaili usage, the دعوة is the living mission that calls souls toward the Imam, and the داعي is the one who carries that call forward in every age."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'eclipse, fear, rain, and the night vigil', 'student:e5d33e0e18e97c65', 4, 'If it is among the binding ones, he must prostrate', 'If it is among the binding ones, he must prostrate', '<p><em>Researched outside this library — the knowledge base carries nothing on this passage. Sources are listed at the end.</em></p><p>In the rich tapestry of Islamic practice, when reciting the Quran, there are certain verses that invite the reader or listener into a profound act of humility: the prostration of recitation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سجود التلاوة</span>). This physical act of bowing down is a beautiful, immediate response to the divine word. However, not all of these verses carry the same weight of obligation; some are considered &quot;binding&quot; or obligatory, while others are highly recommended.</p><p>The distinction between a binding and a non-binding prostration is based on the teachings and practices of the Prophet Muhammad and the Imams who followed him. Within the broader Islamic tradition, there is a consensus on certain verses being obligatory, while the status of others varies among different schools of thought. For Ismailis, the authority flows from the Prophet and, through him, to the Imams.</p><p>The verses that are considered binding, often referred to as the prostrations of determination (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عزائم السجود</span>), are four specific passages in the Quran. When one recites or hears these particular verses, performing a prostration becomes an immediate obligation. These four verses are:</p><ul><li><p>As-Sajdah 32:15</p></li><li><p>Fussilat 41:37-38</p></li><li><p>An-Najm 53:62</p></li><li><p>Al-Alaq 96:19</p></li></ul><p>These are set apart because they contain a direct command to prostrate or describe the prostration of believers in such a powerful way that it compels an immediate physical response of submission.</p><p>Beyond these four, there are other verses in the Quran, numbering around eleven, where prostration is highly recommended but not strictly obligatory. Reciting these verses also calls for a prostration as an act of devotion and humility, following the Sunnah, or practice, of the Prophet. The principle behind this distinction is the emphasis placed on these specific four verses by the Prophet and the Imams, marking them as moments of essential and undeniable submission to Allah.</p><p>***</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عزائم</span> (determination): From the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ز</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">م</span>, which means to determine, to resolve, or to be set upon something. The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عزائم</span> refers to things that are firmly decided or obligatory. In this context, it signifies that the prostration for these specific verses is a determined, binding command, not a matter of choice.</p><p><strong>Sources consulted</strong></p><ul><li><p>islamic-laws.com</p></li><li><p>islamiced.org</p></li><li><p>islamweb.net</p></li><li><p>wordpress.com</p></li><li><p>wikishia.net</p></li><li><p>almerja.com</p></li></ul>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the sickbed and the sound of mourning', 'student:774c13bd9992e969', 1, 'sleep in that posture, nor upon his face', 'sleep in that posture, nor upon his face', '<p>The English phrase &quot;that posture&quot; is actually translating <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">على الحجة</span> directly, and <strong>no sleeping position is named before it</strong> in the passage — the word &quot;that&quot; creates a false impression of a back-reference where none exists.</p><p>What <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحجة</span> means here emerges from <strong>the structure of the whole prohibition</strong>. Three positions are listed:</p><ul><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">على الحجة</span> — the one the English renders as &quot;that posture&quot;</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">على وجهه</span> — face-down, the prone position</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">على ظهرها</span> — flat on the back, for women specifically</p></li></ul><p>Because face-down and back-lying are each named separately, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">على الحجة</span> must refer to something distinct from both. The most defensible reading — consistent with the well-known teaching that the right side is the recommended sleep position — is that it means the <strong>left side</strong>. Sleeping on the left places the body''s full weight on the heart, which classical Islamic medicine considered harmful; the right side was preferred because it eases the heart and mirrors the position of one lying in the grave, facing the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قبلة</span>.</p><p>The prohibition does not end there. Maulana Ali gives the face-down position <strong>its own sharper emphasis</strong> — &quot;whomever you see sleeping on his face, wake him&quot; — making the prone posture the most urgent concern of the three. The passage draws a complete map of positions to avoid, leaving the right side as the one good posture implied by everything it forbids.</p>', '["الحجة: from root ح-ج-ج, meaning to repair purposefully toward a destination. This yields الحجّ (the pilgrimage — the intentional journey) and حجة (the compelling proof — the argument that drives decisively to its conclusion). In على الحجة as a sleeping posture, the most defensible reading is the left side, where the body''s weight presses directly upon the heart.", "العيادة: from root ع-و-د, meaning to return or come back. The same root gives عيد (the recurring festival — the day that returns) and عود (the return itself). To visit the sick (العيادة) is to return to your brother in his most vulnerable hour — the word carries within it the idea of faithful, repeated coming-back.", "البلاء: from root ب-ل-و, meaning to test something by subjecting it to use until its true quality is revealed. The root also yields بلي (to wear out, to decay under pressure). Affliction (البلاء) in this tradition is therefore a refining test — it does not simply punish; it wears away the outer layer and exposes what is genuinely within."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the sickbed and the sound of mourning', 'student:ba6b475ea10d368e', 2, 'calls down upon himself the very causes of affliction by which the so…', 'calls down upon himself the very causes of affliction by which the soul is punished', '<p>The chapter intends both readings, and that double weight is the whole point. The <strong>opening claim</strong> targets the living wailer: to engage in wailing (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النوح</span>) is to call down upon oneself the very conditions through which spiritual punishment moves. The story of ''Abdullah ibn Rawahah reveals exactly where those conditions also land. He sees an angel standing over him, the dying man himself, swinging a flaming iron mace toward his head with every shriek the women around him raise.</p><p>The Prophet''s closing words seal the second reading.</p><blockquote><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَ عَبْدُ اللَّهِ فَمَا بَالُ مَوْتَاكُمْ يُبْتَلَوْنَ بِقَوْلِ أَحْيَائِكُمْ</span> ''Abdullah has spoken the truth. What is the matter with your dead, that they should be made to suffer by the words of your living?</p></blockquote><p>The rebuke is aimed at the living, but <strong>the suffering falls on the dead</strong>. The soul crossing from this world to the next is stripped of the body''s ordinary protections at exactly the moment it is most exposed — and the wailing of the living reaches into that crossing. It is not the dead man''s own sin that brings the angel''s mace down; it is the living mourners'' sin, still moving along the cord that has not yet gone cold between them.</p><p>The harm to the wailer is equally exact: whoever strikes his thigh at a calamity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المصيبة</span>) <strong>forfeits his reward</strong> outright, and the two accursed voices draw Allah''s hatred onto the one who raises them. The wailer damages their own soul in the same moment they harm the one they have lost.</p><p>The living wailer and the departing soul are two ends of a cord the wailing tears at from both directions at once.</p>', '["النوح (wailing): from root ن-و-ح, meaning to moan with a sustained, drawn-out sound. The root suggests continuous public outpouring — not a single cry but a prolonged, rhythmic lament. النوح names the prohibited form: not quiet tears but performed, sustained grief that has crossed the line into display.", "العويل (lamentation): from root ع-و-ل, meaning to cry out in dependency and need. The same root gives عيال — household dependents, those who lean on another. العويل is the cry of someone who has lost their support. That etymology tells you why it so easily becomes desperate and uncontrolled: it is the sound of felt helplessness.", "البكاء (weeping): from root ب-ك-و, meaning to shed tears — quietly, organically. The root carries the sense of tears simply flowing, not shaped into performance. This is the grief the Prophet permitted and modeled himself: natural, involuntary, tender. البكاء and النوح look alike from the outside but are spiritually worlds apart.", "المصيبة (calamity): from root ص-و-ب, meaning to strike a target accurately. A مصيبة is literally ''that which hits its mark'' — calamity conceived not as random misfortune but as a precise stroke. The root also gives أصاب (to hit truly, to be correct). Calamity in Arabic is not scattered noise — it is aimed."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'washing, shrouding, carrying, burying', 'student:c9c3501181e90a5a', 1, 'he is likewise not washed, because he was not lifted from his place', 'he is likewise not washed, because he was not lifted from his place', '<p>The word &quot;likewise&quot; is doing quiet but important legal work here. It points backward — not to anything named in this passage yet, but to a category already established in the tradition: the martyr (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شهيد</span>), the one who dies in the cause of Allah on the field of battle. The ruling that a martyr is not washed before burial is one of the foundational distinctions in Islamic funerary law, and it is the silent benchmark against which the wounded man is being measured.</p><p><strong>What defines the martyr''s exemption from ritual washing (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غسل</span>) is precisely that he was not lifted from the place where he fell.</strong> The battlefield soil, the blood, the position in which he met Allah — all of this is left undisturbed, because that condition is itself considered a form of sacred purity. The text then applies that same criterion to the wounded man — he was not moved, he lived on, he spoke, and when death came, he was still where he had fallen — and folds him into the same protected category.</p><p>The passage confirms this when it notes that if the martyr was in a state of ritual impurity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنابة</span>), he is washed on account of the ritual impurity — the exception only makes sense if the default rule is that martyrs are not washed at all. Even the martyr''s sacred exemption can be overridden — but only by ritual impurity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنابة</span>). The wounded man who never left his place inherits that same dignity: buried in the condition in which Allah received him, the washing that ordinarily honours the dead is here withheld as an even deeper honour.</p>', '["شهيد (martyr, witness): from the root ش-ه-د, meaning to be present, to witness, to testify. The martyr is the ultimate witness — one who testifies with their life. The same root gives شَهَادَة, both testimony and the declaration of faith. Dying in Allah''s cause makes one permanently, actively present in His sight.", "جنابة (ritual impurity): from the root ج-ن-ب, meaning to be to the side, set apart, or at a distance. This state holds a person at the periphery of worship until purification is performed. The root also gives جَنْب (side) and جَانِب (beside) — the image of someone standing aside, waiting to be restored.", "غسل (ritual washing): from the root غ-س-ل, meaning to wash or cleanse with flowing water. The root is direct: water moving over the body carries impurity away with it. It gives both the act غُسْل (the washing itself) and the substance غَسُول (water for washing), holding action and instrument in one family."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'washing, shrouding, carrying, burying', 'student:f96b10bab75f4272', 2, 'if the man in janabah is the more excellent of them, his washing of t…', 'if the man in janabah is the more excellent of them, his washing of the dead is permitted', '<p>The phrase &quot;the more excellent of them&quot; compares the man in a state of ritual impurity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنابة</span>) against the other washers present — not against the deceased. The question settled here is whether <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنابة</span> disqualifies a person entirely when alternatives exist; the answer is no.</p><p>The quality being measured is <strong>ritual fitness</strong>: a compound of religious knowledge, familiarity with the washing procedure according to the sunnah, piety, and community standing. Someone who knows the order of the limbs, the number of washings, and how to manage the cloth over what must remain covered is more excellent as a washer than someone technically pure but ignorant of the rite. Ritual impurity is an impediment, but it does not erase qualifications — it is weighed against them.</p><p>The text builds a ranked set of cases:</p><ul><li><p>When no one else is available, the washer in <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنابة</span> proceeds regardless, doing <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تيمم</span> first if the full bath would harm him.</p></li><li><p>When others are present but the person in <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنابة</span> surpasses them in qualification, his washing remains permitted.</p></li><li><p>When equally or more qualified washers are present and ritually pure, preference passes to them.</p></li></ul><p>The Arabic word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أفضل</span>, which stands behind &quot;more excellent,&quot; comes from a root meaning surplus — bounty that overflows its vessel. In jurisprudential writing, it marks <strong>measurable superiority</strong>: the kind that gives someone the better right to lead a rite.</p>', '["جنابة (ritual impurity): from root ج-ن-ب, meaning to be at one''s side, then by extension to be set apart or distant. A person in this state is estranged from ritual purity until the full bath is performed. The root captures the distance the condition creates between a person and sacred acts — a temporary separation, not a permanent one.", "تيمم (dry ablution): from root ي-م-م, meaning to aim toward or to intend. Performing تيمم is a directed act — turning one''s intention toward the earth as a purposeful substitute for water when water is absent or harmful. The root preserves the idea that purification is always a conscious reaching, even when the means must change.", "أفضل (the more excellent): from root ف-ض-ل, meaning surplus or bounty that overflows its vessel. Something أفضل has more of a quality than what is merely sufficient — it surpasses by excess of virtue. In jurisprudential use, it identifies the person whose accumulated merit gives them the stronger right to perform or lead a sacred act."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'washing, shrouding, carrying, burying', 'student:66d39cbe19909373', 3, 'neither wars nor saffron is put into the hanut of men, while these ar…', 'neither wars nor saffron is put into the hanut of men, while these are put into the hanut of women', '<p>The hanut (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حنوط</span>) is the aromatic mixture applied to the deceased before burial — a final gesture of reverence and care. Camphor (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كافور</span>) forms its required base, but additional perfumes may be added. Wars (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ورس</span>) and saffron (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زعفران</span>) are among those extras, though the sources <strong>reserve them for women''s hanut</strong> and exclude them from a man''s.</p><p>Wars (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ورس</span>) is a plant native to the highlands of Yemen. Its dried leaves and flowers are crushed into a striking yellow-orange powder, traded across the Arabian Peninsula as both a fabric dye and a body cosmetic. Both are <strong>warm, pigmenting substances</strong> long associated with feminine adornment.</p><p>The reason flows from a <strong>single governing principle</strong> — Maulana Ali (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) stated it in his Four Hundred Counsels:</p><blockquote><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا تُجَمِّرُوا مَوْتَاكُمْ وَلَا تَمَسُّوهُمْ بِالطِّيبِ اِلَّا بِالْكَافُورِ فَاِنَّ الْمَيِّتَ بِمَنْزِلَةِ الْمُحْرِمِ</span></p><p>Do not perfume your dead with incense, and do not touch them with any fragrance except camphor, for the dead man is in the position of one consecrated for pilgrimage.</p></blockquote><p>The deceased man is prepared as a <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محرم</span> — one in <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إحرام</span>. <strong>Classical jurisprudence names wars and saffron</strong> among the substances such a man must avoid. A woman faces no such restriction on these coloring substances, and that difference follows her into death — she receives in her hanut what she would be permitted as a living woman pilgrim.</p><p>Wars and saffron are not withheld from men out of frugality. They are withheld because <strong>death is an entry into the sacred</strong> — and the body is prepared as one already consecrated to Allah.</p>', '["ورس: from the root و-ر-س, whose verb وَرَسَ means to yield a yellow or golden-orange dye. The plant ورس is named for this single quality — it is literally the yellowing thing. Classical lexicographers treat it as either a pure Arabic word of Yemeni origin or a loanword from a South Arabian language carried north along the trade routes.", "حنوط: from the root ح-ن-ط, whose verb حَنَّطَ means to treat a body with aromatic spices — to prepare and embalm it for burial. The حنوط is the substance used in that act. The root carries a sense of careful readying: anointing and preserving something at the very threshold between this world and what lies beyond.", "محرم: from the root ح-ر-م, meaning to forbid or to make sacred and inviolable. This root yields حَرَام (the forbidden), الحَرَم (the sacred sanctuary), and إحرام (the state of consecration). A محرم is one for whom certain things are now forbidden — and that very act of forbidding is what makes the person sacred, as a sanctuary is sacred.", "زعفران: the word''s origin is debated — some scholars link it to an Arabic root for golden-yellow color; others see it as a borrowing from Persian, where زَر means gold. The word likely traveled with the spice along the trade routes, carrying in its name the color that defined it: crimson threads that steep into warm, radiant gold."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'washing, shrouding, carrying, burying', 'student:11d929454b167dae', 4, 'the dead man is in the position of one consecrated for pilgrimage', 'the dead man is in the position of one consecrated for pilgrimage', '<p>The equivalence between the dead and the state of ritual consecration (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إحرام</span>) is not a loose metaphor — it rests on a precise doctrinal insight about what death actually is. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إحرام</span> marks the moment a pilgrim crosses a threshold: they shed worldly dress, renounce fragrance and pleasure, and enter a condition entirely consecrated to Allah. <strong>Death is precisely that same crossing</strong>, only final and irreversible — the soul has left the domain of the world (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دنيا</span>) and can no longer hold any claim on its pleasures.</p><p>This is why Maulana Ali''s ruling in the Four Hundred Counsels carries the weight it does. He does not say the dead merely resemble the pilgrim in some poetic sense — he uses the word station (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْزِلَة</span>), the actual rank a person occupies. <strong>The dead person has arrived at the rank of the consecrated pilgrim</strong>, and the practical consequences follow directly: no fragrance may touch the body except camphor (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كافور</span>), and the shroud (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كفن</span>) is a simple unwoven white cloth that echoes the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إحرام</span> garment.</p><p>Camphor (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كافور</span>) is the one permitted exception, and <strong>the permission is itself a confirmation of the rule</strong>. It is cool and preserving rather than pleasurable, and in the Quran it names a spring among the blessings awaiting the righteous:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ الْأَبْرَارَ يَشْرَبُونَ مِن كَأْسٍ كَانَ مِزَاجُهَا كَافُورًا</span> The righteous drink from a cup whose mixture is of camphor. Al-Insan 76:5</p><p>Camphor belongs, symbolically, to the world the dead are approaching — and that is exactly why it may be used while worldly fragrances may not.</p><p>As for whether this status applies to all the dead: Maulana Ali uses the definite <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الميت</span>, meaning <strong>the dead person as a recognized category</strong>, not as a named exception. The text does identify one special case — a person who dies while in actual <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إحرام</span> during pilgrimage carries an additional restriction, because their personal consecration still has active days running, and so the head and face may not be covered. But the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إحرام</span>-like station, and the prohibition on fragrance, holds for every deceased person regardless of the circumstances of their death.</p>', '["إحرام (ritual consecration): from the root ح-ر-م, to forbid or to make sacred. The same root yields حَرَام (the forbidden), حَرَم (the sacred precinct), and مُحْرِم (the consecrated person). The act of entering إحرام — أَحْرَمَ — literally means he made himself forbidden to the world. The word captures a single movement: a person steps out of ordinary life and into divine custody.", "مَنْزِلَة (station): from the root ن-ز-ل, to descend or to alight. A مَنْزِلَة is the place where someone has come to rest — a rank, a stage, a resting point on a journey. When Maulana Ali says the dead person occupies the مَنْزِلَة of the consecrated pilgrim, he means the dead has alighted at that exact ritual station, not merely approached it.", "الميت (the dead person): from the root م-و-ت, meaning death and cessation of life. The form مَيِّت is an intensive adjective — not merely dead but thoroughly, completely so. The definite article الـ makes الميت a recognized category of person with established ritual standing, not simply a body awaiting disposal.", "كافور (camphor): an ancient loanword in Arabic, tracing back through Persian and Sanskrit origins. In Arabic use, its association with كَفَرَ — to cover and conceal — places it in the register of preservation. Its Quranic appearance in Al-Insan links it to the blessings of the next world, setting it apart from the worldly fragrances that إحرام forbids."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the wealth that is not yours', 'student:75b8101b25bdf512', 1, 'seven obligations and twelve sunnahs, like the number of the clauses…', 'seven obligations and twelve sunnahs, like the number of the clauses of the testimony and its letters', '<p>The testimony (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شهادة</span>) spoken of here is not the phrase recited on the tongue alone — it is the full, lived commitment of a believer. In Ismaili teaching, <strong>the testimony is only sincere when it is enacted through the seven pillars of Islam</strong>. Each pillar is a &quot;clause&quot; of the one testimony:</p><ol><li><p>Allegiance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولاية</span>) — love and loyalty to the Imam</p></li><li><p>Purification (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طهارة</span>)</p></li><li><p>Prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلاة</span>)</p></li><li><p>Zakat (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>) — almsgiving</p></li><li><p>Fasting (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صوم</span>)</p></li><li><p>Hajj (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حج</span>) — pilgrimage</p></li><li><p>Jihad (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جهاد</span>) — striving in the way of Allah</p></li></ol><p>When each written character in <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا إله إلا الله</span> is counted one by one, <strong>the total comes to exactly twelve</strong>:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إ</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إ</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> - <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span></p><p>This is not a curiosity — it is architecture. The Mustansiriyya Councils make a precise claim: zakat (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>) has seven obligations, mirroring the seven clauses of the testimony, and twelve sunnahs, mirroring the twelve letters of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا إله إلا الله</span>. Paying zakat (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>) correctly is therefore <strong>a way of pronouncing the whole testimony through one''s wealth</strong>, clause by clause, letter by letter — and withholding it is not merely a legal failing, but a fracture in the testimony itself.</p>', '["شهادة (testimony): from the root ش-ه-د, meaning to be present and to witness. The word شاهد means a witness — someone physically present at an event. When a believer declares the شهادة, they are not just reciting; they are standing as a witness before Allah, placing themselves in the presence of truth.", "زكاة (zakat): from the root ز-ك-و, meaning to grow and to be pure. These twin meanings live inside the same root — purity and growth are inseparable. What you give as زكاة purifies what remains, and that purification becomes the seed of growth in both wealth and soul.", "فريضة (obligation): from the root ف-ر-ض, meaning to cut, to notch, or to make a definite mark. A فريضة is something Allah has carved into existence as a fixed, inescapable duty — the mark is already made. This is why zakat''s obligations cannot be substituted by voluntary giving.", "سنة (sunnah): from the root س-ن-ن, meaning to flow in a smooth, continuous course — like water wearing a groove into stone. A سنة is a practice so established it has become a natural channel for action, the worn and trusted path of the Prophet''s way."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the wealth that is not yours', 'student:746046985429dd92', 2, 'the purchase of his own self, and the redemption of his pledge', 'the purchase of his own self, and the redemption of his pledge', '<p>The Mustansiriyya passage places four financial obligations side by side: zakat (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>), khums (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خمس</span>), &quot;the purchase of one''s self,&quot; and &quot;the redemption of one''s pledge.&quot; The first two are well-known duties; the last two speak of a transaction between the believer and the Imam that cuts to the core of identity and commitment.</p><p>&quot;The purchase of one''s self&quot; (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شراء النفس</span>) is a specific financial payment made to the Imam, acknowledging that one''s soul belongs to Allah and that the Imam — as Allah''s living proof on earth — holds custodianship over it. It is a loving ransom: paid not out of fear but out of recognition that one does not own oneself. It makes the inward truth <strong>visible through an outward act of giving</strong>.</p><p>&quot;The redemption of one''s pledge&quot; speaks directly to the covenant of allegiance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بيعة</span>) sworn to the Imam. When a person enters the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بيعة</span>, they make both a spiritual commitment and a financial promise. The &quot;redemption&quot; is <strong>the honoring of that financial promise</strong> — the oath sustained in act, not merely spoken once.</p><p>Together, these obligations reveal that devotion must be <strong>expressed in deed and sacrifice</strong>, never left to feeling alone. The Prophet''s warning is sharp: there is no prayer (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلاة</span>) for one who withholds zakat (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>). How much more is expected of one who has personally pledged allegiance to the Imam? Failing these obligations leaves a believer short of the rank Allah described:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمِمَّا رَزَقْنَاهُمْ يُنفِقُونَ</span> And out of what We have provided them, they spend. Al-Baqarah 2:3</p>', '["شراء (purchase): from root ش-ر-ي, covering both buying and selling. The same root gives اشترى (he bought) and شَرى (he sold). This duality is powerful: in the \"purchase of one''s self,\" the believer is both the buyer and the price. Every true transaction is mutual — something is always given to receive something back.", "بيعة (pledge of allegiance): from root ب-ي-ع, meaning to trade or sell. The pledge of allegiance is literally a \"sale\" — the believer offers their loyalty, and the Imam accepts it. Related forms include بيع (trade) and بائع (seller). Allegiance is not mere sentiment; it is a binding exchange with real value on both sides.", "عهد (covenant): from root ع-ه-د, meaning to promise, entrust, and take on responsibility. Derivatives include عهدة (charter, responsibility) and معهد (institute — a place where commitments are formed). The root carries a sense of return: a covenant is not a single moment but something one must continuously honor and renew.", "خمس (one-fifth): from root خ-م-س, meaning five. The term names the fraction directly — one part from every five. The same root gives الخميس (Thursday, the fifth day) and خماسي (fivefold). The obligation is proportional: a fixed share of what one earns, returned to those with the rightful claim."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'gold, herds, and harvest', 'student:d91f58d854a46461', 1, 'He begins with the zakat, for it is a debt upon him and is not part o…', 'He begins with the zakat, for it is a debt upon him and is not part of his wealth; then the Hajj, then the khums, then the expiation, then al-Shurwa', '<p>Zakat (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>) comes before all other claims because it was <strong>never truly part of the deceased''s wealth</strong> to begin with. Once the threshold (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نصاب</span>) is reached and the annual cycle (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حول</span>) passes, that portion is held in trust for the poor — not possessed. Settling the estate means returning it before distributing what was actually his.</p><p>Hajj (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حج</span>) comes next because it is one of the five pillars, a universal duty owed to Allah by every capable Muslim. The one-fifth (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خمس</span>) follows because, while deeply binding, it is the <strong>particular claim of the Imam</strong> and the Prophet''s family — a more inward channel, but one that comes after the universal. Expiation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كفارة</span>) and al-Shurwa (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشروى</span>) settle last of all, because they are the most private: broken vows, unmended fasts, and the soul''s own ransom lie between an individual and Allah alone.</p><p>The sequence traces <strong>a movement from the communal to the solitary</strong>:</p><ul><li><p>Zakat: owed to the community of the poor</p></li><li><p>Hajj: owed to Allah as a universal pillar</p></li><li><p>Khums: owed to the Imam as the living axis of the faith</p></li><li><p>Expiation: owed for personal transgressions against one''s covenants</p></li><li><p>Al-Shurwa: owed as the soul''s own ransom before Allah</p></li></ul><p>Imam al-Muizz li-Din Allah described zakat as <strong>mutual consolation</strong> — all of creation as a single soul, every limb turning toward the limb in pain. That image carries the logic of the whole: the estate first heals the community, then honours the pillars that hold it upright, and only then settles the private account the individual soul owes in its own reckoning with Allah.</p>', '["زكاة (purification): from the root ز-ك-و, conveying both purity and growth. To زكّى is to cleanse; but the same root describes a plant shooting upward. Giving زكاة does both — it purifies the remaining wealth while promising its growth in blessing. The word fuses an act of subtraction with a promise of increase.", "خمس (one-fifth): from the root خ-م-س, the Arabic word for five. The obligation is named after the fraction itself: one share taken from every five. The same root gives الخميس, Thursday, the fifth day. In every form, the root simply counts — pointing to position, fraction, and portion.", "كفارة (expiation): from the root ك-ف-ر, meaning to cover. To كفّر a sin is to neutralise and bury it through atonement. Notably, the same root gives كافر — one who covers over the truth of faith. Expiation and rejection share one root; what differs is whether what is covered is one''s own sin or Allah''s light.", "نصاب (threshold): from the root ن-ص-ب, meaning to set upright, to erect. A نصب is a marker planted in the ground. The نصاب is the minimum amount \"erected\" as a boundary — the point at which an obligation stands up and becomes binding on the owner.", "حول (annual cycle): from the root ح-و-ل, meaning to turn, to revolve, to change state. The حول is the year that has \"turned over.\" The same root gives تحوّل (transformation) and حال (condition, state). Time in Arabic is understood as a turning; the حول is the full arc of that turn."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'gold, herds, and harvest', 'student:8ca930b099186e33', 2, 'upon whoever owns two hundred dirhams there are five dirhams, and the…', 'upon whoever owns two hundred dirhams there are five dirhams, and the two hundred are two covenants', '<p>When our master Zayd ibn Muhammad says that two hundred dirhams &quot;are two covenants,&quot; he is <strong>reading the outward in the light of the inward</strong> — a practice at the heart of Ismaili thought. The silver threshold (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نصاب</span>) is not just a financial boundary; it is a marker of its owner''s full standing in the covenantal life of faith.</p><p>The two covenants are the <strong>foundational pair</strong> that together make a believer whole:</p><ul><li><p>The first is the covenant with the Prophet — a commitment to the outward religious law (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شريعة</span>), the visible, shared dimension of the faith.</p></li><li><p>The second is the covenant with the Imam — a commitment to the inward spiritual reality (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حقيقة</span>), the living knowledge that flows through the line of the Imam in every age.</p></li></ul><p>Neither covenant alone is sufficient; a believer who holds only one is like a body with one eye — alive, but unable to see the full depth of what lies before them.</p><p>When the text tells us that whoever owns two hundred dirhams is <strong>forbidden to receive zakat or charity</strong>, it speaks in the same voice: this person has crossed the threshold of need — outwardly in silver, inwardly in covenantal standing — and now bears the full privilege of giving, not taking.</p><p>The role of the full year (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حول</span>) strengthens this reading. A covenant is not a single moment of declaration; it is a <strong>sustained, living bond</strong>. The turning of the full year over one''s wealth symbolizes that endurance — the passing of time proves the covenant is not abandoned but held.</p><p>The esoteric interpretation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span>) reveals that <strong>the zakat of our silver and the zakat of our soul are never truly separate</strong>: every outward obligation is a mirror in which we see our covenants, our standing before Allah, our place in the body of the faithful.</p>', '["عهد (covenant): from root ع-ه-د, meaning to bind oneself in a solemn commitment. The root captures a mutual, held obligation — something entrusted between two parties. That is why عهد extends to mean both \"covenant\" and \"era\": the covenant defines and governs the very time in which it is honored.", "نصاب (threshold): from root ن-ص-ب, meaning to erect, to stand upright, to plant firmly in the ground. Just as a boundary post is driven into the earth to mark where one territory ends and another begins, the نصاب marks the exact point at which an obligation rises.", "حول (year): from root ح-و-ل, meaning to turn, to revolve, to shift. The full revolution of twelve months is built into the word itself — to \"turn\" through time. It is this completion of the circle that triggers the zakat due.", "تأويل (esoteric interpretation): from root أ-و-ل, meaning to return to the first, to trace something back to its origin. The word means \"the act of taking something back to where it came from\" — to interpret is to restore the hidden meaning to its rightful surface."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the charity of the head', 'student:e0836b0e8611ae66', 1, 'a man among the polytheists who has found no Muslim to whom to hand t…', 'a man among the polytheists who has found no Muslim to whom to hand the zakat of his wealth, so he has more right to it than anyone else', '<p>The ruling turns on a precise question: can an obligation be discharged when its proper recipient does not exist? The text presents two edge cases side by side — the man whose debt cancels his asset, and the polytheist who owes purifying alms (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>) but has no Muslim to give them to. When the condition for fulfilling an obligation is absent, <strong>the obligation cannot move forward</strong>.</p><p>The jurisprudential principle at work is the concept of a legitimate channel (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مصرف</span>) — a qualified recipient through whom the duty flows. The Quran itself names eight categories of people who may receive alms (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>); if none of those categories is present, there is no lawful direction in which the wealth can travel. The payer is not released from duty; <strong>the duty is frozen in place</strong>.</p><p>This is why the text says the polytheist &quot;has more right to it than anyone else.&quot; It is not a cancellation of the debt he owes to Allah — <strong>no valid transfer can occur</strong> when the receiving end of the transaction is missing. Think of a bill that is due but the creditor''s office is closed: you still owe the money, but you cannot legally hand it over until the office reopens.</p><p>In Ismaili thought, this ruling deepens when the role of the Imam is brought in. The Mustansiriyya sessions teach that alms (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>) must be delivered to the Imam (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) or to whoever he has appointed. <strong>The chain of delivery is not a formality</strong> — it is part of the obligation itself. A polytheist who enters Islam in a place without any Imam-connected community has no operative end of that chain, and his obligation is suspended, not dissolved.</p><p><strong>The moment a qualified recipient or the Imam''s representative becomes accessible, the duty revives in full.</strong> The wealth that remained with the payer was never truly his to keep; it was held in custody until the legitimate channel (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مصرف</span>) opened.</p>', '["زكاة (purifying alms): from root ز-ك-و, meaning to grow and to be pure. The same root gives both increase and cleanliness — because paying alms purifies what remains and causes it to spiritually multiply. The word holds both meanings at once, which is why giving feels, paradoxically, like gaining.", "مصرف (legitimate channel): from root ص-ر-ف, to turn or divert a flow. A مصرف is where wealth is deliberately ''turned toward'' — the valid recipient who receives and redirects it. The root conveys purposeful re-routing, not random dispersal.", "أهلية (eligibility): from root أ-ه-ل, the word for family or household — those who inherently belong. Legal eligibility means belonging to a recognized category. A recipient must ''belong'' to a qualifying group before any transfer of wealth is lawful.", "واجب (obligation): from root و-ج-ب, to fall due — like something that tips and can no longer be held upright. An obligation has ''fallen upon'' a person and must be discharged. The imagery of falling gives the term its sense of urgency and weight."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the grammar of peace', 'student:cc15bdd2a02dc480', 1, 'the five — the spokesman, the foundation, the culmination, the succes…', 'the five — the spokesman, the foundation, the culmination, the successor, and the wing', '<p>The lower borders (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحدود</span>) are five distinct roles through which divine guidance moves from Allah into the lives of ordinary believers. Imam al-Muizz li-Din Allah names them in his Sunday prayer: the spokesman, the foundation, the culmination, the successor, and the wing — five links in one chain; remove any one, and the chain breaks.</p><p>The first is the spokesman (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الناطق</span>). He proclaims a new revealed law (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شريعة</span>) so all humanity can hear it. In our present prophetic cycle, that is the Prophet Muhammad.</p><p>The second is the foundation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأساس</span>). The foundation guards the hidden, inner meaning — the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>. <strong>Maulana Ali is the foundation</strong> in our cycle, the legatee who receives from the Prophet what the masses cannot yet receive, holding inner truth steady long after the Prophet''s departure.</p><p>The third is the culmination — the <strong>Imam of the age</strong>, in whom both outer law and inner meaning come alive together. The Imam sustains and perfects the cycle across time, generation after generation.</p><p>The fourth is the successor — the one <strong>designated to carry the mission forward</strong>, bridging the Imam''s authority to those below him so that no seeker is ever left without access to guidance.</p><p>The fifth is the wing (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الجناح</span>). A bird cannot fly on one wing. The wing <strong>extends the reach of the mission</strong> outward in every direction, flanking the central authority so the whole structure never collapses inward on itself.</p><p>Together, they form one movement of guidance:</p><ul><li><p>The spokesman voices it.</p></li><li><p>The foundation roots it.</p></li><li><p>The culmination perfects it.</p></li><li><p>The successor transmits it.</p></li><li><p>The wing extends it.</p></li></ul>', '["الناطق (spokesman): from the root ن-ط-ق, meaning to speak or utter clearly. The root yields نُطق (speech) and منطق (logic — the science of correct speech). الناطق is ''the one who gives utterance.'' The spokesman is not merely a herald; he is the living voice through which the divine Word sounds in the world.", "الأساس (foundation): from the root أ-س-س, meaning to lay a base or establish from the ground up. The root gives تأسيس (founding) and مؤسسة (institution). الأساس is ''the base upon which everything stands.'' The legatee bears this name because he holds the inner structure of the revelation upright, just as a foundation holds a building after the builders have gone.", "الجناح (wing): from the root ج-ن-ح, meaning to lean to one side, incline, or extend in flight. The root gives جنوح (inclination) and أجنحة (wings). الجناح is the part that both balances and propels a bird. The supporting figure in the hierarchy extends the mission laterally, leaning outward from the centre to keep the whole structure airborne.", "الحدود (borders): from the root ح-د-د, meaning to cut sharply, to define a boundary, to set a precise limit. The root gives حد (a limit; also a legal penalty — a boundary one must not transgress) and تحديد (definition, specification). The الحدود of the da''wa are precisely defined thresholds marking where one rank of spiritual authority ends and the next begins.", "شريعة (revealed law): from the root ش-ر-ع, meaning to set out toward water, to open a path to a source. The root gives شارع (a road; a legislator) and مشروع (a project — something set on a path). شريعة is the divine ''watering-place,'' the law that leads every soul back to its source, just as a path leads travelers to a stream."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the grammar of peace', 'student:141b403a081b55e2', 2, 'the seven proclaimers, the seven culminations, the seven caliphs, and…', 'the seven proclaimers, the seven culminations, the seven caliphs, and the twelve successors', '<p>The numbers seven and twelve are not decorative — they are <strong>the spine of Ismaili sacred cosmology</strong>. The greeting peace (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سلام</span>) divides into seven forms from above and twelve from below, mirroring the structure of creation itself; the prayers of the saints repeat that same pattern when they petition for the hierarchy of the mission.</p><p>The seven belongs entirely to <strong>the upper world — the prophetic, the celestial, the divine</strong>. The proclaimers (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نطقاء</span>) are the speaking prophets who opened new eras of divine law:</p><ul><li><p>Adam</p></li><li><p>Noah</p></li><li><p>Abraham</p></li><li><p>Moses</p></li><li><p>Jesus</p></li><li><p>Muhammad</p></li><li><p>the awaited Qa''im</p></li></ul><p>Each brought a new outer law while the inner truth remained unchanged. The culminations (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أتمة</span>) are the imams who bring each prophetic cycle to its fullest expression, and the caliphs (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خلفاء</span>) are those who hold that authority within each cycle. All three groups of seven occupy the same vertical axis as the seven forms of peace that descended from Allah and His angels.</p><p>Twelve belongs to the lower world — the temporal, the cyclical, the earthly. Twelve is the count of months in a year, the count of imams within a cycle, and the count of ways peace (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سلام</span>) moves between human beings. Imam al-Muizz (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) makes this explicit when he prays &quot;upon the seven days and the twelve months&quot; — days are celestial, months are terrestrial. The <strong>twelve successors</strong> map onto the twelve human forms of greeting the way months map onto the turning of earthly time.</p><p>The passage is quietly teaching that peace (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سلام</span>), one of the names of Allah, carries the entire cosmic order within it. Seven and twelve are <strong>the grammar in which creation was written</strong>, and every believer who says &quot;peace be upon you&quot; is reading a verse from that grammar aloud.</p>', '["سلام (peace): from the root س-ل-م, meaning wholeness, safety, and freedom from harm. It yields سلامة (wellbeing), سِلم (peace between parties), and إسلام (surrender). The root binds two ideas: being unharmed and handing yourself over. To greet someone with سلام is to offer your own wholeness as a gift.", "نطقاء (proclaimers): from the root ن-ط-ق, to speak or articulate clearly. It yields نطق (speech, utterance) and ناطق (one who speaks). In Ismaili usage, the نطقاء are the prophets who speak a new outer law to the world, as distinct from those who guard its inner meaning in silence.", "أتمة (culminations): from the root ت-م-م, to complete or bring to fullness. It yields تمام (completion) and تمّ (it was finished). The word أتم means more complete, more whole. The أتمة are those who bring a prophetic cycle to its fullest expression before the next proclaimer appears.", "خلفاء (caliphs/successors): from the root خ-ل-ف, to come after, to follow, or to step into another''s place. It yields خلافة (succession) and استخلف (to appoint a successor). A خليفة is literally the one who stands where another stood — not filling a vacancy but carrying forward everything entrusted to it."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'a shield against the fire', 'student:34b0b14aedb162f7', 1, 'does not know the measures of the crescents', 'does not know the measures of the crescents', '<p><em>Researched outside this library — the knowledge base carries nothing on this passage. Sources are listed at the end.</em></p><p>In the Ismaili understanding, the phrase <strong>&quot;the measures of the crescents&quot;</strong> refers to a profound knowledge of astronomy and mathematical calculations used to determine the precise start of the months in the lunar calendar. This stands in contrast to the common practice in the wider Muslim world, which relies on the physical and visual sighting of the new crescent moon to mark the beginning of a new month, including the month of fasting (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَوْم</span>), Ramadan.</p><p>For many Muslims, the start of Ramadan is a matter of suspense; it begins only after reliable witnesses declare they have physically seen the thin sliver of the new moon in the night sky. This method is dependent on factors like weather conditions and the geographical location of the observer. A cloudy sky, for instance, can delay the start of the fast for an entire community. This reliance on visual confirmation is the standard method for those who do not have access to a different source of guidance.</p><p>However, the Ismaili tradition, particularly evident from the Fatimid era, has historically utilized a more scientific approach based on astronomical calculations. &quot;The measures of the crescents&quot; is an elegant way of describing this precise, calculated knowledge. When this knowledge is provided by the Imam of the Time, who holds the authority for religious interpretation, it changes the rules entirely. Instead of waiting for a visual sighting, the community receives a predetermined calendar.</p><p>This knowledge from the Imam removes the uncertainty inherent in the sighting method. The beginning of Ramadan is known in advance, allowing for unified preparation and observance. It reflects a core principle: the presence of a living Imam provides guidance that can clarify and even supersede traditional practices, shifting the method from one of observation to one of intellectual and spiritual certainty based on the Imam''s authority and knowledge. For those who are in the presence of the Imam or have access to his guidance, this calculated measure becomes the definitive way to begin the holy month.</p><p>***</p><p><strong>Etymology</strong></p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هلال</span> (crescent): from the root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>, which carries the meaning of raising the voice, as in acclaim or rejoicing. When the new moon was sighted, people would cry out to announce it. The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هلال</span> thus captures the moment of joyful proclamation that a new month had begun, linking the celestial event to the human response it evokes.</p><p><strong>Sources consulted</strong></p><ul><li><p>wikipedia.org</p></li><li><p>islam.ms</p></li><li><p>aicp.org</p></li><li><p>ismailignosis.com</p></li><li><p>ismaili.imamat</p></li><li><p>the.akdn</p></li></ul>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'a shield against the fire', 'student:6566d154c2a86cb4', 2, 'fasting becomes obligatory upon a boy once he can tell his right hand…', 'fasting becomes obligatory upon a boy once he can tell his right hand from his left', '<p>Two thresholds appear side by side in the chapter without explicit reconciliation. Mukhtasar al-Musannaf sets the <strong>full fasting obligation</strong> on every Muslim who has reached <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بلوغ</span> — puberty and full religious accountability. Tawil al-Shariah then says fasting becomes obligatory upon a boy once he can tell his right hand from his left.</p><p>These are not the same moment in a child''s life. The phrase ''telling right from left'' is the classical Arabic marker for <strong>discernment</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تمييز</span>) — the age when a child begins to reason and distinguish, which typically falls several years before puberty. The chapter is therefore not replacing one criterion with the other; each speaks to a different stage of a young life.</p><p>The Mustansiriyya councils passage, sitting within the same chapter, quietly resolves the tension. When it lists all fasts, it divides them into the obligatory and the <strong>recommended</strong> — and the boy''s fast appears in the recommended column, not the obligatory one:</p><ul><li><p>Obligatory: Ramadan, expiation fasts, vow fasts</p></li><li><p>Recommended: itikaf, Shaban, the three white days — and the fast of a boy when he reaches seven years</p></li></ul><p>This placement tells us that what Tawil al-Shariah calls ''obligatory'' for the discerning boy is not the full burden of Ramadan. It is the <strong>beginning of formation</strong> — a preparatory practice that shapes a child before the weight of full religious accountability (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تكليف</span>) arrives at puberty.</p><p>The ''obligation'' named here most likely falls on the <strong>guardian</strong> — to train, to encourage, to instill the habit — rather than on the child as a personal legal demand. A child who fasts from the age of discernment arrives at puberty already carrying the discipline within him.</p>', '["بلوغ (coming of age): from the root ب-ل-غ, meaning to reach or arrive at a destination. The verb بَلَغَ means ''he reached.'' Maturity in religious law is thus an arrival — a threshold genuinely crossed — capturing that full responsibility is not assigned arbitrarily but reached organically, the way a traveller arrives at the end of a road.", "تمييز (discernment): from the root م-ي-ز, meaning to separate, to sift apart, to distinguish one thing from another. The verb مَازَ means to sort or tell apart. A child with تمييز possesses the inner faculty of distinction — and it is precisely this capacity that ''telling right from left'' is testing and naming.", "تكليف (religious accountability): from the root ك-ل-ف, which carries the sense of burden and being charged with a task. كُلِّفَ means ''he was burdened with.'' The root honestly acknowledges that obligation is a weight — one placed only on those who have genuinely arrived at the capacity to carry it."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the quarrel over the crescent', 'student:a3db1b45cf98dbfa', 1, 'it would be possible for the expiation to be the feeding of sixty poo…', 'it would be possible for the expiation to be the feeding of sixty poor persons or of fifty-eight poor persons', '<p>The sixty-day expiation is <strong>not independently fixed</strong>; it is derived from the month''s length because Islamic legal reasoning treats the alternatives inside an expiation as equivalent weights. Freeing a slave, fasting two consecutive months, or feeding sixty poor persons must all represent the same obligation — otherwise the equivalence collapses.</p><p>The <strong>load-bearing principle</strong> is the one-to-one ratio: one day of missed fasting equals one poor person fed. If the feeding is always sixty, the fasting must always be sixty days, and two consecutive months must therefore be thirty each. Had months ever been twenty-nine days, the feeding would sometimes be fifty-eight — yet the tradition has never said this. The sixty-day expiation is called &quot;like the month of Ramadan,&quot; meaning the penalty doubles the very thing violated.</p><p>The expiation argument, however, is not a lone claim — it is the third of three independent witnesses assembled throughout the chapter. Allah commands fasting for a &quot;counted number of days&quot; (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أيامًا مَعْدُودَاتٍ</span>):</p><p>Al-Baqarah 2:184</p><p>That phrase alone signals days already enumerated and settled, not ones that vary with moon-sighting. Alongside it stand two more witnesses:</p><ul><li><p>The Prophet declared that Shaban is never complete and Ramadan is never deficient — testimony entirely independent of expiation law.</p></li><li><p>The year''s alternating pattern of complete and deficient months places Ramadan in the complete column as a matter of cosmic design, not human observation.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Thirty establishes sixty</strong>, and the fixed sixty we already recognize confirms the thirty being proved. The circularity the question fears only arises when the expiation argument is stripped from the Quranic and Prophetic testimony surrounding it — a court does not dismiss a third witness simply because two have already spoken.</p>', '["كفارة (expiation): from the root ك-ف-ر, meaning to cover or conceal. كَافِر literally means \"one who covers,\" burying truth beneath denial. كَفَّارَة turns that same covering action toward repair — it buries an offense beneath compensatory devotion, concealing the sin from reckoning by replacing it with obedience.", "معدودات (counted days): from the root ع-د-د, meaning to count or enumerate. عَدَدٌ is a number; مَعْدُودٌ is \"that which has been counted.\" The plural معدودات means days already tallied and fixed. What is مَعْدُود cannot be vague — it has been enumerated and settled, leaving no room for a month that varies between twenty-nine and thirty."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'two kinds of sight', 'student:00e0d42a4dce5dc1', 1, 'commands issued from the Pure Presence made it obligatory to begin th…', 'commands issued from the Pure Presence made it obligatory to begin the fast two days before the crescent was seen with the eye', '<p>The passage makes a compelling argument for soul-vision (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رؤية النفس</span>) over naked-eye sighting (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رؤية الطبع</span>), but it does <strong>not derive a formula that produces &quot;two days&quot; as a fixed or universal gap</strong>. That number was tied to year 400 AH, where the Imam''s command from the Pure Presence determined that fasting should begin two days before the community sighted the crescent — defended on principle, not worked backwards from calculation.</p><p>The astronomical principle the text does lay out is this: <strong>the moon''s separation from the sun</strong> after their conjunction (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الاجتماع</span>) is the real cause that makes fasting obligatory. At conjunction, the illuminated half of the moon faces away from the earth. As the moon begins to pull away, light appears along its edge — but the sun''s own radiance acts as a barrier (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجاب</span>), blinding the eye to that thin crescent, just as it hides the stars in daylight. An eclipse reduces the sun''s light and the stars reappear — the crescent is not absent; it is simply overwhelmed.</p><p>There is therefore always <strong>a real, measurable lag between the moment the moon separates from the sun</strong> — which calculation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحساب</span>) can pinpoint — and the moment the crescent clears enough of that light for the naked eye. That lag is not a fixed number; it shifts each month depending on the geometry of the conjunction, the moon''s speed, its angle to the ecliptic, and the observer''s latitude. For year 400 AH, it came to two days. The Imam, whose knowledge is soul-vision at its most exalted, commanded fasting from the moment the cause was established — not from the moment its effect became visible to an eye that can be deceived by light, cloud, or distance.</p>', '["الاجتماع (conjunction): from root ج-م-ع, to gather, collect, or unite. اجتماع is the verbal noun of the Form VIII verb اجتمع — \"they came together.\" In astronomy it names the moment the moon and sun occupy the same degree: two luminaries gathered at one point before separating into their distinct paths.", "رؤية (vision/sighting): from root ر-أ-ي, to see or behold. رؤية is the verbal noun — \"the act of seeing.\" The root carries both physical sight and mental perception, which is why the argument works: the same word covers eye-vision and soul-vision, making the distinction between them linguistically inescapable.", "الحجاب (barrier/veil): from root ح-ج-ب, to screen, conceal, or prevent access. حجاب is whatever stands between the eye and the thing it seeks. Here it names the sun''s overpowering light that hides the crescent — the same word used for the veil separating the mortal world from the divine.", "الحساب (calculation/reckoning): from root ح-س-ب, to count or reckon. الحساب is the reckoning — the same word the Quran uses both for the Day of Reckoning and for astronomical precision: \"so that you might know the number of the years and the reckoning.\" Divine judgment and mathematical calculation share one root.", "الهلال (crescent): from root ه-ل-ل, whose core sense is a joyful cry or the gleam of something new. الهلال names the crescent — not for its shape but for the shout that greeted its appearance each month. The root also gives هلّل, the cry of divine praise, so the crescent carries within its very name a note of spiritual joy."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'two kinds of sight', 'student:2f82d2a705b6a28d', 2, 'the seeing of knowledge scarcely errs in what it encompasses', 'the seeing of knowledge scarcely errs in what it encompasses', '<p>The author does not claim inference is independent of the senses altogether. His argument is more precise: <strong>the soul needs the senses only at the first moment of contact</strong>. When the soul receives the image of a thing for the first time, it stores that image within itself. After that, it works from what it holds — not from fresh sensory reports vulnerable to distortion.</p><p>Beyond that first moment, the soul and the eye operate in entirely different domains. The eye apprehends qualities — color, shape, thickness — that fluctuate with conditions like distance, light, and obstruction. Soul-vision (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رؤية النفس</span>) operates in the domain of <strong>necessary causal and mathematical relations</strong>, where those fluctuations cannot reach. If a drop is removed from a pool, the soul judges with certainty that the water has decreased by exactly that measure.</p><p>The crescent makes this concrete. What actually needs to be established is a positional fact: has the moon separated from the sun and moved to the west of it? Even when every obstruction is lifted, the eye apprehends only color and shape — it never directly apprehends a positional relationship. The sun''s light blocks the eye from seeing the crescent; it cannot block the soul from knowing that <strong>the conjunction has passed and the new month has begun</strong> by mathematical necessity.</p><p>The Quran gives this capacity a name — inference (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">استنباط</span>), from a root meaning to draw hidden water up from a well:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَوْ رَدُّوهُ إِلَى ٱلرَّسُولِ وَإِلَىٰٓ أُو۟لِى ٱلْأَمْرِ مِنْهُمْ لَعَلِمَهُ ٱلَّذِينَ يَسْتَنۢبِطُونَهُۥ مِنْهُمْ</span> &quot;Had they referred it to the Messenger and to those charged with authority among them, those among them who draw out conclusions would have known it.&quot; An-Nisa 4:83</p>', '["رؤية (vision): from the root ر-أ-ي, meaning to see or directly apprehend. The noun رؤية names ''a seeing.'' Crucially, the text applies the same word to both eye-sight and soul-sight — distinguishing not the name but the instrument and reach. Both are genuine acts of perceiving; the root holds them together as two expressions of one faculty.", "استنباط (inference): from the root ن-ب-ط, meaning to dig until water wells up from below. نَبَطَ is the water seeping from a freshly dug well; the Form X verb استنبط means to actively draw up what is hidden deep within. The root fits the argument perfectly: hidden truth is already present; the soul draws it to the surface.", "نفس (soul): from the root ن-ف-س, meaning to breathe or exhale. نَفَس is a single breath. The soul (نفس) is the animating breath — the living, knowing principle within a person. This root connection suggests that the soul''s perception is not an external act but something as intimate and continuous as breathing itself."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'two kinds of sight', 'student:230edc67c0f44c31', 3, 'the sense of lam, as it is used and as it is intended, is that the ac…', 'the sense of lam, as it is used and as it is intended, is that the act must precede that for which it is done', '<p>The entire grammatical argument pivots on what Arabic grammarians call <strong>the lam of purpose</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لام الغاية</span>). When <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لام</span> precedes a noun in Arabic, it signals that the verb is directed toward that noun as its intended aim — a goal that lies ahead. If an action is aimed at a future occasion, the action must already be underway before that occasion arrives. This is one of the principal, documented functions of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لام</span> in the classical Arabic grammatical tradition.</p><p>No named grammarian is cited, but an authority just as weighty is invoked: the Prophet''s own mastery of Arabic. He declared</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا أَفْصَحُ الْعَرَبِ وَلَا فَخْرَ</span> &quot;I am the most eloquent of the Arabs, and this is no boast.&quot;</p><p>Arabic already possesses clear temporal prepositions — <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عند</span> meaning &quot;at the moment of,&quot; and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حين</span> meaning &quot;when&quot; — that would have served if the intended sense were simply &quot;begin fasting the instant you sight it.&quot; A speaker of that calibre does not reach for <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لام</span> when <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عند</span> would do, and that deliberate choice is itself <strong>the grammatical proof</strong>.</p><p>The three analogies demonstrate a consistent pattern in living Arabic usage:</p><ul><li><p>&quot;Prepare things for his arrival&quot; — the preparation is done before he arrives</p></li><li><p>&quot;Compose a poem for the meeting&quot; — the poem is finished before the meeting begins</p></li><li><p>&quot;Work for the Hereafter&quot; — the work is performed before the Hereafter comes</p></li></ul><p>In each instance <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لام</span> points toward a coming occasion, and the action must be complete or underway when that occasion is reached.</p><p>The opponent''s counter-example —</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَقِمِ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ لِدُلُوكِ ٱلشَّمْسِ</span> &quot;Establish the prayer at the declining of the sun&quot; Al-Isra'' 17:78</p><p>— does not trouble the argument at all. The true declining is perceived by <strong>the soul''s vision</strong> before the eye registers it, so the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لام</span> there carries the same anticipatory logic: one should already be in prayer at the soul-perceived moment of the sun''s decline.</p><p>The argument stops short of claiming this is the only grammatically permissible reading. Mainstream jurists read the same <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لام</span> as a simple temporal marker and arrived at the opposite ruling. The case being made is rather that the purposive reading is <strong>the more precise and fitting</strong> for a speaker of supreme eloquence — and the only reading consistent with the broader proof that the vision of the soul is the nobler faculty of the two.</p>', '["رؤية (sighting / vision): from the root ر-أ-ي, meaning to see. The same root gives رأي (considered opinion or view). The root already carries both physical sight and inward perception — one forms a رأي by seeing a truth with the mind. This dual life is what the argument exploits: the crescent''s رؤية can be the eye''s apprehension or the soul''s knowing.", "أفصح (most eloquent): from the root ف-ص-ح, meaning to be clear, pure, and unobstructed — like a spring running free of sediment. فصيح describes speech stripped of all obscurity. The superlative أفصح implies a speaker whose every word choice is exact, never careless, making the appeal to the Prophet''s فصاحة a precision argument: if his language was exact, his choice of لام over عند was deliberate.", "دلوك (the declining of the sun): from the root د-ل-ك, meaning to rub or press by friction. The sun''s دلوك is the moment it rubs past the meridian, crossing from ascent into descent. The metaphor of friction conveys a precise threshold, not a gradual event — reinforcing the argument that دلوك marks an exact instant the soul perceives before the eye can register it.", "غاية (goal / ultimate aim, as in لام الغاية): from the root غ-ي-ي, meaning the furthest point a thing reaches — its outermost limit or boundary. A غاية is not a midpoint; it is the utmost extent. As a grammatical label, it describes the لام''s role exactly: it directs an action toward its furthest aim ahead, which by definition cannot yet have been reached when the action begins."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the sun, the moon, and the order of obedience', 'student:64312d1597979e44', 1, 'that is, temporary marriage (اَلْمُتْعَة) — however people wished, in…', 'that is, temporary marriage (اَلْمُتْعَة) — however people wished, in the early days of Islam', '<p>The chapter does not pause to argue the identification — it inserts it as a gloss, almost in passing. The phrase reads: marriages were contracted without a guardian or two witnesses, <em>that is</em>, temporary marriage (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المتعة</span>). That confident ''that is'' deserves attention, because the logic behind it is jurisprudential rather than historical.</p><p>In classical Islamic law, what separates permanent marriage from temporary is precisely the presence or absence of formal requirements — the guardian (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الولي</span>) and witnesses (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشهود</span>). Permanent marriage requires both; temporary marriage does not. So when the Prophet''s saying — ''There is no marriage except with a guardian and two witnesses'' — rules out marriages that lacked these conditions, the author reads it as <strong>targeting the one known practice that fit the description</strong>: mut''a. The definition of mut''a is its own evidence.</p><p>There is also a structural reason the author reaches for this example. The argument throughout the chapter is that every pillar of Islam was once in a looser state before the Prophet issued a ruling that tightened it and attached it to a qualified authority:</p><ul><li><p>Fasting was set free, then bound to the Imam''s sighting.</p></li><li><p>Religious discourse was open, then bound to silence before the Foundation.</p></li><li><p>Marriage was informal, then bound to a guardian and two witnesses.</p></li></ul><p>Mut''a fits this pattern perfectly — it is the historically recognized form of informal union from the early period. The author is not making an independent legal judgment about mut''a; he is borrowing a fact that his audience already accepted and pressing it into an analogy.</p><p>The chapter gives us the conclusion without the proof-text. It does not cite a hadith that names mut''a as the target of that ruling, nor does it quote a jurist. The identification rests on shared legal knowledge and on the rhetorical needs of the analogy being built.</p>', '["المتعة (temporary marriage): from the root م-ت-ع, whose core sense is enjoyment, benefit, or the taking of pleasure in something for a defined period. The derived noun carries that temporality inside it — متعة is a thing enjoyed for a season and then released. The same root gives us the verb مَتَّعَ, to grant enjoyment, and the Quran uses it of provision given briefly before departure.", "الولي (guardian): from the root و-ل-ي, meaning to be close to, to follow directly upon, or to have charge over. A وَلِيّ is literally the one who stands nearest — the one whose proximity gives him authority. The root also yields وِلَايَة (guardianship or authority) and تَوَلَّى (to take charge). The closeness embedded in the root is not merely physical; it is the closeness of responsible care.", "الشهود (witnesses): from the root ش-ه-د, meaning to be present, to see, and to testify. A شَاهِد is one who was there — whose presence at the event is what qualifies his testimony. The same root gives us شَهَادَة (testimony or martyrdom) and مَشْهَد (a scene or witnessed event). Witnessing in Islamic law is inseparable from presence: you cannot testify to what you did not attend."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'guardians, proofs, and the limits of praise', 'student:f107b2db34c004b4', 1, 'he is the proof of Muhammad, and the earth is his likeness', 'he is the proof of Muhammad, and the earth is his likeness', '<p>In the esoteric reading (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span>) by Jafar bin Mansour al-Yemen, &quot;the earth&quot; in the Quran is not geographical soil — <strong>it is a person</strong>.</p><blockquote><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنُرِيدُ أَن نَّمُنَّ عَلَى ٱلَّذِينَ ٱسْتُضْعِفُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَنَجْعَلَهُمْ أَئِمَّةًۭ وَنَجْعَلَهُمُ ٱلْوَرِثِينَ</span></p></blockquote><p>And We wished to be gracious to those who were made weak in the land, and to make them Imams, and to make them the heirs. Al-Qasas 28:5</p><p>That person is Maulana Ali ibn Abi Talib. His descendants — the Imams — were &quot;made weak in the earth&quot; because they were oppressed and concealed <strong>within his very person</strong>, the way seeds lie buried beneath the ground, waiting for Allah to raise them as Imams and heirs.</p><p>Maulana Ali <em>is</em> the earth because he is the proof (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجة</span>) of the Prophet Muhammad. In Ismaili teaching, every Prophet who speaks revelation openly — the Speaker (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الناطق</span>) — is paired with a Foundation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأساس</span>) who holds the inner meaning. The Prophet is like the sky that sends rain; the Imam is the earth that receives it.</p><p>The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجة</span> means proof — not an abstract argument, but a living demonstration. Maulana Ali proves the truth of Muhammad''s prophethood the way the earth proves it has rained: by what visibly grows from it. He is <strong>the evidence you can touch</strong>, the place where revelation becomes substance and continuity. To be the proof of the Prophet is to be the earth that gives the Prophet''s word its form and its future.</p>', '["حجة (proof): from the root ح-ج-ج, meaning to press toward a goal and prevail in argument. The root yields حج — the journey undertaken to witness and be witnessed — and حجة, the proof that arrives and stands firm. A حجة does not merely assert; it demonstrates, arrives, and overcomes.", "أساس (foundation): from the root أ-س-س, meaning to lay down a base, to establish from below. What is built must rest on its أساس. When Maulana Ali is called the أساس, the image is architectural: he is the ground layer on which the entire structure of the Prophet''s mission stands.", "ناطق (speaker): from the root ن-ط-ق, meaning to articulate, to make a sound ring out clearly and distinctly. The ناطق is not merely one who speaks; he is the one who gives revelation its outward, public form — the word made audible and manifest in the world.", "أرض (earth): from the root أ-ر-ض, meaning to be low, to lie beneath, to settle downward. The أرض is what receives what falls from above. In this esoteric reading, that root sense is theological: the Imam lies beneath the Prophet in rank yet receives, holds, and makes fruitful all that descends.", "استضعاف (being made weak): from the root ض-ع-ف. The Form X pattern استفعل signals that weakness was imposed from outside, not chosen. المستضعفون are those rendered powerless by others — oppressed, not simply frail — which is why their elevation to the Imamat is an act of divine reversal."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'parents, kin, and the neighbor at your side', 'student:eb83d174589cbb39', 1, 'seven that correspond to the seven clauses of the testimony, matching…', 'seven that correspond to the seven clauses of the testimony, matching the number of its clauses, which are not subject to dispute', '<p>The testimony (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شهادة</span>) being referenced is the complete declaration of faith: <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا إله إلا الله محمد رسول الله</span>. The Mustansiriya councils read it not merely as an article of belief but as a <strong>living architectural blueprint</strong> — every word and every letter carrying the weight of a specific duty.</p><p>Count the words of that declaration and you arrive at exactly seven:</p><ol><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا</span></p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إله</span></p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إلا</span></p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الله</span></p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محمد</span></p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رسول</span></p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الله</span></p></li></ol><p>To each word <strong>the seven obligatory rights</strong> of the believer are mapped — obligations that grow directly out of the syllables of the creed itself.</p><p>The twelve letters emerge from the first half of the testimony alone — <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا إله إلا الله</span> — when each letter is counted separately:</p><ul><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا</span> = <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> + <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span> = 2 letters</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إله</span> = <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span> + <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> + <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span> = 3 letters (running total: 5)</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إلا</span> = <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span> + <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> + <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span> = 3 letters (running total: 8)</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الله</span> = <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span> + <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> + <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> + <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span> = 4 letters (running total: 12)</p></li></ul><p>The count reaches <strong>exactly twelve</strong> — one for each of the twelve practices, with righteousness toward parents as the first.</p><p>This is what Ismaili thought calls the inward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>) dimension of the testimony: beneath the outward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظاهر</span>) sounds of the declaration lies a whole architecture of ethical obligation. The word-count and the letter-count are not coincidental — they are <strong>the visible surface of a meaning that runs all the way down</strong>.</p>', '["شهادة (testimony): from the root ش-ه-د, meaning to be present and to witness with one''s own eyes. The شاهد (witness) is literally the one who was there. To declare the testimony is therefore not to recite a formula but to say, from the inside: I have seen this truth and I stand in its presence.", "كلمة (word/clause): from the root ك-ل-م, meaning to speak and, in its other forms, to pierce or wound. A كَلِمَة is a unit of live speech — not a dead symbol but an act that cuts through to meaning. This is why each clause of the testimony carries an obligation: a كَلِمَة does something to the one who utters it."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'parents, kin, and the neighbor at your side', 'student:a550e8089bf9e2ae', 2, 'The seal of this righteousness is a person''s righteousness toward his…', 'The seal of this righteousness is a person''s righteousness toward his own soul', '<p>The passage works on a precise doctrinal logic: <strong>every parent creates a form</strong>. Bodily parents give the person their physical shape and nourish it into fullness. The Imam of the age (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) and his proof (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجة</span>) cause a second form — the soul''s coming into being <em>in knowledge</em>. The Prophet, Maulana Ali, and the Imams carry divine sciences communally, confirmed by the saying:</p><blockquote><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا وَأَنْتَ يَا عَلِيُّ أَبَوَا هَذِهِ الْأُمَّةِ</span> &quot;I and you, O Ali, are the two fathers of this community.&quot;</p></blockquote><p>The acts described in the &quot;seal&quot; passage are unmistakably parental — making truth plain to the soul, awakening its longing, placing holy fear of Allah within it, delivering it from destruction, and rescuing it from bondage. A caring parent does exactly these things for a child, and now the soul must become <strong>its own guardian</strong>. The seal is the moment those parental acts move entirely inside.</p><p>The doctrinal foundation is the Ismaili principle of the inner (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>) completing the outer. Righteousness toward bodily parents is relational and visible; righteousness toward religious parents is the living act of receiving the Imam''s guidance. But <strong>every tier of parental duty points inward</strong> — none of it is sealed until the person plants that truth inside their own soul, awakens its longing, and protects it from what would undo it.</p><p>The soul belongs to this framework in two directions at once: it is the child that all three tiers formed and delivered, and it is the one that must, at last, <strong>parent itself</strong> — making the truth its own from the inside, not merely inherited. That is what makes this the seal: not a fourth type of parent, but the completion of every parental act within the interior life of the soul.</p>', '["خاتم (seal): from the root خ-ت-م, to seal or close. The image is of pressing a signet ring into wax — closing a document and marking it complete and authentic. When the soul''s self-righteousness is called the خاتم, it means the act that closes and authenticates everything that came before it.", "باطن (inner): from the root ب-ط-ن, meaning belly or interior. Related words include بطن (belly) and بطانة (inner lining of a garment). The باطن is not merely hidden — it is structurally interior, the inside that sustains and gives meaning to the surface, as the belly sustains what it carries.", "حجة (proof): from the root ح-ج-ج, whose core idea is a journey to an authoritative, central place — the same root as حج (pilgrimage). The حجة is the one toward whom seekers journey for authoritative knowledge; the word fuses destination with proof, movement with certainty.", "نفس (soul): from the root ن-ف-س, meaning breath. The root gives نَفَس (a single breath) and نَفَّس (to breathe, to give relief). The نفس is the breathing, living center of a person — spiritual identity fused with the simplest act of being alive."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'what must be learned, what must be concealed', 'student:7bc07e7f08eba535', 1, 'The first to reason by analogy was Iblis', 'The first to reason by analogy was Iblis', '<p>Maulana Ali identifies Iblis as the <strong>first to practice analogy</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قياس</span>), and the act behind that judgment is preserved in the Quran:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَالَ أَنَا خَيْرٌ مِّنْهُ خَلَقْتَنِي مِن نَّارٍ وَخَلَقْتَهُ مِن طِينٍ</span> &quot;I am better than him. You created me from fire, and him You created from clay.&quot; Al-A''raf 7:12</p><p>Iblis held himself alongside Adam, isolated one measurable quality — the material of their creation — and used that comparison to <strong>override a divine command</strong>. That is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قياس</span>: reasoning from likeness to displace what Allah has decreed.</p><p>Those who apply <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قياس</span> to religion are not merely called mistaken — they are called <strong>its enemies</strong>, because they repeat Iblis''s exact move. They place their own measure, however refined, above the living authority of the Imam. Religion is not a quality to be weighed; it is a trust to be received.</p><p>Iblis was not ignorant — he was, by many accounts, among the most devoted of worshippers before his fall. <strong>Knowledge without submission</strong> is not a safeguard. The more sophisticated the reasoning, the more convincing the error feels, which is why the instruction is to stop, refer the matter back to the Imams, and wait.</p>', '["قياس (analogy): from the root ق-ي-س, meaning to measure a length or compare dimensions. The verb قاسَ means to hold one thing against another to assess their relative worth. The root makes the prohibition vivid: religion is not a dimension to be measured or a ratio to be calculated — and yet that is precisely what Iblis attempted.", "إبليس (Iblis): from the root أَبْلَسَ, meaning to fall into profound despair or to be struck speechless and confounded. The base ب-ل-س carries the sense of being cut off and bereft of any path forward. His very name is a verdict: the one who chose his own measure over Allah''s command and was sealed from mercy."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'what must be learned, what must be concealed', 'student:58b781022a6e59c9', 2, 'God will give him the cold of iron to taste', 'God will give him the cold of iron to taste', '<p>The verb <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذَاقَهُ</span> — &quot;give him to taste&quot; — is not a distant warning but a bodily experience, and what is tasted is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرْدَ الْحَدِيدِ</span> — the cold of iron. In classical Arabic, this phrase stands for the sword, the shackle, the chain: instruments that stay cold right up to the moment they strike. <strong>Cold iron is a worldly, precise consequence</strong> — ordained by Allah, arriving in this world as the direct result of a clear act of betrayal, not the cosmic fire of the hereafter.</p><p>Fire in the Quran is vast — it overwhelms, surrounds, and consumes — while cold iron is targeted and exact. A blade is cold until it cuts; a shackle is cold as it closes. <strong>This is not a flood but a blade</strong> — it falls precisely where the act of betrayal fell, no wider and no narrower.</p><p>The secret (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِرّ</span>) in Ismaili thought is not hidden information but the inner (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَاطِن</span>) dimension of reality — <strong>the living meaning behind the outer form of the revelation</strong>. The Imam or his caller (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدَّاعِي</span>) extends it only to the soul whose capacity is ready to bear it without distortion. To divulge it harms the unprepared recipient and breaks the bond of trust that keeps the call (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدَّعوة</span>) a living transmission rather than a broadcast.</p><p>The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرْد</span> from root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ب</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ر</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">د</span> also gives us the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِبرَد</span> — the metal file or rasp — so the punishment carries a second resonance: not a sudden burning but a relentless grinding-down. And <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيد</span> — iron — comes from root <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ح</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">د</span>-<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">د</span>, meaning sharp and also a limit or boundary. <strong>Iron is named for its edge</strong>: when the secret is carried past the boundary for which it was not prepared, cold iron — sharp, exact, and without mercy — marks precisely where that limit stood.</p>', '["بَرْد (cold): from root ب-ر-د, meaning to be cold; the same root gives مِبرَد, the metal file or rasp; so the word holds two physical senses at once — the cold sensation of iron against skin, and the slow grinding of file against metal; together they describe both the sharpness and the wearing-down that await the one who betrays the secret.", "حَدِيد (iron): from root ح-د-د, meaning to be sharp, keen, or intense; the same root gives حَدّ, meaning an edge, a limit, or a boundary; iron is literally the sharp-bounded metal — the thing defined by its ability to cut and hold a line; meeting it is meeting a limit that will not negotiate.", "سِرّ (secret): from root س-ر-ر, meaning to be inward, concealed; the same root gives سَرور (joy) and سَريرة (the innermost self); an Arabic secret is not merely locked away — it is the innermost part of a thing, the inner delight that belongs only to the one who has been let inside.", "أَذَاقَ (give to taste): from root ذ-و-ق, meaning to taste; ذَوْق became the central term in Sufi writing for direct, unmediated experience — knowing by living contact rather than by report; the punishment, Maulana Ali says, is not announced from afar — it is tasted, in the body, fully."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'water and intention: the anatomy of ablution', 'student:036a4eb8a48c846e', 1, 'its seven letters and its twelve', 'its seven letters and its twelve', '<p>The word &quot;its&quot; in both phrases — &quot;its seven letters&quot; and &quot;its twelve&quot; — refers in each case to <strong>the testimony itself</strong>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشهادة</span>.</p><p>The Arabic word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشهادة</span>, written with its definite article, contains exactly <strong>seven letters</strong>: <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ش</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">د</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ة</span>.</p><p>The &quot;twelve&quot; refers to the letters of the testimony''s core formula, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا إله إلا الله</span>: <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إ</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إ</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ا</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span>, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ه</span> — <strong>twelve letters</strong> exactly. This is the same twelve the text connects to the twelve practices that attend every obligation of Islamic worship.</p><p><strong>Seven and twelve</strong> together give nineteen, and this equation is what the passage calls &quot;honoring&quot; the number. Each pillar of Islam — including the walaya <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الولاية</span> — carries within it seven core obligations and twelve attached practices.</p><p>The three &quot;clauses of the testimony&quot; the passage names are the three declarations of the Ismaili Shahada:</p><ul><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا إله إلا الله</span> — there is no god but Allah</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">محمد رسول الله</span> — Muhammad is His Messenger</p></li><li><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علي ولي الله</span> — Maulana Ali is the friend of Allah</p></li></ul><p>The <strong>seven-and-twelve</strong> pattern is confirmed in the Basmala <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم</span>, where <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بسم الله</span> holds seven letters and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن الرحيم</span> holds twelve, totaling nineteen:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهَا تِسْعَةَ عَشَرَ</span></p><p>Over it are nineteen. Al-Muddaththir 74:30</p>', '["شهادة (testimony): from the root ش-ه-د, meaning to witness, to be present, to observe directly. The core idea is eyewitness presence — شهد means he was there and saw it. Remarkably, the same noun carries both testimony and martyrdom: the martyr شهيد is the one whose death becomes the ultimate witnessing act.", "ولاية (walaya): from the root و-ل-ي, meaning to be close, proximate, to govern and protect. The root idea is adjacency — ولي is one who is near, in both love and authority. الولاية is therefore the bond of proximity: the love and guardianship flowing between the believer and the Imam.", "وضوء (ablution): from the root و-ض-أ, related to ضوء meaning light and brightness. Ablution is not merely washing; the root implies that the one who performs it becomes luminous — a radiance shining outward before presenting oneself to Allah.", "طهارة (purity): from the root ط-ه-ر, meaning to be clean and free from contamination. The root covers both physical cleanliness and spiritual innocence — the same root names the purified household of the Prophet أهل البيت الطاهرين and the water of ritual washing."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'water and intention: the anatomy of ablution', 'student:057c45e44fbca178', 2, 'When sleep mingles with the heart, ablution becomes obligatory', 'When sleep mingles with the heart, ablution becomes obligatory', '<p>When Mukhtasar al-Musannaf lists sleep plainly among the nullifiers, it gives a general rule; Maulana Ali''s saying adds precision: it is specifically <strong>when sleep has fully mingled with the heart</strong> that ablution (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوضوء</span>) becomes obligatory — not every drowsy nod breaks the state.</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوضوء</span>''s root carries the meaning of brightness and clarity — to perform ablution is to enter a state of luminous, conscious readiness before Allah. Sleep that has fully taken over awareness pulls us out of that state, because we can no longer sense what our body does. <strong>Purity of body and wakefulness of heart are bound together.</strong></p><p>Consider the difference between two kinds of rest:</p><ul><li><p>A light doze where, if someone tapped our shoulder, we would snap awake immediately — awareness is still present at some level</p></li><li><p>A deep sleep where the whole world disappears, and we wake with no sense of time having passed</p></li></ul><p>The first is drowsiness that has not yet mingled with the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القلب</span> — the heart, which Ismaili and Islamic thought holds to be <strong>the seat of conscious awareness and discernment</strong>. The second is the sleep that has.</p><p>The criterion is <strong>depth, not duration</strong>. A person could doze for ten minutes in a settled posture and awareness never fully depart — the ablution holds. Another could fall into a deep sleep for only a moment and wake with consciousness wiped. The question is not &quot;did you close your eyes?&quot; but &quot;did you lose awareness of yourself?&quot;</p><p>This is why Maulana Ali''s saying carries such tenderness: the moment the wakefulness of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القلب</span> is lost, we are called back to the water, back to intention, back to the light.</p>', '["الوضوء (ablution): from root و-ض-أ, meaning brightness, luminosity, and radiant clarity. The derived word carries this light into ritual: to perform الوضوء is to make oneself bright — to enter a state of luminous readiness. The ritual is named not for the water used but for the shining, clear state the water brings about.", "خالط (mingled): from root خ-ل-ط, to mix or blend two things until they can no longer be separated. When sleep خالط the القلب, the two have fused — one can no longer find where wakefulness ends and oblivion begins. The root captures exactly why this sleep is categorically different from a mere doze.", "القلب (heart): from root ق-ل-ب, meaning to turn, flip, or overturn. The القلب is named for its constant turning between states — wakefulness and sleep, intention and distraction. Sleep that truly mingles with it does not just rest the body; it stills the very faculty that keeps us aware, present, and discerning."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the manners of the washing place', 'student:9c31c5046c2c6f83', 1, 'the two recording angels are harmed by it', 'the two recording angels are harmed by it', '<p>The instruction from Maulana Ali in his Four Hundred Manners draws a direct line between something as ordinary as unwashed hands and something as profound as a disturbance in the spirit. The greasy residue of food (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الغمر</span>) left clinging to a child''s skin is not mentioned incidentally — <strong>what we leave uncleaned, we leave unguarded</strong>.</p><p>Satan is described here as being drawn by smell — a sensory, almost animalistic image: <strong>impurity acts as an invitation</strong>. Sleep is precisely the time when a child''s conscious awareness retreats and what was kept at bay during the day can draw near. The child being startled in sleep is that dark approach reaching the body — the soul, stripped of its waking defenses, shudders when something hostile closes in.</p><p>The harm to the recording angels (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الكاتبان</span>) and the child''s startling are two faces of the same single moment. These pure presences cannot abandon their duty — they remain with the child regardless — but <strong>they are made to endure a proximity that wounds them</strong>. Think of pure spring water into which something foul is dripped: the water does not flee, but it suffers.</p><p>In the deeper Ismaili reading woven through this entire chapter, outward washing is never only outward. To wash a child''s hands before sleep is an act of love that closes a spiritual door before nightfall, and <strong>physical cleanliness is a form of guardianship</strong> that watches over the child long after our own eyes have closed.</p>', '["الغمر (food grease): from the root غ-م-ر, meaning to cover, submerge, or overwhelm. When water غَمَرَ something, it envelops it entirely. الغمر is the oily residue that clings to and covers the skin after eating. The root''s sense of something pervading a surface is exactly the problem: it coats, lingers, and draws what we would rather keep at a distance.", "الكاتبان (the two recording angels): from the root ك-ت-ب, meaning to write or inscribe. الكاتب is the writer; الكاتبان is the dual — the two writers. The root also carries an older sense of binding or stitching, as though every deed is stitched permanently into a record that cannot be undone."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the manners of the washing place', 'student:41465878077994c0', 2, 'Water purifies and is not itself purified; and likewise the knowledge…', 'Water purifies and is not itself purified; and likewise the knowledge of the Imam: whomever it does not purify, nothing else will purify him', '<p>The interpretive step rests on a discipline called <strong>interpretation</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span>), which moves from the outward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظاهر</span>) to the inward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>). It does not assign meanings freely; it first isolates the essential property of a term, then identifies which spiritual reality shares exactly that property.</p><p>The property the text isolates: water purifies and is not itself purified. A stream can be dirtied; a well can run foul. The sea, by its sheer abundance, receives the world''s impurity and is not overcome by it — it is <strong>the purifier that has no purifier above it</strong>.</p><p>The chapter itself lays down the governing equation. The summoner Zayd ibn Muhammad ibn al-Fadl states plainly that outward purity is achieved through outward water, and inward purity through inward water — which is knowledge. The question then becomes: whose knowledge holds the sea''s specific property? Ordinary human knowledge is derived — learned from teachers, drawn from books, susceptible to doubt. The Imam''s knowledge flows from divine inspiration and has <strong>no human source above it</strong>; it purifies souls and is not itself in need of purification from any chain.</p><p>The image of the Imam as living <strong>water</strong> is already planted in the same passage, which addresses him as &quot;a spring of water by which the souls of the people of Islam are made to endure.&quot; The hadith then names its fullest form — not a spring but a sea, boundless and self-sufficient.</p><p>The three moves of the interpretation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span>) are:</p><ul><li><p>Isolate the essential property of the term (purifies; is not itself purified)</p></li><li><p>Apply the equation already established in the chapter (outward water = inward knowledge)</p></li><li><p>Identify whose knowledge holds that property without derivation (the Imam''s, because it is inexhaustible and self-originating)</p></li></ul><p>What makes the step intellectually sound rather than arbitrary is that <strong>the sea''s physical behaviour is itself the evidence</strong>. A soul that turns away from the Imam''s living knowledge has no substitute capable of cleansing it of doubt and ignorance — just as there is no body of water greater than the sea to purify what the sea cannot.</p>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the manners of the washing place', 'student:b3852816d1d9894c', 3, 'Of the two washings, one is bounded and the other is unbounded; and o…', 'Of the two washings, one is bounded and the other is unbounded; and of the two wipings, one is bounded and the other is unbounded', '<p>The two <strong>obligatory washings</strong> in ablution (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وضوء</span>) are washing the face and washing the hands up to the elbows. When Jafar ibn Mansur al-Yaman says one is &quot;bounded&quot; and the other &quot;unbounded,&quot; he is speaking about the extent of the body part itself — not the number of times it is washed.</p><p>The face is the <strong>bounded</strong> washing. Its edges are fixed by nature itself: from the hairline to the chin, from one ear to the other. The washing cannot travel beyond those four fixed walls.</p><p>The hands carry an <strong>open-ended</strong> extent. The command is <em>up to the elbows</em> — that sets a ceiling but names no enclosed field below it. The washing flows upward from the fingertips like water finding its level, directed but not enclosed on all sides.</p><p>The same distinction carries into the wipings. Wiping the feet is the <strong>bounded</strong> wiping — the foot has clear edges, fixed at the ankle. Wiping the head is unbounded — the Shariah names no minimum portion, and even a small stroke across part of the head suffices.</p><p>But Jafar ibn Mansur al-Yaman is pointing at something <strong>deeper</strong> than body parts and their edges. He has already said the face represents the Speaker (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الناطق</span>) — the prophet of a specific age, a singular defined rank enclosed like the face itself — while the hands represent the Foundation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأساس</span>) and the da''wa, whose guidance flows without ceiling through every generation. So &quot;bounded&quot; does double work: a legally enclosed surface on the body, and a spiritually defined rank in the hierarchy of faith.</p>', '["وضوء (ablution): from the root و-ض-أ, meaning brightness and cleanliness. The verb وَضُؤَ means \"to be bright or clean.\" The noun وُضُوء names both the act and the water used for it. The root''s essential sense is radiance — ablution does not merely remove dirt; it brightens the limbs. This is why performing it after full purification still earns good deeds.", "محدود (bounded): from the root ح-د-د, whose primary sense is a sharp cutting edge. حَدّ means both a blade''s edge and a legally fixed limit. محدود is the passive participle — \"that which has been given a sharp edge.\" The root also gives حُدُود, the precisely fixed legal penalties of the Shariah, where exactness is everything and vagueness is dangerous.", "الناطق (the Speaker): from the root ن-ط-ق, meaning to produce clear, articulate speech. نُطْق is the power of deliberate expression — not mere sound but shaped, purposeful speech. الناطق names the prophet of each age, the one whose voice gives form to revelation. The root teaches that divine truth must be spoken into the world before it can guide anyone."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'the manners of the washing place', 'student:ad29527098bcc1e8', 4, 'The face bears the meaning of the Speaker, for in it are the seven op…', 'The face bears the meaning of the Speaker, for in it are the seven openings', '<p>In Ismaili teaching, the Speaker (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الناطق</span>) is the prophet who opens a new prophetic cycle by giving divine truth a human voice and establishing a new divine law (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشريعة</span>) for that age. History unfolds through <strong>seven such Speakers</strong>, from Adam through the Prophet Muhammad. The Prophet Muhammad is the sixth Speaker, and his cycle is the one we inhabit now.</p><p>The link between the face (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وجه</span>) and the Speaker (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الناطق</span>) is not decorative — it is doctrinal. In Arabic, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وجه</span> carries two meanings at once: &quot;face&quot; and &quot;the act of turning toward.&quot; Just as we recognize a person by their face, we recognize our path to Allah only through our prophet; and just as turning our face toward someone is an act of attention and submission, <strong>turning toward the Speaker</strong> is how a soul orients itself toward divine light. No one is known except through his prophet, and there is no deliverance except by turning toward him.</p><p>The seven openings of the face — two eyes, two ears, two nostrils, and the mouth — mirror the seven prophetic Speakers across the cycles of history. <strong>The mouth is supreme among them</strong>, because articulate speech (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النطق</span>) is precisely what names and defines the Speaker (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الناطق</span>). Divine knowledge flows downward through the cosmic hierarchy as a Pillar of Light (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عمود نور</span>), and the Speaker is the point where that knowledge breaks into human speech and law, illuminating the world below.</p><p>This is why washing the face in ablution (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الوضوء</span>) carries an inner meaning alongside its outward one. Water removes physical filth; <strong>inward purity through knowledge</strong> washes away the filth of doubt and ignorance. When we wash our face, we are — in the esoteric interpretation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التأويل</span>) — renewing our orientation toward the Speaker (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الناطق</span>), reaffirming that we can only know Allah through His prophet.</p>', '["الناطق (the Speaker): from the root ن-ط-ق, meaning to produce articulate sound. النطق is the human faculty of speech — what sets the human being apart. الناطق is the active participle, ''the one who speaks.'' Applied to the prophet, it marks him as the one who gives silent divine truth a human voice, turning unseen knowledge into enacted law.", "وجه (face / direction): from the root و-ج-ه, meaning to face or to direct. The noun وجه carries two senses at once — ''face'' and ''direction, intention, purpose.'' The verb وجَّه means to orient or aim something toward a goal. This double meaning embeds the doctrine: the face is both how we are known to others and the direction we turn toward.", "الوضوء (ablution): from the root و-ض-أ, connected to وَضَاءة — brightness, luminosity, beauty. الوضوء is not merely cleansing; its very name carries the promise of becoming luminous. In esoteric interpretation, that luminosity is the soul made radiant by turning itself toward knowledge and its source."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'major impurity and the cleanliness of things', 'student:ad95cc315a102d98', 1, 'The washing is a likeness of the removal of doubt, and doubt arises o…', 'The washing is a likeness of the removal of doubt, and doubt arises only at the moment of imparting and receiving.', '<p>The esoteric teaching here turns on a beautiful symmetry: the physical act of marital union is a likeness of the spiritual act of transmission — one soul imparting knowledge, another receiving it. This is the deeper meaning of major ritual impurity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَابَة</span>) — the one rendered in this state is <strong>the one who is far from a thing</strong>, estranged, displaced from the ground they once occupied, standing suddenly in unfamiliar territory.</p><p>Before a great truth is handed to us, we stand on familiar ground, comfortable in what we know. The moment it truly enters us, that ground shifts — what we thought we knew no longer fully holds, and what we have received has not yet taken root. <strong>This suspension is the doubt (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكّ</span>)</strong> the teacher speaks of, and it is not failure; it is the aching, necessary condition of a soul caught between two states of knowing.</p><p>The washing (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غُسل</span>) is then a likeness of resolution — the thorough, unhurried work of integrating what was received until no corner of the soul remains untouched. Just as the Prophetic teaching demands that water reach beneath every hair and cleanse every patch of skin, <strong>the integrating of received knowledge must reach into every assumption and residual confusion</strong>, leaving nothing dry, nothing unexamined. When the washing is complete, the state of being far ends — the soul returns to proximity, to certainty (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقِين</span>), to a purity richer and deeper than what it held before.</p><p><strong>Doubt is not incidental</strong> — it is bound to the very structure of transmission itself. This is why the full washing is obligatory only after the full act of imparting and receiving: lesser contact demands lesser purification, but when something whole passes between two souls, the entire self must be washed in response.</p>', '["جَنَابَة: from root ج-ن-ب, meaning to be at the side of, to be distant, to be estranged. The root gives جانب (side, flank) and اجتنب (to avoid, to keep away from). The state of جَنَابَة is a temporary displacement — the person is set apart from ritual nearness to Allah, a stranger to proximity, until purification restores them.", "شَكّ: from root ش-ك-ك, meaning to pierce through both sides of something, as a spear passing clean through. The image is of a mind pierced between two possibilities — suspended, unable to rest on either side. Doubt is the wound of encountering something new that breaks open settled ground.", "غُسل: from root غ-س-ل, meaning to wash with fullness and thoroughness. A surface merely dampened has not truly been غَسَلَ. The root insists on totality — reaching beneath what is concealed. This connects directly to the teaching that esoteric integration, like water, must leave no inner surface untouched.", "يَقِين: from root ي-ق-ن, meaning to be still and clear, like water that has ceased its turbulence and become transparent. Certainty is not assertion — it is settled clarity. The root captures the state that returns to the soul once all doubt, stirred up by the act of receiving, has been fully washed away."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'major impurity and the cleanliness of things', 'student:671ad1278963ae23', 2, 'for the water has its folk, and the air has its folk', 'for the water has its folk, and the air has its folk', '<p>The phrase <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أهل الماء وأهل الهواء</span> — &quot;the folk of the water and the folk of the air&quot; — points to a reality Maulana Ali takes with complete seriousness: the natural world is not empty. In the classical Islamic understanding, <strong>jinn</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنّ</span>) are created from smokeless fire, invisible to ordinary perception, and present within the spaces we share — standing water, latrines, and the open elements are among their well-known haunts. To intrude upon these spaces carelessly is not merely poor hygiene; it is a disruption of a world with its own order, its own inhabitants, and its own quiet claim on our respect.</p><p>Water (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ماء</span>) is among the most charged symbols in the tradition — the inner interpretation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span>) sees it as an image of purifying knowledge, and the Imam himself is <strong>compared to water</strong> at the very close of this chapter: water purifies everything else but is never itself rendered impure. For water to have folk marks it as a domain with its own guardians, a sanctity the believer is expected to honour.</p><p>Ignoring the warning brings harm on two levels:</p><ul><li><p>Physically: contamination, illness, the wind carrying back what was cast carelessly into it</p></li><li><p>Spiritually: arrogance toward a world whose inhabitants were never consulted, a tearing of the created order</p></li></ul><p>The teaching is, at its heart, refined inner conduct (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أدب</span>) — a discipline that governs how we move through the entire created world, not merely how we treat other people. <strong>Creation does not belong to us alone</strong>; we are guests within it, and guests do not defile the house of those whose presence they cannot see.</p>', '["ماء (water): from the root م-و-ه. Lane records مَوَّهَ as ''to gild a surface'' and also ''to embellish falsehood so it resembles truth, to deceive.'' The root is fluid by nature — water takes whatever shape contains it, can purify and conceal at once. This is the element the Quran names sixty-three times: life and appearance, held in a single word.", "سطح (open expanse): from the root س-ط-ح, conveying the idea of spreading flat. In Lane, مِسْطَح is the rolling-pin that stretches dough wide; the Quran uses سُطِحَتْ to describe the earth spread open. When Maulana Ali warns against urinating في سطح الهواء, سطح paints the sky as a boundless flat surface — and every surface belongs to those who inhabit it.", "أهل (folk): from the root أ-ه-ل, meaning to be inhabited, to be fit, to belong. From it come أهل (family, those for whom a place is home) and تأهَّل (to settle). When Maulana Ali says the water has its أهل, the word is warmer than ''occupants'' — it names beings for whom that space is genuinely home. We are the strangers.", "جنّ (jinn): from the root ج-ن-ن, meaning to be hidden, concealed. From it: جنّة (garden, sheltered enclosure), جنين (fetus, hidden in the womb), مَجَنَّ (shield). The folk of the water are, in the most literal sense, جنّ — beings defined by hiddenness. Their concealment is not absence; it is a different kind of presence, one the believer learns to take seriously."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('isaf-al-talib', 'major impurity and the cleanliness of things', 'student:48b2251a28af95c8', 3, 'Water purifies and is not itself purified. So likewise is the knowled…', 'Water purifies and is not itself purified. So likewise is the knowledge of the Imam: whomever it does not purify, God does not purify him.', '<p>The analogy moves in two distinct steps. The first is a jurisprudential property: purifying water (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طهور</span>) is not simply clean — it actively purifies whatever it contacts, and yet it does not need something else to purify it in return. <strong>The Imam''s knowledge shares this exact structure</strong>: it enters a soul heavy with doubt (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شك</span>) and clears it, and yet the knowledge itself is never rendered doubtful by the doubting soul it enters.</p><p>The second step draws on what this text already established: the washing (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غسل</span>) from ritual impurity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جنابة</span>) is, outwardly, a washing of the body, but inwardly it is the removal of doubt, and doubt arises specifically at the moment of &quot;imparting and receiving.&quot; <strong>That phrase maps the sacred transmission between Imam and believer onto the very act that makes the washing obligatory</strong>. The Imam gives knowledge; the believer receives it; that exchange creates a spiritual condition that calls for inner purification, and the Imam''s knowledge is the water that fulfills it.</p><p>The question of why Maulana Ali says the sea (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البحر</span>) rather than water in general is sharper than it looks. Jurisprudentially, the Prophet had already established seawater as purifying water (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طهور</span>) — even in its saltiness and vastness — removing any objection that a body of water could be insufficient. By reaching for the most extreme and undeniable instance, <strong>the saying removes every possible excuse</strong>: if even that cannot purify you, nothing can.</p><p>But the sea (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البحر</span>) carries more than jurisprudential weight. It is a symbol for the Imam''s knowledge in its totality: inexhaustible, all-surrounding, entered only through complete immersion. The image Maulana Ali leaves us with is <strong>quietly devastating</strong> — an infinite, purifying ocean is present, and the one who remains impure has simply refused to enter it. &quot;May Allah not purify him&quot; is not anger; it is consequence. The door was open. The water was there.</p>', '["طهور: from the root ط-ه-ر, meaning to be clean or pure. طهور is an intensive verbal noun — not simply something that is pure, but something that actively purifies others. The sea is not merely طاهر (clean); it is طهور — a purifier. This is why the Imam''s knowledge earns the same title.", "بحر: from the root ب-ح-ر, meaning to be wide, expansive, unbounded. From the same root comes تبحّر — to immerse oneself deeply in a subject. A person called a بحر of knowledge is one whose learning has no bottom or shore. The choice of البحر over plain water carries the meaning of inexhaustible depth.", "جنابة: from the root ج-ن-ب, meaning to be at the side of, apart from, or distant. A جنب is a stranger — someone far from their own lineage. Ritual impurity (جنابة) places the person spiritually apart from the sacred until the washing re-admits them. Classical usage pairs جنب with ''stranger'' — the impure person is one who stands far from worship."]');
